var e=[{topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Preformulation is the first laboratory stage of product development. Before anyone decides whether a new drug will become a tablet, a capsule or an injection, its physical and chemical properties are measured systematically. The aim is simple: find out what the molecule can and cannot tolerate, so the formulation is designed around facts rather than guesses.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Organoleptic properties: colour, odour and taste of the powder. Recorded because a change later signals degradation.`,`Bulk density: mass of powder divided by the volume it occupies loosely, in g/mL. Tells you what size die or capsule shell you will need.`,`Tapped density: the density after a fixed number of standard taps, when the powder has settled.`,`Solubility: how many mg of drug dissolve in 1 mL of a solvent at a stated temperature.`,`pKa: the pH at which a drug is exactly half ionised and half unionised.`,`Partition coefficient: how the drug shares itself between an oily phase and water. A measure of lipophilicity.`,`Polymorphism: one chemical substance existing in more than one crystal form.`,`Hygroscopicity: the tendency of a solid to pick up water from the air.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The two halves of preformulation`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Study type`,`What is measured`,`Why it matters`],rows:[[`Physicochemical`,`Solubility, pKa, log P, melting point`,`Predicts absorption and choice of salt`],[`Solid state`,`Polymorphs, crystallinity, particle size, habit`,`Controls dissolution rate and stability`],[`Bulk / powder`,`Flow, bulk and tapped density, compressibility`,`Decides tablet vs capsule and machine settings`],[`Stability`,`Hydrolysis, oxidation, photolysis, thermal`,`Sets storage conditions and packaging`],[`Compatibility`,`Drug plus each excipient, stressed and analysed`,`Removes excipients that react with the drug`]],caption:`Preformulation is split into drug-alone studies and drug-with-excipient studies.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The single most asked preformulation idea`,text:`A drug must dissolve before it can be absorbed. So anything that raises dissolution rate (smaller particles, a more soluble salt, a metastable polymorph, an amorphous form) usually raises oral bioavailability, and anything that lowers it does the opposite.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Solubility is usually reported as the amount of solvent needed to dissolve 1 part of solute. In the pharmacopoeial descriptive scale, very soluble needs less than 1 part of solvent, freely soluble 1 to 10 parts, soluble 10 to 30, sparingly soluble 30 to 100, slightly soluble 100 to 1000, very slightly soluble 1000 to 10000 and practically insoluble more than 10000 parts.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A drug with aqueous solubility below about 1 mg/mL will often show dissolution rate limited absorption.`,`Weak acids dissolve better in the intestine, weak bases better in the stomach.`,`Salt formation is the commonest quick fix for poor solubility of an ionisable drug.`,`Melting point below about 50 degrees C warns you the powder may soften and stick during compression.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`What preformulation looks at`,text:`S-P-P-P-S: Solubility, pKa, Partition coefficient, Polymorphism, Stability. Get these five and you can already sketch a dosage form.`}]},{topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level, preformulation means four things you must be able to reproduce cold: the BCS table, polymorphism versus pseudopolymorphism, the partition coefficient with its Henderson-Hasselbalch link, and how DSC is read in a drug-excipient compatibility study.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Biopharmaceutics Classification System (BCS)`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Solubility`,`Permeability`,`Rate limiting step`,`Formulation implication`],rows:[[`I`,`High`,`High`,`Gastric emptying`,`Easiest; eligible for biowaiver if rapidly dissolving`],[`II`,`Low`,`High`,`Dissolution`,`Micronise, salt, solid dispersion, lipid systems; best IVIVC`],[`III`,`High`,`Low`,`Permeation`,`Permeation enhancers, prodrug; dissolution rarely helps`],[`IV`,`Low`,`Low`,`Both`,`Poorest oral candidates; often need a non-oral route`]],caption:`High solubility means the highest dose strength dissolves in 250 mL or less of aqueous media across the physiological pH range. High permeability means at least 85 percent of an administered dose is absorbed.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Class I examples: paracetamol, metoprolol, propranolol, diltiazem.`,`Class II examples: carbamazepine, ketoconazole, glibenclamide, ibuprofen, phenytoin.`,`Class III examples: atenolol, cimetidine, ranitidine, acyclovir, metformin.`,`Class IV examples: furosemide, hydrochlorothiazide, chlorothiazide, taxol.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Polymorphism and pseudopolymorphism`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Form`,`Definition`,`Solubility / stability`],rows:[[`Stable polymorph`,`Lowest free energy crystal form`,`Least soluble, most stable, longest shelf life`],[`Metastable polymorph`,`Higher energy crystal form`,`More soluble and faster dissolving; converts on storage`],[`Pseudopolymorph (solvate)`,`Solvent molecules inside the lattice`,`Hydrates usually dissolve slower than the anhydrate`],[`Amorphous form`,`No long range lattice order`,`Highest solubility and dissolution; least physically stable`]],caption:`Pseudopolymorphs are solvates and hydrates. They differ in composition, so strictly they are not true polymorphs.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The classic polymorphism example`,text:`Chloramphenicol palmitate exists as forms A, B and C plus an amorphous form. Only the metastable form B gives useful blood levels; the stable form A is essentially inactive because it does not dissolve fast enough for the esterase to act. Ritonavir is the famous industrial disaster, where a late-appearing, less soluble form II forced the product off the market.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Partition coefficient and ionisation`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`P = C(oil) / C(water)   and   log P = log10 P`,caption:`P is the partition coefficient, usually measured in n-octanol and water at 25 degrees C. C(oil) and C(water) are the equilibrium concentrations of the unionised drug in each phase.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`pH = pKa + log ([ionised] / [unionised])   for a weak acid`,caption:`Henderson-Hasselbalch. For a weak base the ratio inverts: pH = pKa + log ([unionised] / [ionised]). At pH equal to pKa the drug is 50 percent ionised.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Log P between about 1 and 3 is the usual optimum for passive oral and transdermal absorption.`,`Log P below 0 means the drug is too hydrophilic to cross membranes easily.`,`Log P above 5 means the drug is too lipophilic; it partitions into fat and dissolves poorly.`,`Log D is log P at a stated pH and accounts for ionisation; for a non-ionisable drug log D equals log P.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Hygroscopicity`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Non-hygroscopic: no water uptake below 90 percent relative humidity.`,`Hygroscopic: takes up water above a characteristic relative humidity and holds it.`,`Deliquescent: absorbs so much water that it dissolves in it, for example calcium chloride and potassium iodide.`,`Efflorescent: a hydrate that loses its water of crystallisation to the air, for example sodium sulphate decahydrate.`,`Critical relative humidity (CRH) is the RH above which a water soluble solid begins to absorb moisture from the atmosphere.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Drug-excipient compatibility`},{kind:`para`,text:`A 1:1 binary mixture of drug and each candidate excipient is stressed, usually at 40 to 50 degrees C and 75 percent RH for two to four weeks, and compared against the drug alone. Thermal methods screen quickly; a stability-indicating HPLC assay confirms. Classic incompatibilities are primary amines with lactose (Maillard browning), aspirin with magnesium stearate (alkaline hydrolysis) and oxidisable drugs with peroxide-bearing polyethylene glycols or povidone.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`DSC alone is not proof`,text:`A shifted or broadened melting endotherm in a DSC thermogram only suggests an interaction; simple eutectic formation or dissolution of the drug in a molten excipient gives the same picture without any chemical change. A positive DSC finding must always be confirmed by isothermal stress testing with chromatographic assay.`}]},{topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced preformulation is quantitative: intrinsic dissolution rate, the thermodynamics that decide which polymorph you actually get, thermal analysis read properly, and the numerical treatment of moisture uptake.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Dissolution rate theory`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`dC/dt = D A (Cs - C) / (h V)`,caption:`Noyes-Whitney. D is diffusion coefficient (cm2/s), A the effective surface area (cm2), Cs the saturation solubility, C the bulk concentration, h the diffusion layer thickness (cm) and V the dissolution medium volume (mL).`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`IDR = (dm/dt) / A   in mg min-1 cm-2`,caption:`Intrinsic dissolution rate, measured on a compressed disc of constant surface area in a Wood apparatus. dm/dt is mass dissolved per minute; A is the exposed disc area.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`IDR below 0.1 mg min-1 cm-2 predicts dissolution rate limited absorption.`,`IDR above 1 mg min-1 cm-2 means dissolution will not limit absorption.`,`IDR removes the surface area variable, so it compares salts and polymorphs fairly; ordinary powder dissolution does not.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Polymorph thermodynamics`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Concept`,`Meaning`,`Consequence`],rows:[[`Enantiotropy`,`Two forms swap stability at a transition temperature`,`Both forms can be the stable one, depending on temperature`],[`Monotropy`,`One form is stable at every temperature below the melt`,`The metastable form always tends to convert`],[`Ostwald rule of stages`,`The least stable form crystallises first`,`Rapid precipitation tends to give the metastable form`],[`Solubility ratio`,`Metastable solubility divided by stable solubility`,`Usually 1.1 to 2; rarely worth exploiting if under 1.2`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Metastable does not mean usable`,text:`A metastable polymorph gives a real bioavailability gain only if it survives processing and shelf life. Milling, wet granulation, drying and compression all supply the energy and the moisture that trigger conversion back to the stable form, which then shows up as a slowing dissolution profile on stability.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Thermal analysis`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Technique`,`Signal measured`,`Typical preformulation use`],rows:[[`DSC`,`Heat flow difference vs reference against temperature`,`Melting point, purity, polymorphs, glass transition, compatibility`],[`DTA`,`Temperature difference vs reference`,`Qualitative transitions only; no direct enthalpy`],[`TGA`,`Sample mass against temperature`,`Water of hydration, residual solvent, decomposition onset`],[`Hot stage microscopy`,`Visual change under polarised light`,`Confirms whether a DSC event is melting or a transition`]],caption:`A hydrate shows a low temperature endotherm in DSC with a matching step in the TGA trace; a polymorphic transition shows the endotherm with no mass loss.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A sharp DSC melting endotherm with a narrow onset indicates high crystallinity and purity.`,`The van t Hoff plot of the DSC melting endotherm gives purity without a reference standard, valid above about 98 mole percent.`,`Loss of the drug endotherm in a binary mixture, or a new exotherm, is the signal to escalate to stressed HPLC.`,`The amorphous form shows a glass transition (a step in baseline) rather than a melting peak.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Moisture uptake quantitatively`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`CRH(AB) = CRH(A) x CRH(B) / 100`,caption:`Elder equation for the critical relative humidity of a mixture of two water-soluble salts that do not interact. CRH values are percentages. The mixture CRH is always lower than either component, so blends pick up moisture more readily than either powder alone.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Worked CRH point`,text:`If salt A has CRH 80 percent and salt B has CRH 70 percent, the mixture has CRH = 80 x 70 / 100 = 56 percent. A room controlled at 60 percent RH is safe for either powder alone but will make the blend damp.`}]},{topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A tablet is a solid dosage form made by compressing a powder or granulation between two punches inside a die. It is the most used dosage form because it is accurate, stable, cheap and easy for the patient. Almost no drug can be compressed on its own, so a tablet is really a drug plus a set of excipients, each doing one job.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Excipient categories with examples`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Category`,`Job`,`Named examples`],rows:[[`Diluent (filler)`,`Bulks up a low dose drug to a handleable size`,`Lactose, microcrystalline cellulose, dicalcium phosphate, mannitol, starch`],[`Binder (adhesive)`,`Holds particles together as granules and as a tablet`,`Starch paste, povidone (PVP), HPMC, gelatin, acacia`],[`Disintegrant`,`Breaks the tablet apart in fluid`,`Starch, sodium starch glycolate, croscarmellose sodium, crospovidone`],[`Lubricant`,`Cuts friction between tablet and die wall on ejection`,`Magnesium stearate, stearic acid, sodium stearyl fumarate`],[`Glidant`,`Improves powder flow from hopper into die`,`Colloidal silicon dioxide (Aerosil), talc, maize starch`],[`Antiadherent`,`Stops powder sticking to the punch faces`,`Talc, maize starch, magnesium stearate`],[`Colour / flavour`,`Identification and palatability`,`Lake dyes, titanium dioxide, saccharin sodium, aspartame`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Lubricant, glidant, antiadherent are three different jobs`,text:`A lubricant reduces die wall friction so the tablet ejects; a glidant reduces particle to particle friction so the powder flows; an antiadherent stops material sticking to the punch tip. Talc and magnesium stearate can do more than one, which is exactly why examiners use them as distractors. The definition, not the substance, is the answer.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The three ways of making a tablet`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Wet granulation: powders are wetted with a binder solution, massed, screened wet, dried and screened again, then lubricated and compressed.`,`Dry granulation: powders are compacted without liquid, either as large slugs or through a roller compactor, then milled to granules and compressed.`,`Direct compression: powders are simply blended with directly compressible excipients and compressed. No granulation step at all.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Granulation exists to improve flow, prevent segregation of a fine drug from coarse excipients and improve compressibility.`,`Wet granulation is ruled out for moisture sensitive or heat sensitive drugs.`,`Direct compression is the cheapest and fastest but only works if the powder already flows and compresses.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Presses`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Single punch (eccentric) press: one die and one pair of punches; only the upper punch moves down to compress. Used for development and small batches.`,`Rotary (multistation) press: a rotating turret carries many dies past two compression rollers, so both punches squeeze together. Used for production.`,`Key parts of any press: hopper, feed frame or shoe, die, upper and lower punches, cam tracks, compression rollers and the ejection cam.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`The three strokes of a press`,text:`F-C-E: Fill, Compress, Eject. Every tablet press, single punch or rotary, is just those three strokes repeated.`}]},{topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This is the heaviest scoring topic in Pharmaceutical Technology. You need the granulation comparison, the superdisintegrant mechanisms, every tablet defect with its cause and remedy, and the numerical limits of the official evaluation tests.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Granulation methods compared`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Wet granulation`,`Dry granulation`,`Direct compression`],rows:[[`Number of steps`,`Most (8 to 10)`,`Intermediate`,`Fewest (blend and compress)`],[`Moisture / heat`,`Both used`,`Neither used`,`Neither used`],[`Suits labile drugs`,`No`,`Yes`,`Yes`],[`Suits high dose poorly flowing drug`,`Yes, best option`,`Yes`,`Often no`],[`Suits very low dose drug`,`Yes, uniform`,`Moderate`,`Risk of segregation`],[`Equipment / cost`,`Highest`,`Moderate`,`Lowest`],[`Typical binders`,`Starch paste, PVP solution, HPMC`,`Dry PVP, MCC, ethylcellulose`,`None needed`],[`Main limitation`,`Degradation of labile drugs`,`Loss of compressibility on re-compaction, dust`,`Excipient lot variability, content uniformity`]]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Wet granulation sequence: weighing and milling, dry mixing, preparation of binder solution, wet massing, wet screening through a coarse sieve, drying (tray or fluid bed), dry screening through a finer sieve, blending with lubricant and glidant, compression.`,`Dry granulation by slugging: blend, compress into large slugs on a heavy duty press, mill the slugs, screen, lubricate, compress.`,`Dry granulation by roller compaction: blend, feed between two counter rotating rollers (chilsonator) to give a ribbon, mill the ribbon, screen, lubricate, compress.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Work hardening in dry granulation`,text:`Material compacted once loses some of its ability to bond a second time, because the plastic deformation capacity has already been used. That is why roller compacted granules often give softer tablets than wet granulated ones at the same compression force.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Disintegrants and superdisintegrants`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Agent`,`Usual level`,`Main mechanism`],rows:[[`Maize starch (conventional)`,`5 to 15 percent`,`Swelling and capillary wicking`],[`Sodium starch glycolate`,`2 to 8 percent`,`Very rapid swelling, up to 300 percent`],[`Croscarmellose sodium`,`0.5 to 5 percent`,`Wicking plus swelling; fibrous`],[`Crospovidone`,`2 to 5 percent`,`Wicking and shape recovery; does not gel`],[`Low substituted HPC`,`1 to 5 percent`,`Swelling`],[`Effervescent couple`,`As required`,`Carbon dioxide evolution (bicarbonate plus acid)`]],caption:`Superdisintegrants work at a tenth of the level of starch. Mechanisms overall: swelling, wicking (capillary action), deformation recovery, and particle repulsion.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Intragranular disintegrant breaks the granules; extragranular breaks the tablet into granules. Splitting the dose between both is common.`,`Crospovidone is preferred where a gel layer would slow release, because it does not form a viscous gel.`,`Sodium starch glycolate loses efficiency at high levels because a viscous barrier forms.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Tablet defects, causes and remedies`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Defect`,`Appearance`,`Main cause`,`Remedy`],rows:[[`Capping`,`Top or bottom crown separates`,`Entrapped air, too many fines, deep concave punches`,`Add pre-compression, slow turret, tapered die, reduce fines`],[`Lamination`,`Splits into horizontal layers`,`Over-lubrication with stearates, air entrapment, oily drug`,`Cut stearate level and mixing time, add colloidal silica, tapered die`],[`Picking`,`Material lifts off into punch engraving`,`Excess moisture, low melting drug, fine lettering`,`Dry granules further, add adsorbent, polish and chrome plate punches, enlarge letters`],[`Sticking`,`Granule adheres to die wall or punch face`,`Insufficient lubricant, high moisture, low melting point`,`Increase lubricant, dry more, add antiadherent, clean punches`],[`Mottling`,`Uneven colour on the surface`,`Dye migration on drying, coloured degradant, drug colour differs`,`Use lakes not soluble dyes, lower drying temperature, improve mixing`],[`Chipping`,`Edges break away`,`Too dry a granulation, worn punch edges`,`Increase binder, add plasticiser, replace or polish punches`],[`Cracking`,`Fine cracks on the upper or lower centre`,`Large granules, rapid elastic recovery, cold punches`,`Reduce granule size, add fines, use dry binder, warm the punches`],[`Binding`,`Hard ejection with a screeching sound`,`Insufficient lubricant, worn or undersized die`,`Increase lubricant, use tapered die, clean and polish die`],[`Double impression`,`Blurred or doubled monogram`,`Free rotation of an engraved punch`,`Use keyed or anti-turning punches`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Evaluation tests and their official limits`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Test`,`Method`,`Limit`],rows:[[`Hardness`,`Monsanto, Pfizer, Strong-Cobb, Erweka testers`,`4 to 10 kg/cm2 usual; chewable about 3; SR 10 to 20`],[`Friability`,`Roche friabilator, 100 revolutions at 25 rpm (4 min)`,`Weight loss not more than 1.0 percent w/w`],[`Weight variation`,`20 tablets weighed individually against the mean`,`IP: 10 percent up to 80 mg; 7.5 percent above 80 and below 250 mg; 5 percent at 250 mg and above`],[`Content uniformity`,`10 dosage units assayed individually`,`85 to 115 percent of label claim, RSD not more than 6 percent`],[`Thickness`,`Vernier calliper or micrometer`,`Within plus or minus 5 percent of the standard`]],caption:`In the weight variation test not more than two tablets may deviate by more than the stated percentage, and no tablet may deviate by more than twice it.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Tablet type`,`Disintegration time limit (IP)`],rows:[[`Uncoated`,`15 minutes`],[`Film coated`,`30 minutes`],[`Sugar coated`,`60 minutes`],[`Enteric coated`,`No disintegration in 0.1 M HCl for 2 h, then 60 min in pH 6.8 buffer`],[`Dispersible / soluble`,`3 minutes in water at 15 to 25 degrees C`],[`Effervescent`,`Within 5 minutes in 200 mL water`],[`Buccal`,`4 hours`]],caption:`Disintegration apparatus: basket rack with six tubes, 28 to 32 cycles per minute, stroke 55 mm, medium at 37 plus or minus 2 degrees C.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`USP apparatus`,`Name`,`Best suited to`],rows:[[`I`,`Rotating basket (usually 100 rpm)`,`Capsules, floating or disintegrating units`],[`II`,`Paddle (usually 50 rpm)`,`Conventional and coated tablets; the default`],[`III`,`Reciprocating cylinder`,`Modified release, bead products, pH change profiles`],[`IV`,`Flow-through cell`,`Poorly soluble drugs, implants, suppositories, powders`],[`V`,`Paddle over disc`,`Transdermal patches`],[`VI`,`Rotating cylinder`,`Transdermal patches`],[`VII`,`Reciprocating holder`,`Small volume media, implants and stents`]],caption:`Vessel volume 900 or 1000 mL, medium at 37 plus or minus 0.5 degrees C.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Friability sample size`,text:`For tablets weighing 650 mg or less, take a sample of whole tablets nearest to 6.5 g. For tablets above 650 mg, take 10 tablets. Any tablet that caps, cracks or splits during the test fails outright, whatever the weight loss.`}]},{topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced tablet questions turn on compression physics, the classification of tablet types with named examples, and the quantitative treatment of granule and powder properties.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Stages of compression`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Die filling and initial repacking, where particles slide and rearrange under low pressure.`,`Elastic deformation, which is fully recoverable and stores energy that later causes capping.`,`Plastic deformation, which is permanent and forms most of the bonds (microcrystalline cellulose is the model plastic material).`,`Brittle fracture, where particles fragment and present clean surfaces for bonding (lactose and dicalcium phosphate fragment).`,`Decompression and ejection, when stored elastic energy is released and radial die wall pressure must be overcome.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`ln (1 / (1 - D)) = K P + A     (Heckel equation)`,caption:`D is the relative density of the compact at applied pressure P (MPa). K is the slope; its reciprocal is the mean yield pressure Py. A low Py means the material deforms plastically at low pressure.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Carr compressibility index = 100 x (tapped density minus bulk density) / tapped density.`,`Hausner ratio = tapped density / bulk density. Below 1.25 is good flow, above 1.5 is poor.`,`Angle of repose 25 to 30 degrees is excellent, 31 to 35 good, 36 to 40 fair, 41 to 45 passable, above 46 poor.`,`Compressibility index up to 10 percent is excellent, 11 to 15 good, 16 to 20 fair, 21 to 25 passable, above 26 poor.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Hardness is not crushing strength divided by anything`,text:`Tablet hardness readings from different testers are not interchangeable. Strong-Cobb units are roughly 1.4 times Monsanto kilogram readings for the same tablet, so a specification must name the instrument. Tensile strength, calculated as 2F divided by pi times diameter times thickness, is the instrument-independent quantity.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Types of tablets`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Route / class`,`Type`,`Note`],rows:[[`Oral, swallowed`,`Compressed, multiple compressed, sugar/film/enteric coated`,`Multiple compressed includes layered and compression coated`],[`Oral, in the mouth`,`Chewable, lozenge (troche), buccal, sublingual`,`Sublingual and buccal avoid first pass metabolism`],[`Oral, in liquid`,`Effervescent, dispersible, soluble, tablet triturate`,`Effervescent must be packed with a desiccant`],[`Other routes`,`Vaginal, implant (pellet), hypodermic`,`Implants are sterile and give release over months`],[`Modified release`,`Sustained, controlled, delayed, pulsatile`,`Enteric coating is delayed release, not sustained release`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Compression coated (dry coated) tablets are used to separate two incompatible ingredients, one in the core and one in the coat.`,`Molded tablet triturates are made by forcing a moist mass into a mould plate, not by compression.`,`Mannitol is the diluent of choice for chewable tablets because its negative heat of solution gives a cooling mouthfeel and it is non-hygroscopic.`,`Directly compressible grades include spray-dried lactose, microcrystalline cellulose and unmilled dicalcium phosphate dihydrate.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Magnesium stearate, the classic double-edged excipient`,text:`It is the most effective common lubricant but it is hydrophobic and forms a film over granule surfaces. Over-blending, typically beyond about five minutes, lowers tablet hardness, slows disintegration and retards dissolution. This is why the lubricant is always added last and blended for the shortest validated time.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Dissolution efficiency = (area under the dissolution curve up to time t / (100 x t)) x 100`,caption:`DE is expressed as a percentage of the rectangle described by 100 percent dissolution over the same time. It compares whole profiles rather than a single time point.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`f2 = 50 x log { [1 + (1/n) x sum(Rt - Tt)^2]^-0.5 x 100 }`,caption:`Similarity factor. Rt and Tt are percent dissolved for reference and test at each of n time points. Values of f2 between 50 and 100 mean the profiles are similar.`}]},{topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Coating means applying a thin layer of material to the outside of a tablet, capsule or granule. The core is made first and the coat is applied afterwards in a rotating pan or a fluidised bed. Nothing about the drug changes; only what surrounds it does.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Mask a bitter taste or an unpleasant odour.`,`Protect the drug from moisture, light and air.`,`Make swallowing easier and the tablet more elegant.`,`Give the product a distinctive colour and identity.`,`Separate two incompatible ingredients, one in the core and one in the coat.`,`Control where and how fast the drug is released.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The four kinds of coating`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`What it is`,`Weight gain`],rows:[[`Sugar coating`,`Successive layers of sucrose syrup built up in a pan`,`30 to 50 percent, sometimes more`],[`Film coating`,`A thin polymer membrane sprayed from solution or dispersion`,`2 to 4 percent`],[`Enteric coating`,`A film of an acid-insoluble polymer`,`4 to 8 percent`],[`Compression (dry) coating`,`A dry powder coat compressed around a preformed core`,`Substantial; a tablet around a tablet`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why enteric coating exists`,text:`Two reasons only: to protect an acid-labile drug such as omeprazole or erythromycin from gastric acid, and to protect the stomach from an irritant drug such as aspirin or diclofenac. It is delayed release, not sustained release.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Equipment`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Conventional (standard) coating pan: a pear or hexagonal pan with drying air blown onto the bed surface. Slow and inefficient, used mostly for sugar coating.`,`Perforated pan: the pan wall is perforated so drying air is drawn through the tablet bed. Trade names include Accela-Cota, Hi-Coater, Driacoater and Glatt coater.`,`Fluidised bed (Wurster) coater: tablets or pellets are suspended in an air stream and sprayed from the bottom. The most efficient, and the standard for coating pellets.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`A film coating solution has four parts: the polymer that forms the film, a plasticiser that makes the film flexible, a colourant or opacifier, and the solvent or the water that carries it all. The solvent evaporates; only the first three stay on the tablet.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Film coating formula`,text:`P-P-C-S: Polymer, Plasticiser, Colourant, Solvent. Four ingredients, and only the solvent leaves.`}]},{topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Exam questions on coating concentrate on three things: the ordered stages of sugar coating, which polymers are enteric and at what pH they dissolve, and the coating defects with their causes.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Sugar coating, in order`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Sealing (waterproofing): shellac, cellulose acetate phthalate, PVP or zein is applied to stop syrup soaking into the core.`,`Subcoating: alternating gum solution and dusting powder (calcium carbonate, talc, acacia) rounds the sharp edges and builds bulk.`,`Smoothing (grossing): dilute sucrose syrup fills in the roughness and gives a smooth white surface, often with titanium dioxide.`,`Colouring: dilute syrup containing a lake pigment is applied in many thin coats.`,`Polishing: waxes such as beeswax and carnauba wax are applied in a canvas-lined polishing pan to give gloss.`,`Printing: the identifying mark is printed with edible ink, since a sugar coated tablet cannot be engraved.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Sugar coating order`,text:`Seal, Sub, Smooth, Colour, Polish, Print. The first three all begin with S and come before any colour is added.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Coating polymers`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Polymer`,`Class`,`Solubility behaviour`],rows:[[`HPMC (hypromellose)`,`Non-enteric film former`,`Soluble across the pH range; the industry default`],[`Hydroxypropyl cellulose`,`Non-enteric film former`,`Water soluble, tacky, usually combined with HPMC`],[`Ethylcellulose`,`Non-enteric, insoluble`,`Water insoluble; used for sustained release`],[`Eudragit E100`,`Aminoalkyl methacrylate`,`Dissolves below pH 5, so taste masking and gastric release`],[`Cellulose acetate phthalate (CAP)`,`Enteric`,`Dissolves above about pH 6; hydrolyses on storage, poor moisture barrier`],[`HPMC phthalate (HPMCP)`,`Enteric`,`HP-50 dissolves at pH 5.0, HP-55 at pH 5.5`],[`Polyvinyl acetate phthalate`,`Enteric`,`Dissolves above about pH 5`],[`Eudragit L100-55 / L30D-55`,`Enteric methacrylate`,`Dissolves above pH 5.5`],[`Eudragit L100`,`Enteric methacrylate`,`Dissolves above pH 6.0`],[`Eudragit S100`,`Enteric methacrylate`,`Dissolves above pH 7.0; used for colonic delivery`],[`Eudragit RL / RS`,`Ammonio methacrylate`,`Insoluble but permeable; RL more permeable than RS`],[`Shellac`,`Natural enteric resin`,`Dissolves only above about pH 7; ages and slows release`]],caption:`All enteric polymers carry ionisable acidic groups (phthalate or carboxylic acid) that stay unionised in gastric acid and ionise in the intestine.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Plasticisers: propylene glycol, glycerin, polyethylene glycol, triacetin, triethyl citrate, dibutyl sebacate, diethyl phthalate, castor oil.`,`A plasticiser lowers the glass transition temperature of the film, which reduces cracking and bridging.`,`Opacifier: titanium dioxide. Colourants are used as insoluble lakes to avoid migration.`,`Aqueous dispersions such as Aquacoat (ethylcellulose) and Eudragit L30D-55 avoid organic solvents but need curing after coating.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Film coating defects`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Defect`,`Appearance`,`Cause`,`Remedy`],rows:[[`Blistering`,`Local lifting of film from the core`,`Too fast drying, trapped solvent vapour`,`Reduce inlet air temperature, dry gently`],[`Blushing`,`Whitish haze in the film`,`Coating temperature too high, polymer precipitates`,`Lower process temperature, change solvent ratio`],[`Bridging of the logo`,`Film lifts out of the engraving`,`Film shrinkage, poor adhesion, low plasticiser`,`Increase plasticiser, reduce solids, redesign the engraving`],[`Infilling`,`Monogram filled in and lost`,`Foam or bubbles in the coating liquid`,`Add an antifoam or alcohol, lower spray rate`],[`Orange peel (roughness)`,`Pebbled, uneven surface`,`Spray dried droplets, viscous solution, high spray rate`,`Thin the solution, lower atomising pressure`],[`Cracking / splitting`,`Cracks across the film`,`Internal stress exceeds film strength`,`Use a lower molecular weight polymer, more plasticiser`],[`Picking and sticking`,`Tablets adhere to each other`,`Over-wetting, spray rate above drying rate`,`Increase drying air, reduce spray rate, raise pan speed`],[`Twinning`,`Two tablets stuck together`,`Capsule shaped tablets, over-wetting`,`Reduce spray rate, increase pan speed, change tablet shape`],[`Mottling / colour variation`,`Uneven colour`,`Poor mixing, migration of soluble dye, uneven spray`,`Use lakes, improve spray distribution and mixing`],[`Pitting`,`Pits in the core surface under the film`,`Bed temperature above the melting point of a core component`,`Lower inlet air temperature`],[`Chipping`,`Film broken at edges and crown`,`Too low plasticiser, high pan speed, brittle film`,`Increase plasticiser, reduce mechanical stress`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Blushing versus blooming`,text:`Blushing is a whitish haze appearing during coating because the polymer precipitates at too high a temperature. Blooming is a dull, hazy film that develops on storage, usually because a low molecular weight plasticiser migrates to the surface. Same look, different timing and cause.`}]},{topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced level is about process control: how a coating film actually forms, the thermodynamic balance of the pan, and the tests applied to a coated product.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Film formation from an aqueous dispersion`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Water evaporates and the latex particles pack closely together at the tablet surface.`,`Capillary forces from the receding water menisci deform the particles into a continuous layer.`,`Coalescence and interdiffusion of polymer chains across particle boundaries complete the film.`,`Curing, typically 40 to 60 degrees C for several hours, drives coalescence to completion and stabilises the release profile.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Minimum film forming temperature`,text:`An aqueous polymeric dispersion will only coalesce above its minimum film forming temperature, which is close to the plasticised glass transition temperature. Coating below MFFT gives a porous, non-continuous film and a sustained release product whose dissolution keeps drifting on stability.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Uncured aqueous ethylcellulose films continue to coalesce slowly during storage, so release slows over months. Curing prevents this.`,`Talc or glyceryl monostearate is added as an antitacking agent to aqueous dispersions.`,`Organic solvent coating gives instant film formation but brings solvent residue limits, explosion risk and recovery cost.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Process variables`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Variable`,`If too high`,`If too low`],rows:[[`Spray rate`,`Over-wetting, picking, twinning`,`Spray drying, orange peel, poor coverage`],[`Inlet air temperature`,`Blushing, pitting, spray drying`,`Over-wetting, tablet erosion`],[`Pan speed`,`Attrition and edge chipping`,`Poor mixing and colour variation`],[`Atomising air pressure`,`Spray drying, coating loss`,`Large droplets, orange peel`],[`Gun to bed distance`,`Droplets dry before landing`,`Localised over-wetting`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Evaluation of a coated product`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Coat weight gain and coating uniformity by individual tablet weighing before and after.`,`Film thickness and adhesion, measured by the tensile force needed to peel the film.`,`Disintegration: 30 minutes for film coated, 60 minutes for sugar coated.`,`Enteric integrity: no disintegration and, for delayed release, not more than 10 percent drug released after 2 hours in 0.1 M hydrochloric acid, followed by a buffer stage at pH 6.8.`,`Accelerated stability to confirm that dissolution does not drift, especially for aqueous film coats.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Compression coating is not film coating`,text:`Compression coating uses no solvent at all. A preformed core is centred in a die and coating granulation is compressed around it on a special press such as the Drycota. It suits moisture sensitive drugs and physically separates incompatible ingredients, but core centring is difficult and any off-centre core gives a thin wall that fails.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Coat weight gain (percent) = [(Wc - Wu) / Wu] x 100`,caption:`Wu is the mean weight of uncoated tablets and Wc the mean weight after coating, both in mg. This is the routine in-process control for a coating run.`}]},{topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A capsule is a dose of medicine enclosed in a soluble shell, almost always made of gelatin. The patient swallows the shell, it dissolves in the stomach and the contents are released. Capsules come in two families: hard gelatin capsules made of two pieces, and soft gelatin capsules made as one sealed piece.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Hard gelatin capsule`,`Soft gelatin capsule`],rows:[[`Construction`,`Two pieces: body and cap`,`One piece, sealed in the same operation`],[`Made and filled`,`Shell made first, filled later, often elsewhere`,`Shell formed and filled in one continuous step`],[`Contents`,`Powders, granules, pellets, mini-tablets`,`Oils, solutions, suspensions, pastes`],[`Plasticiser content`,`Little or none`,`High: glycerin or sorbitol, 20 to 30 percent of shell`],[`Moisture in shell`,`13 to 16 percent`,`6 to 13 percent when dry`],[`Shape`,`Cylindrical only`,`Oval, oblong, round, tube, suppository shapes`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Where gelatin comes from`,text:`Gelatin is obtained by the partial hydrolysis of collagen from animal skin, bone and connective tissue. Type A is produced by acid hydrolysis, usually of pork skin, with an isoelectric point around pH 7 to 9. Type B is produced by alkaline (lime) hydrolysis, usually of bone, with an isoelectric point around pH 4.7 to 5.2.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Capsule sizes`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Human capsule sizes run 000, 00, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.`,`Size 000 is the largest; size 5 is the smallest. The bigger the number, the smaller the capsule.`,`Approximate capacities: 000 about 1.37 mL, 00 about 0.95 mL, 0 about 0.68 mL, 1 about 0.50 mL, 2 about 0.37 mL, 3 about 0.30 mL, 4 about 0.21 mL, 5 about 0.13 mL.`,`Sizes 0 to 2 cover most commercial products; sizes above 00 are hard for patients to swallow.`,`Veterinary bolus sizes 10, 11 and 12 exist and are much larger, up to about 30 mL.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Capsule size direction`,text:`Numbers go up as the capsule goes down. 5 is the tiniest, 000 the biggest. Read the zeros as "extra large".`},{kind:`para`,text:`The shell itself contains gelatin, water, a colourant, an opacifier such as titanium dioxide, and sometimes a preservative. Capsules are made by dipping cold stainless steel pins into a warm gelatin solution, rotating them so the film is even, drying them in controlled humidity air, stripping, trimming to length and joining the cap onto the body.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Advantages: taste and odour are masked automatically, the shell is easy to swallow, filling needs no compression and dissolution is usually fast.`,`Disadvantages: gelatin is animal derived, deliquescent and hygroscopic drugs cannot be filled, highly soluble salts can irritate the gastric mucosa, and capsules cost more than tablets.`]}]},{topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level you need the manufacturing routes for both capsule types, the two filling principles used by automatic machines, alternatives to gelatin, and the official evaluation limits.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Making soft gelatin capsules`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Process`,`Principle`,`Notes`],rows:[[`Rotary die (Scherer)`,`Two gelatin ribbons meet between rotating dies; fill is injected`,`The industry standard; continuous and accurate`],[`Plate process`,`Fill placed on a gelatin sheet in a mould plate, second sheet on top`,`Older, batch process, largely obsolete`],[`Reciprocating die (Accogel)`,`Ribbons pass over a reciprocating die row`,`Can encapsulate dry powders as well as liquids`],[`Bubble / seamless (Globex)`,`Concentric nozzle drops fill inside a gelatin droplet into chilled oil`,`Gives seamless spherical pearls`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Soft gelatin shell composition is roughly gelatin 1 part, plasticiser 0.4 to 0.6 part and water 1 part, with the water removed on drying.`,`The plasticiser to gelatin ratio decides shell hardness: more glycerin gives a softer, more flexible capsule.`,`Fill liquids must have a pH between about 2.5 and 7.5; a strongly acidic fill hydrolyses gelatin, a strongly alkaline fill tans it.`,`Water, low molecular weight alcohols, ketones and ammonium salts cannot be used as the fill because they dissolve or migrate into the shell.`,`Bloom strength measures gel rigidity; hard capsule gelatin is typically 150 to 280 g bloom and soft capsule gelatin 150 to 200 g.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Filling hard gelatin capsules`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Principle`,`How the dose is measured`,`Example machines`],rows:[[`Dosator (dosing tube)`,`Powder is compressed into a tube by a piston and ejected`,`Zanasi, Macofar, MG2`],[`Tamping / dosing disc`,`Powder is tamped into holes in a rotating disc by pins`,`Hofliger-Karg, Bosch GKF`],[`Auger fill (semi-automatic)`,`A rotating screw pushes powder into the body`,`Older semi-automatic fillers`],[`Hand filling`,`Punch method or hand-operated filling machine`,`Extemporaneous and small scale`]],caption:`Both automatic principles form a plug of powder, so hard capsule powders still need good flow and a lubricant, typically magnesium stearate at 0.5 to 1 percent.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Alternatives to gelatin`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`HPMC (hypromellose) capsules: vegetarian and halal acceptable, low moisture content so they suit hygroscopic fills, and they do not become brittle on drying. Dissolution can be slower and more variable in some media.`,`Pullulan capsules: a fungal polysaccharide shell, very low oxygen permeability.`,`Starch capsules: made by injection moulding, used for enteric and colonic delivery systems.`,`Crosslinking is the main gelatin problem: aldehyde traces, heat and humidity form a pellicle that will not dissolve. Dissolution is repeated with added enzyme (pepsin or pancreatin) when crosslinking is suspected.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Evaluation`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Test`,`Requirement`],rows:[[`Uniformity of weight`,`20 capsules; IP limit 10 percent if average content is under 300 mg, 7.5 percent if 300 mg or more`],[`Disintegration`,`Hard and soft gelatin capsules: within 30 minutes in water at 37 degrees C, with discs`],[`Enteric coated capsules`,`No disintegration in 0.1 M HCl for 2 hours, then within 60 minutes in pH 6.8 buffer`],[`Dissolution`,`Usually USP apparatus I (basket) at 100 rpm, because capsules float on a paddle`],[`Content uniformity`,`10 units, 85 to 115 percent of label claim`],[`Moisture content`,`Shell 13 to 16 percent for hard gelatin; loss on drying on the whole capsule`]],caption:`In the weight uniformity test not more than two capsules may exceed the stated percentage and none may exceed twice it.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Why basket and not paddle for capsules`,text:`A gelatin capsule shell is less dense than the medium, so it floats up against the vessel wall under a paddle and dissolution becomes erratic. The basket holds it in the flow. If a paddle must be used, a sinker is added, and the sinker type has to be specified because it changes the profile.`}]},{topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced capsule work concerns shell manufacture detail, capsule sealing and tamper evidence, fill selection for soft gelatin, and the stability problems that only capsules have.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Hard shell manufacture in sequence`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Dipping: room temperature stainless steel pins are dipped into a 25 to 30 percent gelatin solution held at about 50 degrees C.`,`Spinning (rotation): the pins are rotated so the gelatin distributes evenly and no bead forms at the tip.`,`Drying: kiln air of controlled temperature and humidity removes water; only evaporative drying is used, since heat would melt the film.`,`Stripping: the dried shells are pushed off the pins by brass jaws.`,`Trimming: rotating knives cut body and cap to the exact length.`,`Joining: cap and body are pushed together to the pre-lock position and printed.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Pre-lock versus final lock`,text:`Hard capsules carry two sets of indentations. The pre-lock position keeps the empty capsule assembled for transport without gripping the body tightly, so the filling machine can separate the halves. The final lock, engaged after filling, prevents accidental opening.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Banding with a gelatin band around the cap-body join gives tamper evidence and prevents leakage of liquid fills.`,`Liquid filled hard capsules (LFHC) use thixotropic or thermosoftening fills such as PEG or gelucires, and are sealed by banding or by hydroalcoholic fusion sealing.`,`Capsules can be made enteric by coating the finished capsule, by banding with an enteric polymer, or by filling enteric coated pellets into a normal shell. Filling coated pellets is the most robust.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Stability problems specific to capsules`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Problem`,`Cause`,`Control`],rows:[[`Brittleness`,`Shell moisture falls below about 10 percent`,`Store at 40 to 60 percent RH; avoid desiccant excess`],[`Softening / sticking`,`Shell moisture rises above about 18 percent`,`Tight containers, controlled packing room humidity`],[`Crosslinking`,`Aldehydes from excipients or packaging, heat, humidity, light`,`Avoid reducing sugars and peroxide-bearing polymers; add enzymes to the dissolution medium`],[`Fill migration into the shell`,`Low molecular weight polar liquids in soft capsules`,`Restrict fill to water-immiscible or high molecular weight liquids`],[`Colour fading`,`Light-sensitive dyes`,`Opacify with titanium dioxide, use amber packaging`]]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Fill weight (mg) = capsule body volume (mL) x tapped density (g/mL) x 1000`,caption:`Used to select a capsule size. The tapped or plug density is used rather than bulk density because both dosator and tamping machines consolidate the powder before ejecting it.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Choosing the size is a density problem, not a weight problem`,text:`Two powders of the same 300 mg dose may need different capsule sizes if their densities differ. A light, fluffy powder of tapped density 0.4 g/mL needs about 0.75 mL and therefore size 0, while a dense powder at 0.9 g/mL needs only about 0.33 mL and fits a size 3.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Soft gelatin capsules give the best bioavailability for poorly water soluble drugs, because the drug is already in solution in a lipid or PEG vehicle.`,`Soft gelatin is preferred for very low dose potent drugs, since a solution fill avoids the content uniformity risk of a powder blend.`,`Rectal and vaginal soft capsules are made in tube and suppository shapes and often carry a wipe-off finish.`]}]},{topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A parenteral preparation is a sterile product given by injection, infusion or implantation, bypassing the gastrointestinal tract. The word comes from para enteron, meaning beside the intestine. Because the product goes straight past every natural barrier, it must be sterile, free of pyrogens, free of visible particles and, ideally, close to the pH and tonicity of blood.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Routes of administration`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Route`,`Usual volume`,`Notes`],rows:[[`Intravenous (IV)`,`1 mL to several litres`,`Aqueous only; no oils or suspensions; instant and complete effect`],[`Intramuscular (IM)`,`Up to 2 mL deltoid, 5 mL gluteal`,`Oils, suspensions and depots allowed; deltoid, gluteus, vastus lateralis`],[`Subcutaneous (SC)`,`1 to 2 mL`,`Must be near isotonic and non-irritant; insulin, heparin, vaccines`],[`Intradermal (ID)`,`About 0.1 mL`,`Diagnostic and sensitivity tests, BCG`],[`Intrathecal`,`Small, into the CSF`,`Must be preservative free, isotonic and of low volume`],[`Intra-articular`,`Into a joint space`,`Corticosteroid suspensions`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Only IV gives 100 percent bioavailability`,text:`There is no absorption step, so the whole dose reaches the systemic circulation immediately. That is also why an IV dose cannot be recalled and why IV products must never contain particles or air.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Vehicles`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Aqueous: Water for Injection is the standard vehicle. Sodium chloride injection and Ringer solution are also used.`,`Water miscible cosolvents: ethanol, propylene glycol, polyethylene glycol 300 and 400, glycerin. Used to dissolve poorly soluble drugs.`,`Non-aqueous: fixed vegetable oils such as arachis (groundnut), sesame, cottonseed, castor and maize oil, used for oily depot injections given intramuscularly.`,`Mineral oil is never used as a parenteral vehicle because it is not metabolised and causes granulomas.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Additives used in injections include antimicrobial preservatives (only for multi-dose containers), antioxidants such as sodium metabisulphite and ascorbic acid, chelating agents such as disodium edetate, buffers, tonicity adjusters such as sodium chloride and dextrose, and suspending or solubilising agents.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Preservatives are forbidden in some injections`,text:`No antimicrobial preservative may be used in single dose products for the intrathecal, intracisternal, epidural or intraocular route, nor in any injection intended to be given in a volume above 15 mL, nor in a large volume parenteral.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`What every injection must be`,text:`S-P-P-I: Sterile, Pyrogen free, Particle free, Isotonic where possible. Four words that cover most of the quality control chapter.`}]},{topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core of parenterals is Water for Injection and its specification, pyrogens and the two tests that detect them, isotonicity arithmetic, glass container selection, choice of sterilisation route, and the quality control battery.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Water for Injection`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Produced by distillation, most often in a multiple effect still or a vapour compression still. Modern pharmacopoeias also permit reverse osmosis and other equivalent purification trains under validated control.`,`Stored either at above 80 degrees C in a continuously circulating loop, or below 25 degrees C and used within 24 hours of collection.`,`It is pyrogen free but not necessarily sterile; Sterile Water for Injection is WFI that has been packed and sterilised.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Attribute`,`Typical limit`],rows:[[`pH`,`5.0 to 7.0`],[`Conductivity at 25 degrees C`,`Not more than 1.3 microsiemens per cm`],[`Total organic carbon`,`Not more than 500 ppb (0.5 mg/L)`],[`Bacterial endotoxins`,`Less than 0.25 EU per mL`],[`Total aerobic microbial count`,`Not more than 10 CFU per 100 mL (action limit)`],[`Nitrates, heavy metals`,`Complies with the monograph limit tests`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Pyrogens and endotoxins`},{kind:`para`,text:`Pyrogens are substances that produce fever when injected. The most important are bacterial endotoxins, which are lipopolysaccharides from the outer cell wall of Gram-negative bacteria. The pyrogenic activity resides in the lipid A portion. Endotoxins are thermostable, survive autoclaving, are water soluble, non-volatile and are not removed by ordinary sterilising filters.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Depyrogenation of glass and metal: dry heat at 250 degrees C for 30 minutes, or 180 degrees C for longer, destroying the molecule.`,`Removal from solution: adsorption on activated charcoal, ultrafiltration, reverse osmosis, distillation, anion exchange.`,`Prevention is the real answer: WFI freshly produced, clean components, short holding times and low bioburden.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Rabbit pyrogen test`,`LAL bacterial endotoxin test`],rows:[[`Basis`,`Rise in rectal temperature after IV injection`,`Gelation of Limulus amoebocyte lysate by endotoxin`],[`Source of reagent`,`Live healthy rabbits`,`Amoebocyte lysate of the horseshoe crab Limulus polyphemus`],[`Detects`,`All pyrogens, endotoxin and non-endotoxin`,`Gram-negative bacterial endotoxin only`],[`Sensitivity`,`About 0.5 to 1 ng/mL`,`0.03 to 0.5 EU/mL depending on lysate`],[`Time`,`About 3 hours plus animal conditioning`,`15 to 60 minutes`],[`Result`,`Qualitative pass or fail`,`Gel-clot limit test, or quantitative by turbidimetry or chromogenic assay`],[`Limitations`,`Animals, cost, variability, not for radiopharmaceuticals or cytotoxics`,`Misses fungal and viral pyrogens; interference needs inhibition testing`]],caption:`IP rabbit test: three rabbits, injection into the ear vein within 10 minutes. The product passes if no rabbit shows a rise of 0.6 degrees C or more and the sum of the three rises is not more than 1.4 degrees C.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Isotonicity`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Sodium chloride required (g per 100 mL) = 0.9 - (E x C)`,caption:`E is the sodium chloride equivalent of the drug, the weight of NaCl with the same osmotic effect as 1 g of drug. C is the drug concentration in g per 100 mL. The answer is the extra NaCl needed to make the solution isotonic.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`W = (0.52 - a) / b`,caption:`Freezing point method. W is grams of adjusting substance per 100 mL, 0.52 is the freezing point depression of blood and tears in degrees C, a is the depression produced by the drug already present, and b is the depression produced by 1 percent w/v of the adjusting substance (0.576 for sodium chloride).`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Isotonic solutions: 0.9 percent w/v sodium chloride, 5 percent w/v dextrose, 1.9 percent w/v boric acid.`,`Blood and tears freeze at minus 0.52 degrees C and have an osmolarity of about 290 to 310 mOsm/L.`,`Hypotonic solutions cause haemolysis; hypertonic solutions cause crenation and pain on injection.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Choosing the sterilisation route`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`Conditions`,`Applied to`],rows:[[`Moist heat (autoclave)`,`121 degrees C for 15 min, or 115 degrees C for 30 min`,`Aqueous injections, dressings, media; the first choice`],[`Dry heat`,`160 C for 2 h, 170 C for 1 h, 180 C for 30 min`,`Glassware, metal, fixed oils, powders; also depyrogenation at 250 C`],[`Filtration`,`0.22 micrometre membrane, then aseptic filling`,`Heat labile solutions such as vitamins, hormones, antibiotics`],[`Gaseous`,`Ethylene oxide with humidity, then aeration`,`Plastic devices, catheters, heat labile equipment`],[`Radiation`,`Gamma from cobalt-60, usually 25 kGy`,`Disposable syringes, sutures, prepacked single use items`]],caption:`Terminal sterilisation is always preferred. Aseptic processing is used only when the product cannot survive terminal treatment.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Glass type selection for injections`,text:`Type I borosilicate for all parenterals, and mandatory for alkaline or highly sensitive products. Type II surface-treated soda-lime for acidic and neutral aqueous parenterals. Type III soda-lime for non-aqueous parenterals and for powders for injection. Type NP is for oral and topical use only, never for injections.`}]},{topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced level covers the full quality control battery with its numerical limits, and lyophilisation stage by stage with the physical reason each stage exists.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Quality control of parenterals`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Test`,`Method`,`Requirement`],rows:[[`Sterility`,`Membrane filtration or direct inoculation`,`FTM at 30 to 35 C for anaerobes, SCDM at 20 to 25 C for aerobes and fungi, 14 days`],[`Bacterial endotoxin`,`LAL gel-clot, turbidimetric or chromogenic`,`Below the endotoxin limit K/M for the product`],[`Pyrogen`,`Rabbit rectal temperature`,`Sum of three rises not more than 1.4 C, none 0.6 C or more`],[`Clarity / visible particles`,`Inspection against black and white panels`,`Illumination about 2000 to 3750 lux; free from visible particles`],[`Sub-visible particles (LVP)`,`Light obscuration, method 1`,`Not more than 25 per mL at 10 micrometre, 3 per mL at 25 micrometre`],[`Sub-visible particles (SVP)`,`Light obscuration, method 1`,`Not more than 6000 per container at 10 um, 600 at 25 um`],[`Leak test`,`Dye (methylene blue) vacuum test, or spark tester`,`No dye ingress into any sealed ampoule`],[`Uniformity of volume`,`Withdraw with a dry syringe and measure`,`Not less than the labelled volume; excess volume is filled to allow for holdup`]]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Endotoxin limit = K / M`,caption:`K is the threshold pyrogenic dose, 5 EU per kg body weight for most routes and 0.2 EU per kg for the intrathecal route. M is the maximum human dose per kg administered in one hour. The result is in EU per mg or EU per mL.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Worked endotoxin limit`,text:`For a drug whose maximum dose is 10 mg per kg in one hour by the IV route, the limit is 5 divided by 10, which is 0.5 EU per mg of drug. If the injection contains 20 mg/mL, the solution limit is 10 EU per mL.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Lyophilisation (freeze drying)`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Stage`,`What happens`,`Purpose`],rows:[[`Freezing`,`Product cooled below the eutectic or glass transition temperature`,`Immobilise the solute and form the ice crystal network that becomes the pore structure`],[`Annealing (optional)`,`Hold above the glass transition, then re-cool`,`Grow larger ice crystals, giving faster sublimation and a more uniform cake`],[`Primary drying`,`Chamber pressure taken below the triple point, shelf heated gently`,`Sublime the frozen free water; removes about 90 percent of total water`],[`Secondary drying`,`Shelf raised to 30 to 50 degrees C at deep vacuum`,`Desorb the bound, unfrozen water down to 0.5 to 3 percent residual moisture`],[`Stoppering`,`Partial stoppers pushed home inside the chamber`,`Seal under vacuum or under nitrogen before exposure to room air`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The triple point of water is 0.0098 degrees C at 4.58 mm Hg. Primary drying must be run below that pressure so ice sublimes instead of melting.`,`Product temperature during primary drying must stay below the collapse temperature, otherwise the cake shrinks, loses porosity and reconstitutes poorly.`,`Bulking agent: mannitol, which crystallises and gives a firm elegant cake. Lyoprotectants: sucrose and trehalose, which stay amorphous and protect proteins.`,`Advantages: heat labile drugs are preserved, moisture is very low so shelf life is long, and reconstitution is rapid because the cake is porous.`,`Disadvantages: slow, expensive, needs sterile diluent and an extra reconstitution step at the bedside.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Eutectic point versus glass transition`,text:`A crystalline solute such as sodium chloride has a eutectic point, the temperature at which the last liquid solidifies, and the product must be frozen below it. An amorphous solute such as sucrose has no eutectic point; it has a glass transition temperature of the maximally freeze-concentrated solution, and collapse occurs just above it. Applying the eutectic rule to an amorphous formulation is a common error.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`F0 = sum of 10^((T - 121)/z) x delta t`,caption:`F0 is the equivalent time in minutes at 121 degrees C delivered to the product. T is the product temperature in degrees C, z is 10 degrees C for moist heat, and delta t is the time interval in minutes. An F0 of at least 8 to 12 minutes is normally required for terminal sterilisation.`}]},{topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Ophthalmic preparations are sterile products applied to the eye: eye drops, eye lotions, eye ointments, gels and inserts. The eye has no natural defence against injected or instilled organisms once the cornea is damaged, so sterility is not negotiable. Everything else in the formulation exists to make the drop comfortable and to keep it on the eye long enough to work.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`What an ophthalmic product must be`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Sterile, and free from fungal and bacterial contamination.`,`Isotonic, or close to it, so it does not sting.`,`At a comfortable pH, buffered weakly so tears can bring it back to normal.`,`Free from visible particles, which would scratch the cornea.`,`Of a suitable viscosity, so it stays in contact instead of draining away.`,`Preserved if it is a multi-dose container, and preservative free if it is for surgery or an injured eye.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The tonicity range the eye tolerates`,text:`The eye is comfortable with solutions equivalent to 0.9 percent w/v sodium chloride, and tolerates roughly 0.5 to 1.8 percent w/v equivalents without pain. Tears freeze at minus 0.52 degrees C, the same as blood, and their normal pH is about 7.4.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Only a small fraction of an eye drop is absorbed. A drop delivers about 50 microlitres but the cul-de-sac holds only 7 to 10 microlitres, so most is lost immediately down the nasolacrimal duct. With tear turnover of roughly 1 microlitre per minute washing away the rest, typically only 1 to 10 percent of the dose reaches the intraocular tissues.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Dosage form`,`Feature`],rows:[[`Eye drops (solution)`,`Simplest, but shortest contact time`],[`Eye drops (suspension)`,`For insoluble drugs such as corticosteroids; must be resuspended`],[`Eye ointment`,`Long contact, but blurs vision; base must be sterile`],[`Eye gel / in situ gel`,`Liquid on instillation, gels on the eye surface`],[`Eye lotion`,`Large volume for irrigation; no preservative, use within 24 hours of opening`],[`Ocular insert`,`Solid or semisolid placed in the cul-de-sac for prolonged release`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`The five checks on an eye drop`,text:`S-T-P-C-V: Sterility, Tonicity, pH, Clarity, Viscosity. Run through those five and you have covered the formulation requirements.`}]},{topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Exam questions on ophthalmics concentrate on the preservatives, which ones are excluded and why, the buffer and viscosity systems, the ocular inserts by name, and the evaluation tests.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Preservatives`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Preservative`,`Usual concentration`,`Note`],rows:[[`Benzalkonium chloride`,`0.004 to 0.02 percent`,`The most used; cationic quaternary, rapid, wide spectrum`],[`Chlorhexidine acetate`,`0.01 percent`,`Good against Pseudomonas; cationic`],[`Phenylmercuric nitrate or acetate`,`0.002 percent`,`Organomercurial, slow acting, use restricted on toxicity grounds`],[`Thiomersal`,`0.005 to 0.01 percent`,`Useful where BKC is incompatible, but sensitising`],[`Chlorobutanol`,`0.5 percent`,`Volatile; decomposes above 25 degrees C and above pH 6`],[`Methyl and propyl parabens`,`0.05 and 0.02 percent`,`Weak against Pseudomonas, so rarely used alone`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Benzalkonium chloride is cationic, so it is incompatible with anionic drugs such as fluorescein sodium, sodium salicylate, nitrates and sulphacetamide sodium.`,`Disodium edetate 0.01 to 0.1 percent is added with benzalkonium chloride to potentiate the action against Pseudomonas aeruginosa by chelating the metal ions in the cell wall.`,`Benzalkonium chloride binds to soft contact lenses and must be avoided in lens care solutions.`,`Phenol, cresol and benzoic acid are unsuitable for ophthalmic use because they are too irritant at effective concentrations.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`When preservative is prohibited`,text:`No preservative is permitted in intraocular solutions, in preparations for use during eye surgery, or in products applied to an eye whose cornea has been injured. These must be supplied in single dose, single use containers. Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection of an abraded cornea can destroy the eye within 48 hours, which is why sterility here matters more than convenience.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Buffers, tonicity and viscosity`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Buffer systems used: boric acid vehicle, Sorensen phosphate buffer and Feldman borate buffer. Buffer capacity is kept low so that tears can restore physiological pH quickly.`,`The pH chosen is a compromise: it must keep the drug stable and soluble, and be tolerable to the eye. Pilocarpine nitrate drops are buffered around pH 5 for stability even though this is below comfort optimum.`,`Tonicity adjusters: sodium chloride, dextrose, boric acid, potassium chloride, mannitol.`,`Viscosity builders: methylcellulose 0.25 to 1 percent, hydroxypropyl methylcellulose, polyvinyl alcohol about 1.4 percent, povidone, sodium hyaluronate.`,`A viscosity of roughly 15 to 25 mPa s (cps) prolongs contact without blurring vision or feeling sticky.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Ocular inserts`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Insert`,`Polymer / basis`,`Behaviour`],rows:[[`Ocusert Pilo-20 and Pilo-40`,`Pilocarpine reservoir in ethylene vinyl acetate membranes`,`Insoluble, zero order release for 7 days, removed after use`],[`Lacrisert`,`Hydroxypropyl cellulose, drug free`,`Soluble rod for dry eye; lubricates as it dissolves`],[`SODI`,`Soluble acrylamide copolymer film`,`Dissolves completely; developed for cosmonauts`],[`NODS`,`Polyvinyl alcohol flag on a paper handle`,`Water soluble, delivers a precise small dose`],[`Collagen shield`,`Porcine or bovine collagen`,`Soluble corneal bandage that also carries drug`],[`Minidisc`,`Silicone based hydrophilic or hydrophobic disc`,`Insoluble contoured disc held in the cul-de-sac`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Manufacture and evaluation`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Preferred route: dissolve, filter through 0.22 micrometre, fill and seal, then terminally sterilise by autoclaving if the drug allows. Otherwise sterile filter and fill aseptically.`,`Containers: low density polyethylene dropper bottles (the Drop-Tainer type) and amber glass bottles with a dropper assembly. Plastic droppers are pre-sterilised by ethylene oxide or gamma radiation.`,`Evaluation of drops: sterility, clarity and particulate matter, pH, tonicity, viscosity, assay of drug, preservative efficacy testing and volume in container.`,`Evaluation of eye ointments: sterility, metal particle test, leakage test, uniformity of weight and drug content.`,`Metal particle test: 10 tubes are melted in flat dishes and examined; not more than one tube may contain more than eight particles of 50 micrometres or larger, and the total of all ten must not exceed 50 particles.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Eye lotions and irrigations are not eye drops`,text:`Large volume eye lotions and intraocular irrigating solutions carry no preservative at all and must be discarded within 24 hours of opening. Only small multi-dose drop containers are preserved.`}]},{topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced ophthalmic work is about the barriers to ocular penetration, the arithmetic of tonicity adjustment, and the strategies used to defeat the very short precorneal residence time.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Barriers to ocular absorption`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Precorneal loss: drainage through the nasolacrimal duct, tear dilution and turnover, and non-productive conjunctival absorption. This is the largest single loss.`,`The corneal epithelium is lipophilic and tightly junctioned; the stroma beneath it is hydrophilic. A drug must therefore have balanced lipophilicity, with log P around 2 to 3 being close to optimum.`,`The blood-aqueous barrier removes drug that has entered the anterior chamber.`,`Reflex blinking and lacrimation triggered by an irritant formulation multiply the loss.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Differential solubility of the cornea`,text:`Because the epithelium is lipid and the stroma is aqueous, a purely hydrophilic drug is stopped at the epithelium and a purely lipophilic one is stopped at the stroma. Drugs that exist in equilibrium between ionised and unionised forms, such as pilocarpine, penetrate best.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Tonicity calculation worked`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`E = 17 x Liso / M`,caption:`E is the sodium chloride equivalent, Liso the molar freezing point depression constant for the ionic class (1.9 for non-electrolytes, 3.4 for uni-univalent electrolytes, 4.3 for uni-divalent, 5.2 for di-univalent) and M the molecular weight in g/mol.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Worked isotonicity numerical`,text:`Make 30 mL of 1 percent w/v pilocarpine nitrate drops isotonic. The sodium chloride equivalent of pilocarpine nitrate is 0.23. NaCl represented by the drug is 0.23 x 1 = 0.23 g per 100 mL. NaCl still required is 0.9 minus 0.23 = 0.67 g per 100 mL, that is 0.67 x 30/100 = 0.201 g, about 201 mg for the 30 mL batch.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`White-Vincent method: V = w x E x 111.1, giving the volume in mL of water in which w grams of drug can be dissolved to make an isotonic solution, which is then made up to final volume with an isotonic diluting vehicle.`,`Sprowls method is the White-Vincent calculation pre-tabulated for 0.3 g of drug, so no arithmetic is needed at the bench.`,`The freezing point method uses 0.52 degrees C as the target depression and 0.576 as the depression caused by 1 percent w/v sodium chloride.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Prolonging residence time`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Strategy`,`Example system`,`Trigger`],rows:[[`Viscosity increase`,`HPMC, PVA, hyaluronate solutions`,`None; simply slower drainage`],[`In situ gelling by ions`,`Gellan gum (Gelrite) with timolol`,`Cations in the tear fluid`],[`In situ gelling by temperature`,`Poloxamer 407 systems`,`Rise from room to ocular temperature`],[`In situ gelling by pH`,`Cellulose acetate phthalate latex, carbomer`,`Rise from acidic formulation pH to tear pH 7.4`],[`Mucoadhesion`,`Carbomer, chitosan, sodium hyaluronate`,`Binding to conjunctival mucin`],[`Particulate carriers`,`Liposomes, niosomes, nanosuspensions`,`Slow release and mucoadhesion combined`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Viscosity has diminishing returns`,text:`Raising viscosity increases contact time only up to about 20 to 25 mPa s. Beyond that the reflex blink rate rises and lacrimation increases, so the extra drug is washed away faster and vision is blurred. More viscosity is not automatically more bioavailability.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Ophthalmic ointment bases must themselves be sterile: the usual base is white or yellow soft paraffin with liquid paraffin and wool fat, sterilised by dry heat and filtered while molten.`,`A dose of drug given as an ointment has a longer contact time than a drop but a slower onset, because the drug must first partition out of the greasy base.`,`Nanosuspension and prodrug approaches (for example dipivefrine as the prodrug of adrenaline) attack the permeability barrier rather than the residence time.`]}]},{topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Semisolids are preparations that hold their shape until a force is applied and then spread. Ointments, creams, gels and pastes are applied to skin or mucous membranes; suppositories and pessaries are moulded solids that melt or dissolve after insertion. In every case the base is chosen first, because the base decides how much drug is released and how the patient experiences the product.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The four ointment base classes`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Water content`,`Examples`,`Behaviour`],rows:[[`Oleaginous (hydrocarbon)`,`None; will not take water`,`White and yellow soft paraffin, hard paraffin, plastibase`,`Occlusive, emollient, greasy, hard to wash off`],[`Absorption`,`Anhydrous but takes up water`,`Wool fat (anhydrous lanolin), hydrophilic petrolatum, wool alcohols ointment`,`Becomes a water in oil system when water is added`],[`Absorption (already emulsified)`,`Contains water as W/O`,`Hydrous wool fat, cold cream, oily cream`,`Emollient, still not water washable`],[`Water-removable (washable)`,`Oil in water emulsion`,`Hydrophilic ointment, aqueous cream, vanishing cream`,`Non-greasy, washes off with water, can be diluted`],[`Water-soluble (greaseless)`,`No oil phase at all`,`Polyethylene glycol ointment (macrogol ointment)`,`Completely washable, dissolves in water, dehydrating`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`The four base classes`,text:`O-A-W-W: Oleaginous, Absorption, Water-removable, Water-soluble. The order also runs from greasiest to least greasy, and from least washable to most washable.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`How semisolids are made`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Fusion: the base components are melted together, the highest melting one first, the drug is stirred in and the mass is stirred until cold. Used whenever waxes, paraffins or high melting solids are present.`,`Trituration (incorporation): the drug is levigated with a small amount of liquid on an ointment slab with a spatula and worked into the base. Used for small quantities and heat labile drugs.`,`Levigating agent: mineral oil for an oleaginous base, glycerin or propylene glycol for a water-soluble base. The rule is to use a liquid miscible with the base.`,`Large scale equipment: planetary mixer, triple roller mill and colloid mill for particle size reduction and uniformity.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The other semisolid forms`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Form`,`Definition`,`Example`],rows:[[`Cream`,`A semisolid emulsion, O/W or W/O`,`Aqueous cream (O/W), cold cream (W/O)`],[`Gel`,`A semisolid in which a liquid is trapped in a polymer or particle network`,`Carbomer gel, bentonite magma`],[`Paste`,`A stiff ointment with 20 to 50 percent solids`,`Zinc oxide paste, Lassar paste`],[`Suppository`,`A moulded solid for the rectum`,`Glycerin suppository, bisacodyl suppository`],[`Pessary`,`A moulded solid for the vagina`,`Clotrimazole pessary`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why a paste is not an ointment`,text:`A paste carries a much higher proportion of insoluble powder, typically 20 to 50 percent. That makes it stiffer, less greasy, less occlusive and more absorptive of exudate, and it stays exactly where it is put. Pastes are therefore protective, used on weeping lesions and as a barrier around ulcers.`}]},{topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable heart of this topic is the suppository bases, the displacement value calculation, and the evaluation battery for both ointments and suppositories.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Suppository bases`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Base class`,`Examples`,`Melting or dissolving behaviour`],rows:[[`Fatty / oleaginous`,`Theobroma oil (cocoa butter), Witepsol, Massa Estarinum, Suppocire`,`Melts at body temperature, 30 to 36 degrees C`],[`Water soluble`,`Glycero-gelatin base, polyethylene glycol blends`,`Dissolves in the aqueous secretions rather than melting`],[`Emulsifying`,`Polyoxyl 40 stearate, Tween 61 blends`,`Melts and emulsifies with the surrounding fluid`]],caption:`Glycero-gelatin base of the IP is roughly glycerin 70 parts, gelatin 20 parts and water 10 parts; it is mainly used for pessaries.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Theobroma oil is polymorphic. The stable beta form melts at about 34.5 degrees C. If heated above about 36 degrees C it recrystallises as the unstable alpha form melting near 24 degrees C, and the suppositories will not set properly.`,`Theobroma oil needs a mould lubricant such as soft soap and glycerin; it also has a marked contraction on cooling.`,`PEG bases are unaffected by ambient temperature, so they need no refrigeration, but they are hygroscopic and dehydrating, so the suppository should be moistened before insertion.`,`PEG bases are incompatible with several drugs including benzocaine, aspirin and some phenolics, and with certain plastic and cellophane wrappers.`,`Semisynthetic hydrogenated vegetable oil bases (Witepsol) largely replaced cocoa butter: no polymorphism problem, no mould lubricant needed, and a sharp melting range.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Displacement value`},{kind:`para`,text:`The displacement value of a drug is the number of parts by weight of the drug that displaces one part by weight of the base. It exists because a suppository mould measures volume, not weight, and a drug denser than the base occupies less volume for the same weight.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Base required = (N x M) - (N x D / DV)`,caption:`N is the number of suppositories, M the mould calibration weight of pure base in g, D the weight of drug per suppository in g and DV the displacement value. The answer is grams of base to weigh out.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Substance`,`Displacement value in cocoa butter`],rows:[[`Zinc oxide`,`4.7`],[`Bismuth subgallate`,`2.7`],[`Boric acid`,`1.5`],[`Hamamelis dry extract`,`1.5`],[`Phenobarbitone`,`1.2`],[`Aspirin`,`1.1`],[`Ichthammol`,`1.0`],[`Castor oil and most oils`,`1.0`]],caption:`A displacement value of 1.0 means the substance has the same density as the base, so no correction is needed.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Worked displacement value numerical`,text:`Prepare 12 suppositories, each 1 g mould, containing 300 mg zinc oxide (DV 4.7). Total drug = 12 x 0.3 = 3.6 g. Base displaced by that drug = 3.6 / 4.7 = 0.766 g. Base required = (12 x 1) - 0.766 = 11.234 g. In practice you calculate for 14 or 15 units to allow for losses in the mould.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`DV = (b x x / 100) / [a - b + (b x x / 100)]`,caption:`Determining an unknown displacement value. a is the weight of six suppositories of pure base in g, b the weight of six suppositories containing x percent w/w of the drug, and x the percentage of drug. The numerator is the weight of drug in the six units.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Evaluation`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Test`,`What it measures`,`Typical requirement`],rows:[[`Melting range (macro melting)`,`Time to melt completely in a 37 degrees C bath`,`Fatty bases should melt within about 30 minutes`],[`Liquefaction time`,`Time to liquefy under conditions mimicking the rectum`,`Measured in the Krowczynski apparatus`],[`Breaking / crushing test`,`Mechanical strength during handling and packing`,`Erweka hardness tester; expressed as the mass needed to break`],[`Disintegration`,`Time to disperse or dissolve`,`Fat based within 30 minutes, water soluble within 60 minutes`],[`Weight variation`,`Uniformity of the moulded units`,`As per the pharmacopoeial uniformity of weight test`],[`Dissolution and content`,`Drug release and assay`,`Apparatus with a sinker or flow-through cell`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Evaluation of ointments and creams: appearance, pH, viscosity and rheology, spreadability, extrudability, drug content, globule size for creams, homogeneity, loss on drying, microbial limits and minimum fill.`,`In vitro drug release from a semisolid is measured in a Franz diffusion cell across a synthetic membrane; the amount released is plotted against the square root of time.`]}]},{topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions ask for the release kinetics of a semisolid, the physical chemistry of gels, and the specific composition of the official bases and creams.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Q = [D Cs (2A - Cs) t]^0.5`,caption:`Higuchi equation for release from an ointment in which the drug is suspended. Q is the amount released per unit area, D the diffusion coefficient in the base, Cs the drug solubility in the base, A the total drug concentration and t the time. A plot of Q against the square root of t is linear.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Release is diffusion controlled, so the square root of time plot is the diagnostic. Deviation from linearity signals base breakdown or drug depletion.`,`A drug releases fastest from the base in which it is least soluble, because the thermodynamic activity in the vehicle is highest.`,`Occlusive oleaginous bases hydrate the stratum corneum and raise percutaneous absorption, which is why petrolatum bases enhance corticosteroid activity.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Gels in detail`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Term`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Single phase gel`,`Macromolecules uniformly distributed, no boundary; carbomer, tragacanth, HPMC`],[`Two phase gel (magma)`,`Floccules of small distinct particles; bentonite magma, aluminium hydroxide gel`],[`Thixotropy`,`Isothermal, reversible sol-gel transformation on shaking and standing`],[`Syneresis`,`Spontaneous shrinking of the gel with exudation of the liquid phase`],[`Imbibition`,`Uptake of liquid by a gel without any increase in volume`],[`Swelling`,`Uptake of liquid accompanied by an increase in volume`],[`Xerogel`,`A gel from which the liquid has been removed, leaving the framework`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Carbomer needs neutralisation`,text:`Carbomer (Carbopol 934, 940, 941) is dispersed in water as an acidic, low viscosity slurry. It only gels when neutralised to about pH 6 to 7 with triethanolamine or sodium hydroxide, which ionises the carboxyl groups and makes the coiled polymer chains uncoil by charge repulsion. Excess electrolyte or a strongly cationic drug will collapse the gel again.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Official base compositions worth knowing`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Simple ointment: wool fat 5, hard paraffin 5, cetostearyl alcohol 5, white soft paraffin 85 parts.`,`Emulsifying ointment: emulsifying wax 30, white soft paraffin 50, liquid paraffin 20 parts; emulsifying wax is cetostearyl alcohol with sodium lauryl sulphate.`,`Aqueous cream: emulsifying ointment 30 parts with freshly boiled and cooled purified water to 100; an oil in water cream.`,`Cold cream: beeswax, borax, almond or mineral oil and water; the borax reacts with free cerotic acid in the beeswax to form the soap that emulsifies the system as water in oil.`,`Polyethylene glycol ointment: PEG 3350 40 parts and PEG 400 60 parts, fused together; the ratio is altered to change consistency.`,`Lassar paste (zinc and salicylic acid paste): zinc oxide 24, starch 24, salicylic acid 2 and white soft paraffin 50 parts.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Dilution of creams is not harmless`,text:`Diluting a preserved cream lowers the preservative below its effective concentration and can also break the emulsion by changing the phase volume ratio. Diluents must be specified by the manufacturer, prepared with boiled cooled water, and the diluted product given a short expiry, typically two to four weeks.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Required HLB = sum (fraction of each oil x its required HLB)`,caption:`Used when blending emulsifiers for a cream. Fractions are of the total oil phase. A blend of a high and a low HLB surfactant is then made to hit the required value: 3 to 6 gives water in oil, 8 to 18 gives oil in water.`}]},{topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Liquid orals are medicines swallowed as liquids. They act faster than tablets because there is no disintegration or dissolution step, and the dose can be adjusted freely, which makes them the standard for children and the elderly. Against that, they are bulkier, less stable, harder to mask for taste and easier to dose wrongly at home.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The main liquid oral forms`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Form`,`Definition`,`Key point`],rows:[[`Syrup`,`Concentrated aqueous solution of sucrose or a sugar substitute`,`IP simple syrup is 66.7 percent w/w sucrose`],[`Elixir`,`Clear, sweetened hydroalcoholic solution`,`Alcohol content usually 5 to 40 percent`],[`Linctus`,`Viscous sweet liquid sipped slowly for cough`,`High syrup content is the active demulcent principle`],[`Mixture`,`Liquid containing dissolved or suspended drugs`,`Usually extemporaneous, shake before use if a suspension`],[`Suspension`,`Solid particles dispersed in a liquid`,`Must be redispersible and must not cake`],[`Emulsion`,`One liquid dispersed as droplets in another`,`Needs an emulsifying agent to stay together`],[`Aromatic water`,`Saturated aqueous solution of a volatile oil`,`Used as a flavoured vehicle`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why a saturated syrup needs no preservative`,text:`At about 65 percent w/w sucrose the water in the syrup is so heavily bound as sucrose hydrate that the water activity is too low for microbial growth. A syrup that is diluted, or one where some sucrose has been replaced, loses that self-preserving property and must have a preservative added.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`What goes into a liquid oral`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Vehicle: purified water, syrup, alcohol, glycerin, propylene glycol or a mixture.`,`Sweetener: sucrose, sorbitol, glycerin, saccharin sodium, aspartame, sucralose.`,`Preservative: benzoic acid or sodium benzoate 0.1 to 0.2 percent, methyl and propyl parabens, potassium sorbate.`,`Flavour and colour, matched to each other and to the expected patient.`,`Buffer and antioxidant where the drug needs them.`,`Viscosity builder in suspensions, emulsifier in emulsions.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Elixir versus syrup versus linctus`,text:`All three are sweet liquids, but an elixir always contains alcohol and is always a clear solution, a syrup is aqueous and sugar-based, and a linctus is defined by its viscosity and its purpose, which is to coat the throat. A sugar free elixir still contains alcohol; a sugar free syrup does not.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Sorbitol and saccharin sodium are used for diabetic patients because they are not metabolised to glucose.`,`Aspartame must not be given to patients with phenylketonuria and is not heat stable, so it cannot be used in a hot syrup process.`,`Alcohol-containing elixirs are avoided in children and in patients taking metronidazole or disulfiram.`]}]},{topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The scoring content here is syrup manufacture, the additives for suspensions with the flocculated versus deflocculated comparison, and the emulsion section with emulsifier classes, preparation methods and the identification tests.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Methods of preparing syrups`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`How`,`When used`],rows:[[`Hot process (with heat)`,`Sucrose dissolved in heated water, then cooled`,`Fastest; not for volatile or heat labile ingredients`],[`Cold process (agitation)`,`Sucrose dissolved by stirring at room temperature`,`For volatile oils and thermolabile drugs; slower`],[`Percolation`,`Water percolated slowly through a bed of sucrose`,`Gives a clear syrup without heat; used for ginger syrup`],[`Addition of a medicating liquid`,`A tincture or fluid extract is added to simple syrup`,`When the drug is already in a liquid extract`]],caption:`Excessive heat inverts sucrose to glucose and fructose, which darkens the syrup and makes it more prone to fermentation.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Suspension additives`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Additive class`,`Purpose`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Wetting agent`,`Displaces air from hydrophobic particle surfaces`,`Polysorbates, sodium lauryl sulphate, alcohol, glycerin, propylene glycol`],[`Suspending agent`,`Raises viscosity and slows settling`,`Acacia, tragacanth, sodium alginate, xanthan gum, methylcellulose, sodium CMC`],[`Clay thickener`,`Builds a thixotropic structured vehicle`,`Bentonite, veegum, magnesium aluminium silicate`],[`Flocculating agent`,`Forms loose flocs that resist caking`,`Electrolytes, ionic surfactants, polymers`],[`Preservative`,`Prevents microbial growth in the aqueous phase`,`Parabens, benzoic acid, sorbic acid`],[`Buffer / antioxidant`,`Chemical stability of the dissolved fraction`,`Citrate, phosphate, sodium metabisulphite`]]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Property`,`Flocculated suspension`,`Deflocculated suspension`],rows:[[`Particle state`,`Loose aggregates (flocs)`,`Separate individual particles`],[`Rate of sedimentation`,`Fast, the flocs settle together`,`Slow, particles settle individually`],[`Sediment volume`,`High and loose`,`Low and closely packed`],[`Cake formation`,`No hard cake`,`Hard cake that will not redisperse`],[`Redispersibility`,`Easy, on gentle shaking`,`Very difficult or impossible`],[`Supernatant`,`Clear`,`Remains cloudy for a long time`],[`Pharmaceutical elegance`,`Poor, an obvious sediment line`,`Good until it cakes`]],caption:`The ideal product is a controlled flocculation in a structured vehicle: flocculated so it never cakes, thickened so the sediment line takes a long time to appear.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`v = d^2 (rho(s) - rho(l)) g / (18 x eta)`,caption:`Stokes law. v is the sedimentation velocity in cm/s, d the particle diameter in cm, rho(s) and rho(l) the densities of solid and liquid in g/cm3, g the acceleration due to gravity and eta the viscosity of the medium in poise.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`F = Vu / Vo     and     beta = F / F(infinity)`,caption:`F is the sedimentation volume, the ratio of the final sediment volume Vu to the original suspension volume Vo. Beta is the degree of flocculation, comparing F with the sedimentation volume of the deflocculated suspension. F equal to 1 is ideal.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Emulsions`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Emulsifier class`,`Examples`,`Note`],rows:[[`Natural polysaccharide`,`Acacia, tragacanth, agar, pectin`,`Acacia is the classic for oral emulsions`],[`Natural from animals`,`Gelatin, lecithin, cholesterol`,`Lecithin also stabilises intravenous fat emulsions`],[`Anionic surfactant`,`Sodium lauryl sulphate, soaps of alkali metals`,`Gives oil in water; external use mostly`],[`Cationic surfactant`,`Benzalkonium chloride, cetrimide`,`External use; incompatible with anionics`],[`Non-ionic surfactant`,`Spans (sorbitan esters), Tweens (polysorbates)`,`Least toxic and least reactive; used orally`],[`Finely divided solid`,`Bentonite, magnesium hydroxide, aluminium hydroxide`,`Pickering emulsions; the solid sits at the interface`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Dry gum (continental) method: the 4:2:1 ratio of oil, water and gum is triturated to a primary emulsion which cracks with a sharp clicking sound when ready, then diluted.`,`Wet gum (English) method: the gum is first dispersed in the water, then the oil is added slowly. Slower but more forgiving.`,`Bottle (Forbes) method: for volatile and low viscosity oils; oil and gum are shaken in a dry bottle before water is added.`,`HLB guide: 3 to 6 gives water in oil emulsifiers, 7 to 9 wetting agents, 8 to 18 oil in water emulsifiers, 13 to 15 detergents, 15 to 18 solubilisers.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Identification test`,`Oil in water result`,`Water in oil result`],rows:[[`Dilution with water`,`Dilutes freely`,`Does not dilute`],[`Dye solubility with amaranth (water soluble)`,`Continuous phase is coloured`,`Only droplets are coloured`],[`Dye solubility with Sudan III (oil soluble)`,`Only droplets are coloured`,`Continuous phase is coloured`],[`Electrical conductivity`,`Conducts`,`Does not conduct`],[`Filter paper test`,`Spreads rapidly with a wet ring`,`Does not spread`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Creaming is not cracking`,text:`Creaming is the upward or downward movement of droplets under gravity, and it is reversible by shaking. Cracking, also called breaking, is coalescence of the droplets into a separate continuous layer, and shaking will not restore it. Phase inversion is a third failure, where an oil in water emulsion turns into water in oil, usually because the emulsifier type changed or the phase volume exceeded about 74 percent.`}]},{topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced level is about the physical chemistry that determines whether a disperse system survives: zeta potential, DLVO theory, and the numerical treatment of sedimentation and dilution.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Zeta potential and flocculation`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Zeta potential is the electrical potential at the plane of shear around a dispersed particle, measured in millivolts.`,`A high zeta potential, positive or negative, means strong repulsion and a deflocculated system, which will eventually cake.`,`Reducing the magnitude of the zeta potential to roughly plus or minus 20 to 25 mV allows the particles to approach into the secondary minimum and floc loosely.`,`Reducing it to zero, the point of zero charge, causes irreversible aggregation in the primary minimum, which is caking.`,`Controlled flocculation therefore means adjusting zeta potential deliberately, usually with an electrolyte such as potassium dihydrogen phosphate or aluminium chloride.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`DLVO in one line`,text:`The total interaction energy is the sum of electrical repulsion and van der Waals attraction. The primary minimum at very short distance is deep and irreversible (caking); the secondary minimum at larger distance is shallow and reversible (flocculation). Good suspension design keeps particles in the secondary minimum.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Schulze-Hardy: flocculating power increases sharply with counter-ion valency`,caption:`A trivalent counter-ion such as aluminium is far more effective at flocculating a negatively charged sol than a divalent or monovalent ion at the same concentration. The critical coagulation concentration falls steeply as valency rises.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Worked numericals`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Sedimentation volume calculation`,text:`A 100 mL suspension leaves a sediment of 30 mL after standing. F = Vu/Vo = 30/100 = 0.3. If the deflocculated control gives 15 mL, then F(infinity) = 0.15 and the degree of flocculation beta = 0.3/0.15 = 2, meaning the sediment volume has doubled through flocculation.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Alcohol strength in an elixir`,text:`To make 500 mL of an elixir containing 20 percent v/v alcohol from 90 percent v/v alcohol: alcohol needed = 500 x 20/100 = 100 mL of absolute equivalent; volume of 90 percent alcohol = 100 x 100/90 = 111.1 mL. The rest of the vehicle makes up to 500 mL.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Stability and evaluation`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Parameter`,`Method`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Sedimentation volume F`,`Measure sediment height over time`,`Closer to 1 is better`],[`Redispersibility`,`Number of inversions to resuspend`,`Fewer than about 30 inversions is acceptable`],[`Rheology`,`Brookfield viscometer, flow curve`,`Pseudoplastic with thixotropy is ideal`],[`Globule size`,`Microscopy, laser diffraction, Coulter counter`,`Growth over time predicts coalescence`],[`Zeta potential`,`Microelectrophoresis`,`Predicts flocculation state`],[`Freeze-thaw cycling`,`Repeated cycles between low and high temperature`,`Accelerated test for physical instability`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Why a suspension should be pseudoplastic and thixotropic, not Newtonian`,text:`On the shelf you want a high apparent viscosity so particles do not settle. When the bottle is shaken and the dose is poured you want low viscosity. Only a shear thinning system gives both, and thixotropy adds the useful delay in structure recovery that keeps the product pourable while it is being measured.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Ostwald ripening in emulsions: small droplets dissolve and redeposit onto large ones, so the mean droplet size creeps upward even without coalescence.`,`Phase volume above about 74 percent forces either inversion or a change to a non-spherical packed structure, since 74 percent is the closest packing of uniform spheres.`,`A structured vehicle uses a polymer network that yields above a critical shear stress, giving a genuine yield value rather than mere high viscosity.`]}]},{topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A pharmaceutical aerosol is a pressurised dosage form in which the drug is dissolved, suspended or emulsified in a propellant, and is expelled as a fine spray, foam or semisolid when a valve is opened. Inhalation aerosols deliver the drug straight to the lung, which means a small dose, a fast onset and few systemic effects.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The five parts of an aerosol`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Container: holds the pressurised contents. Aluminium, tinplate, stainless steel or coated glass.`,`Propellant: supplies the pressure that expels the product and helps break it into droplets.`,`Concentrate: the drug with any cosolvent, surfactant or suspending aid.`,`Valve: controls how much comes out and in what form; a metering valve for inhalers.`,`Actuator: the button the patient presses, which contains the orifice that shapes the spray.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`System`,`Composition`,`Output`],rows:[[`Two phase (solution)`,`Drug in liquefied propellant plus its vapour`,`Fine spray or space spray`],[`Three phase (suspension)`,`Solid drug, liquid propellant and vapour`,`Powder aerosol; needs shaking`],[`Three phase (emulsion)`,`Aqueous phase, propellant and vapour`,`Foam or wet spray`],[`Compressed gas`,`Gas above the product, no liquefied propellant`,`Wet stream or foam; pressure falls with use`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Particle size decides where the drug lands`,text:`Particles of 1 to 5 micrometres aerodynamic diameter deposit in the bronchi and alveoli, which is the therapeutic target. Above about 10 micrometres they impact in the mouth and throat and are swallowed. Below about 0.5 micrometres they stay suspended and are simply breathed out again.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Advantages: rapid onset, dose delivered directly to the site, drug protected from air and moisture, no contamination of remaining contents, no first pass metabolism.`,`Disadvantages: expensive, pressurised container hazard, patient technique problems with metered dose inhalers, and propellant environmental issues.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Aerosol components`,text:`C-P-C-V-A: Container, Propellant, Concentrate, Valve, Actuator. Five components, in the order you would build the can.`}]},{topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core is the propellants with their numbering, the reason CFCs were replaced by HFAs, the container and valve types, and the comparison of the three inhaler devices.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Propellants`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Propellant`,`Chemical name`,`Boiling point / note`],rows:[[`Propellant 11`,`Trichloromonofluoromethane`,`Boils at 23.8 C; a liquid at room temperature, used as a cosolvent`],[`Propellant 12`,`Dichlorodifluoromethane`,`Boils at minus 29.8 C; the main CFC propellant`],[`Propellant 114`,`Dichlorotetrafluoroethane`,`Boils at 3.6 C; blended with 12 to lower the pressure`],[`HFA-134a`,`1,1,1,2-tetrafluoroethane (norflurane)`,`The main CFC replacement for MDIs`],[`HFA-227ea`,`1,1,1,2,3,3,3-heptafluoropropane (apaflurane)`,`Second HFA, higher density, blended with 134a`],[`Hydrocarbons`,`Propane, butane, isobutane`,`Cheap and non-ozone depleting but flammable; topical use only`],[`Compressed gases`,`Nitrogen, carbon dioxide, nitrous oxide`,`Inert; nitrogen gives a dry spray, CO2 and N2O give foams`]],caption:`In the numbering system the first digit plus 1 is the number of carbon atoms, the second digit minus 1 is the hydrogen atoms and the last digit is the fluorine atoms; the remainder of the valency is chlorine.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Chlorofluorocarbons deplete stratospheric ozone because the chlorine released by ultraviolet photolysis catalyses ozone destruction. Under the Montreal Protocol of 1987 they were phased out, and metered dose inhalers were reformulated with hydrofluoroalkanes, which contain no chlorine. HFAs have zero ozone depletion potential but are still greenhouse gases.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`What changed when MDIs moved to HFA`,text:`HFAs are poor solvents for the traditional sorbitan and lecithin surfactants, so ethanol had to be added as a cosolvent and new surfactants or solution formulations were developed. The plume is warmer and softer, the taste is different, and some solution HFA products give a much finer particle size and therefore higher lung deposition at the same nominal dose. Doses are not interchangeable between CFC and HFA products.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Containers and valves`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Container material`,`Strength / pressure`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Aluminium (monobloc)`,`High`,`Seamless, light, corrosion resistant; the standard for MDIs, often internally lacquered`],[`Tinplated steel`,`High`,`Cheap, three-piece with side seam; risk of corrosion with aqueous products`],[`Stainless steel`,`Very high`,`Expensive; for small volume high pressure products`],[`Glass, uncoated`,`Low, below about 25 psig`,`Chemically inert but fragile`],[`Glass, plastic coated`,`Low to moderate`,`Coating contains the fragments if the container breaks`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Continuous spray valve: gives output as long as the actuator is held down. Used for topical sprays and foams.`,`Metering valve: delivers a fixed volume, typically 50 to 150 microlitres, each time it is actuated. Essential for inhalation products.`,`Valve parts: actuator, stem, gasket, spring, mounting cup (ferrule), valve housing and dip tube.`,`Tilt valve and foam (whipped cream) valves are variants for topical and foam products.`,`Filling: cold filling chills the concentrate and propellant to about minus 30 to minus 40 degrees C and pours them in; pressure filling adds the propellant through the fitted valve. Pressure filling is preferred for pharmaceuticals because there is no moisture condensation and no need for refrigeration.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`MDI versus DPI versus nebuliser`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Metered dose inhaler`,`Dry powder inhaler`,`Nebuliser`],rows:[[`Driving force`,`Propellant pressure`,`The patient inspiratory flow`,`Compressed air or ultrasound`],[`Coordination needed`,`Yes, actuation with inhalation`,`No, it is breath actuated`,`No, normal tidal breathing`],[`Inspiratory effort`,`Low, slow deep breath`,`High, 30 to 60 L/min needed`,`Minimal`],[`Typical lung deposition`,`10 to 20 percent, better with a spacer`,`15 to 30 percent`,`5 to 15 percent but a large dose is given`],[`Propellant`,`HFA required`,`None`,`None`],[`Moisture sensitivity`,`Low`,`High; humidity causes powder aggregation`,`Not applicable`],[`Time to give a dose`,`Seconds`,`Seconds`,`10 to 20 minutes`],[`Best for`,`Most ambulant adults`,`Adults and older children with adequate flow`,`Infants, the elderly, acute severe attacks`],[`Examples`,`Salbutamol HFA inhaler`,`Rotahaler, Turbuhaler, Diskus`,`Jet, ultrasonic and vibrating mesh nebulisers`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Spacers solve coordination, not everything`,text:`A spacer removes the need to synchronise actuation with inhalation and traps large particles that would otherwise hit the throat, which cuts oral candidiasis with inhaled steroids. It does not help a dry powder inhaler, which needs the patient inspiratory flow to disperse the powder in the first place.`}]},{topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced aerosol content is the physics of deposition, the vapour pressure arithmetic used to blend propellants, and the full evaluation battery.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mechanisms of pulmonary deposition`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Mechanism`,`Particle size where it dominates`,`Region affected`],rows:[[`Inertial impaction`,`Above about 5 micrometres`,`Oropharynx and large conducting airways`],[`Gravitational sedimentation`,`About 1 to 5 micrometres`,`Small airways and alveoli; helped by breath holding`],[`Brownian diffusion`,`Below about 0.5 micrometres`,`Alveoli; most such particles are exhaled again`],[`Interception`,`Elongated or fibrous particles`,`Airway bifurcations`]],caption:`Mass median aerodynamic diameter (MMAD) with the geometric standard deviation is the standard way of reporting an inhalation aerosol size distribution.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`da = dg x (rho / rho0 x 1/chi)^0.5`,caption:`Aerodynamic diameter da relates to the geometric diameter dg through the particle density rho (relative to unit density rho0 of 1 g/cm3) and the dynamic shape factor chi. A porous low density particle can be geometrically large yet aerodynamically small.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Pa = Pa(pure) x mole fraction of A     (Raoult law)`,caption:`The partial vapour pressure of a propellant in a blend equals its pure vapour pressure times its mole fraction. The total pressure in the can is the sum of the partial pressures, which is how a blend of propellant 12 and 114 is designed to a target pressure.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why compressed gas systems behave differently`,text:`A liquefied propellant maintains constant pressure throughout the life of the can, because vapour is replenished from the liquid reservoir as it is used. A compressed gas system has no reservoir, so pressure falls steadily as the container empties, the spray becomes coarser and wetter, and the last portion may not expel at all.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Evaluation of aerosols`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Test group`,`Tests`],rows:[[`Flammability`,`Flame extension test, flash point by Tag open cup`],[`Physicochemical`,`Vapour pressure by gauge, density by pycnometer or hydrometer, moisture by Karl Fischer, identity of propellant by gas chromatography or IR`],[`Performance`,`Valve discharge rate, spray pattern, delivered dose uniformity, net contents, leakage rate`],[`Particle size`,`Cascade impactor (Andersen), twin impinger, laser diffraction`],[`Foam products`,`Foam density, stability, bubble size, completeness of discharge`],[`Biological`,`Therapeutic activity, inhalation toxicity, skin irritation for topicals`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Delivered dose uniformity is checked at the beginning, middle and end of unit life, since a suspension can stratify.`,`Fine particle fraction (also called respirable fraction) is the proportion of the delivered dose below 5 micrometres, measured on a cascade impactor. It is the single most predictive in vitro measure of clinical performance.`,`Leakage rate is calculated from weight loss over a defined storage period and extrapolated to a yearly figure.`,`Dry powder inhalers add tests for the carrier, usually alpha-lactose monohydrate, and for flow rate dependence of the emitted dose.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Carrier lactose is not inert bulk`,text:`In a dry powder inhaler the micronised drug is blended with coarse lactose so that the mixture flows and can be metered. The drug must then detach from the carrier during inhalation. Lactose surface roughness, fines content and the drug to carrier adhesion force therefore control the fine particle fraction directly, so a change of lactose grade is a formulation change, not an excipient substitution.`}]},{topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Packaging is everything that surrounds the product between the factory and the patient. It has to contain the medicine, protect it from moisture, oxygen, light, microorganisms and physical damage, present the information required by law, and be convenient enough that the patient actually uses the product correctly.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Level`,`Definition`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Primary`,`In direct contact with the product`,`Ampoule, vial, blister, strip, bottle, collapsible tube`],[`Secondary`,`Encloses the primary pack`,`Carton, leaflet, outer box`],[`Tertiary`,`For bulk handling and transport`,`Shipper carton, shrink wrap, pallet`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Well-closed container: protects from extraneous solids and loss of contents in ordinary handling.`,`Tight container: protects from contamination by liquids, solids and vapours, and from efflorescence, deliquescence and evaporation.`,`Hermetically sealed container: impervious to air and any other gas under ordinary handling; a sealed glass ampoule is the model.`,`Light-resistant container: protects the contents from the effect of light, usually amber glass or an opaque outer.`,`Single dose container holds one dose only and cannot be resealed; multi-dose containers must be preserved.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The materials used`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Material`,`Main advantage`,`Main drawback`],rows:[[`Glass`,`Inert, transparent, impermeable, can be sterilised`,`Heavy, breakable, alkali can leach into the contents`],[`Plastic`,`Light, unbreakable, mouldable, cheap`,`Permeable, can leach additives or sorb the drug`],[`Metal`,`Strong, opaque, impermeable`,`Costly, may corrode or react, needs internal lacquer`],[`Rubber`,`Resealable and elastic`,`Extractables, sorption of preservative, fragmentation on puncture`],[`Paper and board`,`Cheap, printable, recyclable`,`No barrier properties on its own`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why glass is graded at all`,text:`Ordinary soda-lime glass releases sodium ions into an aqueous product, raising the pH and possibly degrading the drug. Boric oxide in borosilicate glass ties up that alkali in the network, which is why Type I is the only glass permitted for alkaline and highly sensitive injections.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`The functions of packaging`,text:`C-P-P-C: Contain, Protect, Present, Convenience. Anything a package does falls under one of those four.`}]},{topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Exam questions here are precise: the four glass types with composition, use and test; the named plastics with their applications; the rubber elastomers; and container closure integrity testing.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Glass types`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Composition`,`Hydrolytic resistance`,`Use`,`Test applied`],rows:[[`I`,`Borosilicate; boric oxide replaces part of the alkali`,`Highest`,`All parenterals, especially alkaline and sensitive products`,`Powdered glass test`],[`II`,`Soda-lime, surface dealkalised with sulphur dioxide`,`High, but only at the surface`,`Acidic and neutral aqueous parenterals; single use`,`Water attack (surface) test`],[`III`,`Regular soda-lime silica, untreated`,`Moderate`,`Non-aqueous parenterals, powders for injection`,`Powdered glass test`],[`NP (IV)`,`General purpose soda-lime`,`Low`,`Oral and topical products only; never injections`,`Powdered glass test`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Powdered glass test: glass is crushed and sieved, 10 g of grains are autoclaved with water at 121 degrees C for 30 minutes and the extracted alkali is titrated with 0.02 N sulphuric acid. Limits: Type I not more than 1.0 mL, Type III not more than 8.5 mL, Type NP not more than 15.0 mL.`,`Water attack test: intact containers, not powder, are autoclaved the same way, because it is only the treated inner surface of Type II that resists attack. Limits: containers of 100 mL or less, not more than 0.7 mL of 0.02 N acid; containers above 100 mL, not more than 0.2 mL.`,`Type II must never be used for alkaline products, because the alkali strips off the dealkalised surface layer and exposes ordinary soda-lime glass underneath.`,`Amber glass owes its colour to iron oxide with carbon or manganese dioxide, and it screens light below about 470 nm.`,`Glass composition in general: silica about 70 percent, sodium oxide, calcium oxide, with alumina for durability, boric oxide for neutral glass, potassium and barium oxides for specific properties.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Plastics in packaging`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Polymer`,`Properties`,`Typical use`],rows:[[`LDPE`,`Flexible, translucent, poor gas barrier`,`Squeeze bottles, eye drop droppers, LVP bags, liners`],[`HDPE`,`Rigid, opaque, good moisture barrier`,`Tablet and capsule bottles, closures`],[`Polypropylene`,`High melting, autoclavable at 121 C, good chemical resistance`,`Syringes, IV bottles, hinged caps, closures`],[`PVC (rigid)`,`Clear, thermoformable, moderate moisture barrier`,`Blister base web`],[`PVC (plasticised)`,`Soft and flexible`,`IV bags and tubing; DEHP plasticiser leaching is a concern`],[`PVDC / Aclar`,`Outstanding moisture and gas barrier`,`Coating or laminate on blister film for hygroscopic drugs`],[`PET`,`Clear, strong, good barrier, glass-like`,`Oral liquid bottles, jars`],[`Polystyrene`,`Clear, rigid, brittle, poor barrier`,`Ointment jars, disposable items; not for oxygen sensitive drugs`],[`Polycarbonate`,`Very strong, clear, autoclavable`,`Syringes, reusable devices`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`The five problems of plastic containers`,text:`Permeation of gases and moisture through the wall; leaching of plasticisers, stabilisers and antioxidants into the product; sorption of drug or preservative onto the plastic; chemical reaction between product and polymer; and alteration of the physical properties of the container itself, such as panelling and stress cracking. Sorption of preservative is why a preservative efficacy test must be repeated in the final container.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Rubber closures`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Elastomer`,`Character`,`Use`],rows:[[`Natural rubber`,`Elastic and cheap, but high extractables and latex allergy`,`Largely replaced in pharmaceuticals`],[`Butyl and halobutyl`,`Lowest gas and moisture permeability`,`Vial stoppers, especially for lyophilised products`],[`Nitrile`,`Good oil and solvent resistance`,`Closures for oily injections`],[`Silicone`,`Excellent heat resistance and inertness, but high permeability and cost`,`Specialised closures, often as a coating`],[`Neoprene / polychloroprene`,`Good general resistance`,`Plungers and stoppers`]],caption:`A rubber formulation contains the elastomer plus a vulcanising agent (sulphur), an accelerator, an activator (zinc oxide), fillers such as carbon black or clay, a softener, an antioxidant and pigments. Every one of these can be an extractable.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Fluoropolymer or PTFE laminated stoppers put a barrier film on the product-contact face, which almost eliminates leaching and sorption.`,`Tests on rubber closures include fragmentation, self-sealability, penetrability, sterility, and chemical tests on the extract for pH change, reducing substances, heavy metals, zinc, ammonium, volatile sulphides, UV absorbance and residue on evaporation.`,`Fragmentation test: a needle is passed through the closure a set number of times and the fragments in the liquid are counted; the limit is a small stated number of fragments across the closures tested.`,`Siliconisation of stoppers improves machinability but excess silicone can appear as particulate matter in the product.`]}]},{topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced packaging is about proving performance rather than describing materials: leak testing by deterministic methods, moisture permeation classification, strip versus blister, and extractables and leachables.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Container closure integrity testing`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Approach`,`Method`,`Character`],rows:[[`Deterministic`,`Helium mass spectrometry leak detection`,`Most sensitive; quantitative in standard leak rate units`],[`Deterministic`,`Vacuum decay`,`Non-destructive, widely used for filled units`],[`Deterministic`,`Pressure decay`,`For rigid containers and devices`],[`Deterministic`,`High voltage leak detection (HVLD)`,`For conductive liquids in glass or plastic; non-destructive`],[`Deterministic`,`Laser headspace analysis (frequency modulated spectroscopy)`,`Measures headspace oxygen, moisture or pressure through the wall`],[`Probabilistic`,`Microbial ingress / immersion challenge`,`Traditional but variable and slow`],[`Probabilistic`,`Dye (methylene blue) ingress`,`Cheap, destructive, low and variable sensitivity`],[`Probabilistic`,`Bubble emission test`,`Qualitative and operator dependent`]],caption:`Current pharmacopoeial guidance favours deterministic methods and treats microbial ingress as the reference only for defining what leak size matters.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Maximum allowable leakage limit`,text:`Microbial ingress becomes a real risk at a defect size of roughly 0.2 to 0.3 micrometres and above, which is why a sterile product package is normally required to have no leak path larger than that. A test method must be shown capable of detecting a defect at or below the maximum allowable leakage limit set for the product.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Moisture permeation and light transmission`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Moisture permeation test: containers are filled with a desiccant, sealed, stored at defined temperature and humidity and weighed periodically. Weight gain per day per litre of container volume classifies the container as tight or well-closed.`,`Light transmission test: the wall of a light-resistant container must transmit no more than the stated small percentage of incident light between 290 and 450 nm.`,`Other plastic container tests: leakage, collapsibility for collapsible tubes, clarity of aqueous extract, non-volatile residue, residue on ignition, heavy metals and buffering capacity.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Strip versus blister packing`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Strip pack`,`Blister pack`],rows:[[`Construction`,`Two flat webs sealed around each unit`,`A thermoformed or cold-formed cavity closed with a lidding foil`],[`Cavity`,`No preformed pocket`,`Preformed pocket holds the unit`],[`Typical materials`,`Aluminium or paper-poly-foil laminate on both sides`,`PVC, PVC/PVDC or Alu-Alu base with aluminium lid`],[`Barrier`,`Excellent when foil-foil`,`Depends on base web; cold-formed Alu-Alu is best`],[`Visibility of product`,`None`,`Yes, unless cold-formed aluminium is used`],[`Suits`,`Moisture sensitive and irregular units`,`Most solid dosage forms, good for unit dose compliance packs`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Cold-formed Alu-Alu blisters trade visibility for barrier`,text:`Cold forming stretches an aluminium laminate into a cavity, giving an almost perfect barrier to moisture, oxygen and light. The price is that the pack is opaque, the cavities are larger and shallower so fewer fit on a card, and the process is slower than thermoforming PVC.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Extractables are the species that come out of a packaging material under exaggerated laboratory conditions; leachables are the subset that actually migrate into the product under normal storage. Every leachable is a potential extractable, but not the reverse.`,`Aluminium collapsible tubes are internally lacquered with epoxy or phenolic resin to stop reaction with the product, and are often fitted with a membrane seal.`,`Closure liners (wads) of pulp, foam, PVC or foil complete the seal on a bottle; induction sealing with an aluminium-faced liner gives both a hermetic seal and tamper evidence.`,`Child resistant closures use push-and-turn or squeeze-and-turn actions; these must be balanced against senior friendliness, since arthritic patients may be unable to open them.`]}]}],t=[{id:`q-pt-preformulation-1`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Pseudopolymorphism refers to`,options:[`Two crystal forms of the same compound with the same composition`,`A completely amorphous solid with no lattice order`,`Solvates and hydrates, where solvent is part of the crystal lattice`,`Two different crystal habits with the same internal lattice`],answer:2,explanation:`Pseudopolymorphs are solvates, of which hydrates are the commonest. Solvent molecules occupy defined positions in the lattice, so the composition differs and they are not true polymorphs. Two crystal forms of identical composition is the definition of true polymorphism, and crystal habit refers only to external shape with an unchanged internal lattice.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-preformulation-2`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The n-octanol/water partition coefficient of a drug is a measure of its`,options:[`Degree of ionisation`,`Lipophilicity`,`Aqueous solubility at saturation`,`Rate of hydrolysis`],answer:1,explanation:`P is the ratio of the unionised drug concentration in octanol to that in water, so it measures how the molecule favours a lipid environment, that is its lipophilicity. Degree of ionisation depends on pKa and pH and is dealt with by the Henderson-Hasselbalch relationship, not by P.`},{id:`q-pt-preformulation-3`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug shows low aqueous solubility but high intestinal permeability. Which BCS class does it belong to, and what limits its absorption?`,options:[`Class I, limited by gastric emptying`,`Class III, limited by permeation`,`Class IV, limited by both`,`Class II, limited by dissolution rate`],answer:3,explanation:`Low solubility with high permeability is Class II, and dissolution is the rate limiting step; these drugs benefit most from micronisation, salt formation and solid dispersions and give the best in vitro to in vivo correlation. Class III is the mirror image, high solubility and low permeability, where improving dissolution achieves nothing.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-preformulation-4`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Chloramphenicol palmitate is the standard textbook example used to illustrate`,options:[`The therapeutic consequence of polymorphism`,`The effect of particle size on dissolution`,`The effect of pH on drug stability`,`Drug-excipient incompatibility detected by DSC`],answer:0,explanation:`Only the metastable form B of chloramphenicol palmitate dissolves fast enough for gut esterases to liberate active chloramphenicol; the stable form A is essentially inactive. This is the classic demonstration that a polymorphic change can abolish clinical efficacy. Particle size effects are usually illustrated with griseofulvin.`},{id:`q-pt-preformulation-5`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Under the BCS, a drug substance is classified as highly soluble when the highest dose strength dissolves in`,options:[`900 mL of water at 37 degrees C`,`250 mL or less of aqueous media across the physiological pH range`,`100 mL of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid only`,`1000 mL of pH 6.8 phosphate buffer`],answer:1,explanation:`The solubility criterion is 250 mL or less of aqueous media over the physiological pH range, 250 mL being taken as the volume of a glass of water with which a dose is swallowed. The 900 mL figure is the usual dissolution vessel volume and is a different concept entirely.`},{id:`q-pt-preformulation-6`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In a drug-excipient compatibility study by DSC, the drug melting endotherm disappears in the 1:1 mixture with an excipient. The correct interpretation is that`,options:[`A chemical incompatibility is proven and the excipient must be rejected`,`The excipient is inert because no new peak appeared`,`An interaction is suspected and must be confirmed by isothermal stress testing with a stability-indicating assay`,`The drug has changed to a more stable polymorph`],answer:2,explanation:`Loss of the melting endotherm can be caused by simple eutectic formation or by the drug dissolving in a molten excipient, neither of which is a chemical reaction. DSC is a rapid screen only; confirmation requires stressing the binary mixture at elevated temperature and humidity and assaying by a stability-indicating chromatographic method.`,tag:`Trap`},{id:`q-pt-preformulation-7`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Two water-soluble salts have critical relative humidity values of 75 percent and 60 percent. Using the Elder equation, the CRH of their mixture is approximately`,options:[`67.5 percent`,`60 percent`,`45 percent`,`81 percent`],answer:2,explanation:`CRH of the mixture = 75 x 60 / 100 = 45 percent. The result is always lower than either component, which is why a blend picks up moisture at a humidity that is safe for the individual powders. The tempting distractor 67.5 percent is the arithmetic mean, which the Elder equation does not use.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-preformulation-8`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A new solid form gives a DSC endotherm at 95 degrees C accompanied by a corresponding step in the TGA trace. The most likely explanation is that the material is`,options:[`An enantiotropic polymorph undergoing a solid-solid transition`,`An amorphous solid passing through its glass transition`,`A eutectic mixture melting`,`A hydrate losing its water of crystallisation`],answer:3,explanation:`A mass loss detected by TGA at the same temperature as the endotherm means a volatile component is leaving, which for a pharmaceutical solid almost always means water of crystallisation, so the sample is a hydrate. A polymorphic transition gives the endotherm with no mass change at all, and a glass transition appears as a baseline step rather than a peak.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-1`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The function of a glidant in a tablet formulation is to`,options:[`Improve the flow of the powder or granules from the hopper into the die`,`Reduce friction between the tablet and the die wall during ejection`,`Prevent the material sticking to the punch faces`,`Cause the tablet to break up in gastrointestinal fluid`],answer:0,explanation:`A glidant such as colloidal silicon dioxide reduces interparticulate friction and so improves flow. Reducing die wall friction is the definition of a lubricant, and preventing adhesion to the punch face is that of an antiadherent; talc and magnesium stearate can act in more than one role, which is exactly why the definitions rather than the substances must be learnt.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-2`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following is a superdisintegrant?`,options:[`Maize starch`,`Povidone`,`Dicalcium phosphate`,`Croscarmellose sodium`],answer:3,explanation:`Croscarmellose sodium is a crosslinked cellulose superdisintegrant effective at 0.5 to 5 percent, compared with 5 to 15 percent for plain maize starch. Povidone is a binder and dicalcium phosphate a diluent; the crosslinked form of povidone, crospovidone, is the superdisintegrant, not povidone itself.`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-3`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Separation of a tablet into two or more distinct horizontal layers is called`,options:[`Capping`,`Lamination`,`Chipping`,`Picking`],answer:1,explanation:`Lamination is horizontal splitting into layers, and it is most often traced to over-lubrication with stearates or to entrapped air. Capping, its close cousin, is separation of the top or bottom crown only. Chipping is loss of material at the edges and picking is adhesion of material to the punch engraving.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-4`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`According to IP, tablets of average weight 200 mg must comply with a weight variation limit of`,options:[`5 percent`,`10 percent`,`7.5 percent`,`15 percent`],answer:2,explanation:`The IP limits are 10 percent for tablets of 80 mg or less, 7.5 percent for tablets above 80 mg and below 250 mg, and 5 percent for 250 mg and above. A 200 mg tablet therefore takes 7.5 percent. Not more than two of the twenty tablets may exceed this and none may deviate by more than twice it.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-5`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The USP dissolution apparatus of choice for a hard gelatin capsule product is`,options:[`Apparatus II, paddle`,`Apparatus I, rotating basket`,`Apparatus IV, flow-through cell`,`Apparatus V, paddle over disc`],answer:1,explanation:`A capsule shell is less dense than the medium and floats to the surface under a paddle, giving erratic results, so the basket is used to hold it in the flow. Apparatus II is the general default for tablets, apparatus IV suits poorly soluble drugs and implants, and apparatus V is for transdermal patches.`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-6`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The friability of uncoated tablets is determined in a Roche friabilator operated at 25 rpm for 4 minutes, and the accepted limit for weight loss is`,options:[`Not more than 1.0 percent`,`Not more than 0.1 percent`,`Not more than 5.0 percent`,`Not more than 10.0 percent`],answer:0,explanation:`Twenty-five revolutions per minute for four minutes gives 100 revolutions, and the loss in weight must not exceed 1.0 percent w/w. In addition, any tablet that caps, cracks or splits during the test fails outright, whatever the numerical weight loss.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-7`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Extending the blending time of magnesium stearate from 2 minutes to 15 minutes in a tablet granulation would be expected to`,options:[`Increase tablet hardness and shorten disintegration time`,`Have no effect provided the concentration is unchanged`,`Increase friability but improve dissolution`,`Decrease tablet hardness and prolong disintegration and dissolution`],answer:3,explanation:`Magnesium stearate is hydrophobic and delaminates during blending to film the granule surfaces. Prolonged mixing spreads that film further, which interferes with interparticulate bonding, so hardness falls, and it repels water, so disintegration and dissolution slow. This is why the lubricant is added last and blended for the shortest validated time.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-8`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A granulation has a bulk density of 0.42 g/mL and a tapped density of 0.60 g/mL. Its Carr compressibility index and the corresponding flow character are`,options:[`30 percent, poor flow`,`43 percent, very poor flow`,`18 percent, fair flow`,`12 percent, good flow`],answer:0,explanation:`Carr index = 100 x (0.60 - 0.42) / 0.60 = 100 x 0.18 / 0.60 = 30 percent, which falls in the poor flow band of 26 to 31 percent. The 43 percent distractor comes from wrongly dividing the difference by the bulk density instead of the tapped density; the denominator is always the tapped density.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-1`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The first stage in sugar coating a tablet is`,options:[`Subcoating`,`Smoothing`,`Polishing`,`Sealing or waterproofing`],answer:3,explanation:`Sealing with shellac, CAP, PVP or zein is applied first to stop the aqueous syrup penetrating and softening the core. Subcoating follows to round the edges, then smoothing, colouring, polishing and finally printing.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-2`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following is an enteric coating polymer?`,options:[`Hydroxypropyl methylcellulose`,`Ethylcellulose`,`Cellulose acetate phthalate`,`Polyvinyl alcohol`],answer:2,explanation:`Cellulose acetate phthalate carries free phthalic acid groups that stay unionised in gastric acid and ionise above about pH 6, so the film dissolves only in the intestine. HPMC and PVA are freely water soluble at all pH values and ethylcellulose is water insoluble throughout, so it is used for sustained release, not enteric release.`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-3`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Eudragit S100 differs from Eudragit L100 mainly in that it dissolves`,options:[`Below pH 5`,`Above pH 5.5`,`Above pH 6.0`,`Above pH 7.0`],answer:3,explanation:`Eudragit S100 dissolves above pH 7.0, which is why it is used for colonic delivery, while L100 dissolves above pH 6.0 and L100-55 above pH 5.5. Dissolution below pH 5 is the property of Eudragit E, the aminoalkyl methacrylate used for taste masking and gastric release.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-4`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A film coated batch shows the tablet logo filled in and no longer legible, with a foamy coating suspension in the tank. This defect and its remedy are`,options:[`Infilling, remedied by adding an antifoam and reducing the spray rate`,`Bridging, remedied by increasing the plasticiser level`,`Blushing, remedied by lowering the inlet air temperature`,`Orange peel, remedied by thinning the coating solution`],answer:0,explanation:`Foam in the coating liquid deposits bubbles that fill the engraving, which is infilling; an antifoam such as alcohol or simethicone plus a lower spray rate fixes it. Bridging looks similar but is the film lifting out of the engraving because of shrinkage stress, and its remedy is more plasticiser, not less foam.`,tag:`Trap`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-5`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The main function of a plasticiser such as triethyl citrate in a film coating formulation is to`,options:[`Increase the solubility of the polymer in water`,`Lower the glass transition temperature so the film is flexible and resists cracking`,`Act as an opacifier and hide the core colour`,`Speed up evaporation of the coating solvent`],answer:1,explanation:`A plasticiser inserts itself between polymer chains, reduces chain to chain interaction and lowers the glass transition temperature, so the film deforms rather than cracking or bridging over an engraving. Opacification is the job of titanium dioxide, and plasticisers are non-volatile so they slow, not speed, drying.`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-6`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The coating equipment in which tablets or pellets are suspended in an upward air stream and sprayed from below is the`,options:[`Conventional pear-shaped coating pan`,`Perforated pan coater of the Accela-Cota type`,`Fluidised bed Wurster coater`,`Immersion sword system`],answer:2,explanation:`The Wurster coater is a fluidised bed with a bottom spray nozzle inside a central partition; it gives the most efficient drying and is the standard for coating pellets and granules. Perforated pans draw air through a tumbling tablet bed but the tablets are not fluidised, and the immersion sword is an accessory added to a conventional pan.`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-7`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A sustained release product coated with an aqueous ethylcellulose dispersion shows dissolution that slows progressively over six months on stability. The most likely cause is`,options:[`Crosslinking of the polymer by residual peroxides`,`Incomplete coalescence of the latex film because curing was inadequate`,`Migration of the colourant into the core`,`Loss of plasticiser by evaporation during storage`],answer:1,explanation:`An aqueous polymeric dispersion forms its film by particle coalescence, which continues slowly at room temperature if the coated product is not cured above the minimum film forming temperature. As coalescence completes on the shelf the film becomes less porous and release falls. Adequate curing, typically 40 to 60 degrees C for several hours, stabilises the profile.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-8`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Compression coating is chosen over film coating principally when`,options:[`A very glossy elegant finish is required`,`The lowest possible weight gain is needed`,`The product must be enteric coated`,`Two ingredients are incompatible and must be physically separated, or the drug is moisture sensitive`],answer:3,explanation:`Compression coating uses no solvent and no water at all, and it produces a genuine physical barrier of coating granulation around a separate core, so it suits moisture sensitive drugs and lets two incompatible ingredients be placed in core and coat. It gives a large weight gain, not a small one, and gloss is a property of polishing in sugar coating.`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-1`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Among the hard gelatin capsule sizes used for human medicines, the largest and the smallest are respectively`,options:[`0 and 5`,`000 and 5`,`5 and 000`,`00 and 4`],answer:1,explanation:`Size 000 has the greatest capacity, about 1.37 mL, and size 5 the smallest, about 0.13 mL. The numbering is inverse: as the number rises the capsule gets smaller, and the sizes with zeros are the extra large ones.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-2`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Gelatin used in capsule shells is obtained by the partial hydrolysis of`,options:[`Collagen`,`Cellulose`,`Chitin`,`Casein`],answer:0,explanation:`Gelatin is the partial hydrolysis product of collagen from animal skin, bone and connective tissue. Type A comes from acid hydrolysis of pork skin and Type B from alkaline lime treatment of bone. Cellulose gives the vegetarian HPMC alternative but is not gelatin.`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-3`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The rotary die process of Scherer is used for the manufacture of`,options:[`Hard gelatin capsule shells`,`Enteric coated pellets`,`Soft gelatin capsules`,`Compression coated tablets`],answer:2,explanation:`In the rotary die process two continuous gelatin ribbons pass between counter-rotating dies while the fill is injected between them, so the shell is formed, filled and sealed in one operation, which defines soft gelatin capsule manufacture. Hard shells are made separately by dipping stainless steel pins into gelatin solution.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-4`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Automatic hard capsule filling machines measure the dose by one of two principles. These are`,options:[`Auger fill and gravity fill`,`Vacuum fill and vibratory fill`,`Volumetric cup and weight cell feedback`,`Dosator (dosing tube) and tamping with a dosing disc`],answer:3,explanation:`The dosator machines such as Zanasi and MG2 compress a plug inside a dosing tube and eject it into the body, while the tamping machines such as Bosch GKF and Hofliger-Karg tamp powder into holes in a rotating dosing disc. Both form a consolidated plug, which is why powder flow and lubrication still matter for capsules.`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-5`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`According to IP, the uniformity of weight limit for hard gelatin capsules of average content 500 mg is`,options:[`5 percent`,`7.5 percent`,`10 percent`,`15 percent`],answer:1,explanation:`The IP capsule limits are 10 percent where the average content is less than 300 mg and 7.5 percent where it is 300 mg or more, so a 500 mg fill takes 7.5 percent. As with tablets, not more than two of the twenty may exceed the limit and none may exceed twice it.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-6`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The principal advantage of HPMC capsule shells over gelatin shells is that they`,options:[`Dissolve faster in every dissolution medium`,`Are cheaper to manufacture`,`Are of plant origin and have a low shell moisture content, so they suit hygroscopic fills and vegetarian patients`,`Do not require any colourant or opacifier`],answer:2,explanation:`HPMC shells are cellulose-derived, so they are acceptable to vegetarian and religiously restricted patients, and they hold much less water than gelatin, so they neither embrittle when a hygroscopic fill dries them out nor soften at high humidity. They are more expensive than gelatin and their dissolution can actually be slower and more medium dependent.`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-7`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A gelatin capsule product on stability fails dissolution, and a pellicle is seen on the shell. Adding pepsin to the dissolution medium restores compliance. The underlying cause is`,options:[`Crosslinking of gelatin, typically by trace aldehydes with heat and humidity`,`Polymorphic transition of the drug`,`Sorption of the drug onto the shell`,`Loss of shell moisture below 10 percent`],answer:0,explanation:`Trace aldehydes from excipients or packaging, aided by heat, humidity and light, crosslink gelatin into an insoluble pellicle that will not dissolve but is digested by an enzyme, which is why the pharmacopoeias allow a two-tier test with added pepsin or pancreatin. Low shell moisture makes capsules brittle but does not create a pellicle or change dissolution in this way.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-8`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A powder of tapped density 0.90 g/mL is filled at 300 mg per capsule. Given capacities of 0.68 mL (size 0), 0.50 mL (size 1), 0.37 mL (size 2) and 0.30 mL (size 3), the smallest suitable capsule size is`,options:[`Size 0`,`Size 1`,`Size 2`,`Size 3`],answer:2,explanation:`Volume needed = mass divided by tapped density = 0.300 g / 0.90 g per mL = 0.333 mL. Size 3 at 0.30 mL is too small, so the smallest that will hold the fill is size 2 at 0.37 mL. The point is that capsule size selection is decided by the powder density, not by the dose weight alone; a lighter powder of the same 300 mg dose could need size 0.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-1`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which vehicle is never permitted for parenteral use?`,options:[`Arachis oil`,`Sesame oil`,`Mineral (liquid) paraffin`,`Propylene glycol`],answer:2,explanation:`Mineral oil is a hydrocarbon that the body cannot metabolise, and injecting it produces granulomas, so only fixed oils of vegetable origin such as arachis, sesame, cottonseed and castor are permitted. Propylene glycol is an accepted water-miscible cosolvent within concentration limits.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-2`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The most potent pyrogens encountered in parenteral manufacture are`,options:[`Exotoxins of Gram-positive bacteria`,`Fungal spores`,`Residual antioxidants`,`Lipopolysaccharide endotoxins from Gram-negative bacterial cell walls`],answer:3,explanation:`Bacterial endotoxins are lipopolysaccharides shed from the outer membrane of Gram-negative organisms, and the lipid A portion is responsible for the fever. They are thermostable, survive autoclaving and pass through 0.22 micrometre sterilising filters, which is why depyrogenation is a separate operation from sterilisation.`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-3`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The reagent for the bacterial endotoxin test is obtained from`,options:[`Amoebocyte lysate of the horseshoe crab Limulus polyphemus`,`Rabbit serum`,`Bovine thrombin`,`A recombinant Escherichia coli extract`],answer:0,explanation:`Limulus amoebocyte lysate is prepared from the blood cells of the horseshoe crab and clots on contact with endotoxin. It detects only Gram-negative endotoxin, whereas the rabbit test detects any fever-producing substance; the two are therefore not fully interchangeable.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-4`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Depyrogenation of glass ampoules is normally achieved by`,options:[`Autoclaving at 121 degrees C for 15 minutes`,`Dry heat at 250 degrees C for 30 minutes`,`Filtration through a 0.22 micrometre membrane`,`Exposure to ethylene oxide gas`],answer:1,explanation:`Endotoxins survive autoclaving, so moist heat sterilises but does not depyrogenate. Dry heat at 250 degrees C for 30 minutes destroys the lipopolysaccharide molecule itself, and a validated cycle must demonstrate at least a three log reduction in endotoxin challenge. Sterilising filters remove bacteria but not dissolved endotoxin.`,tag:`Trap`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-5`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which glass type is required for an alkaline parenteral solution?`,options:[`Type I borosilicate`,`Type II treated soda-lime`,`Type III soda-lime`,`Type NP`],answer:0,explanation:`Only Type I borosilicate glass has bulk hydrolytic resistance throughout its thickness. Type II relies on a thin dealkalised surface layer which an alkaline product strips away, exposing ordinary soda-lime glass; Type III is for non-aqueous parenterals and powders, and Type NP is not permitted for injections at all.`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-6`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`During lyophilisation, the purpose of the secondary drying stage is to`,options:[`Sublime the frozen free water`,`Freeze the product below its eutectic point`,`Desorb the residual bound water that did not freeze`,`Sterilise the dried cake`],answer:2,explanation:`Primary drying sublimes the ice and removes roughly 90 percent of the water. Secondary drying then raises the shelf temperature to 30 to 50 degrees C under deep vacuum to desorb the bound, unfrozen water and bring residual moisture down to about 0.5 to 3 percent, which is what determines shelf life.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-7`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`For a drug given intravenously at a maximum dose of 2 mg per kg per hour, the bacterial endotoxin limit is`,options:[`0.4 EU per mg`,`2.5 EU per mg`,`5 EU per mg`,`10 EU per mg`],answer:1,explanation:`The limit is K divided by M, where K is 5 EU per kg for parenteral routes other than intrathecal and M is the maximum dose per kg in one hour. Here 5 divided by 2 gives 2.5 EU per mg of drug. Using K equal to 0.2 would give 0.1 EU per mg, but that value applies only to the intrathecal route.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-8`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A 2 percent w/v solution of a drug with a sodium chloride equivalent of 0.20 is to be made isotonic. The sodium chloride to be added per 100 mL is`,options:[`0.90 g`,`0.70 g`,`0.50 g`,`0.40 g`],answer:2,explanation:`Sodium chloride represented by the drug = E x C = 0.20 x 2 = 0.40 g per 100 mL. The extra required = 0.9 minus 0.40 = 0.50 g per 100 mL. The 0.40 g distractor is the tonicity already contributed by the drug rather than the amount still to be added.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-1`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The most commonly used preservative in multi-dose eye drops is`,options:[`Sodium benzoate`,`Benzalkonium chloride`,`Phenol`,`Sodium metabisulphite`],answer:1,explanation:`Benzalkonium chloride at about 0.01 percent is the standard ophthalmic preservative because it is rapid and broad spectrum. Phenol is far too irritant for the eye, sodium benzoate is an oral preservative with poor activity at ocular pH, and sodium metabisulphite is an antioxidant, not a preservative.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-2`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Ophthalmic preparations intended for use during eye surgery or on an injured eye must be`,options:[`Preserved with a double concentration of benzalkonium chloride`,`Buffered to pH 5 for stability`,`Made hypertonic to reduce corneal oedema`,`Supplied preservative free in single use containers`],answer:3,explanation:`Preservatives are toxic to exposed corneal and intraocular tissue, so intraocular solutions and products for a traumatised eye carry no preservative and are supplied as single dose, single use units. Doubling the preservative would increase toxicity, and a pH of 5 is chosen only where drug stability forces it.`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-3`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Disodium edetate is added along with benzalkonium chloride in eye drops in order to`,options:[`Enhance activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa by chelating cell wall metal ions`,`Adjust the tonicity of the solution`,`Increase the viscosity of the drop`,`Buffer the preparation to pH 7.4`],answer:0,explanation:`EDTA chelates the divalent cations that stabilise the Pseudomonas outer membrane, so the cell becomes permeable and benzalkonium chloride acts far more effectively. It has no useful tonicity, viscosity or buffering role at the 0.01 to 0.1 percent levels used.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-4`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Benzalkonium chloride cannot be used to preserve eye drops containing`,options:[`Pilocarpine nitrate`,`Atropine sulphate`,`Sodium fluorescein`,`Homatropine hydrobromide`],answer:2,explanation:`Benzalkonium chloride is a cationic quaternary ammonium compound and is inactivated by anionic drugs. Sodium fluorescein is anionic and forms an insoluble complex, so chlorobutanol or a mercurial is used instead. The alkaloidal salts listed are cationic themselves and are compatible.`,tag:`Trap`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-5`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The Ocusert system for pilocarpine is best described as`,options:[`A soluble hydroxypropyl cellulose rod that dissolves in the tear fluid`,`An insoluble reservoir insert with ethylene vinyl acetate rate-controlling membranes giving zero order release for a week`,`An in situ gelling solution triggered by tear cations`,`A collagen shield loaded with drug`],answer:1,explanation:`Ocusert Pilo-20 and Pilo-40 are insoluble elliptical inserts with a pilocarpine reservoir between two ethylene vinyl acetate membranes that control the rate, giving near zero order delivery for seven days, after which the insert is removed. The soluble hydroxypropyl cellulose rod is Lacrisert, which is drug free and used for dry eye.`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-6`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The freezing point depression of lacrimal fluid, used as the target for isotonicity adjustment of eye drops, is`,options:[`0.28 degrees C`,`0.90 degrees C`,`1.86 degrees C`,`0.52 degrees C`],answer:3,explanation:`Tears and blood both freeze at minus 0.52 degrees C, and a solution matched to that depression is isotonic. The value 0.90 is the percentage of sodium chloride that achieves it, and 1.86 is the molal freezing point depression constant of water, a different quantity altogether.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-7`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Only about 1 to 10 percent of a drug instilled as an eye drop reaches the intraocular tissues. The single largest reason is`,options:[`Metabolism by corneal enzymes`,`Binding of the drug to the conjunctival mucin layer`,`Precorneal loss by drainage and tear turnover, since a 50 microlitre drop enters a cul-de-sac holding only 7 to 10 microlitres`,`Active efflux from the anterior chamber`],answer:2,explanation:`Most of an instilled drop is expelled immediately because the cul-de-sac cannot hold it, and the remainder is washed into the nasolacrimal duct by a tear turnover of about 1 microlitre per minute within a few minutes. Corneal metabolism and efflux exist but are minor compared with this simple volumetric and drainage loss.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-8`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`To prepare 15 mL of isotonic 2 percent w/v drug solution where the drug has a sodium chloride equivalent of 0.15, the sodium chloride to be weighed is approximately`,options:[`45 mg`,`90 mg`,`135 mg`,`180 mg`],answer:1,explanation:`NaCl represented by the drug = 0.15 x 2 = 0.30 g per 100 mL. Additional NaCl needed = 0.9 minus 0.30 = 0.60 g per 100 mL. For 15 mL this is 0.60 x 15/100 = 0.09 g, that is 90 mg. The 135 mg distractor comes from forgetting to subtract the drug contribution and taking the full 0.9 percent.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-1`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Polyethylene glycol ointment is an example of which base class?`,options:[`Oleaginous`,`Absorption`,`Water-removable`,`Water-soluble`],answer:3,explanation:`PEG ointment contains no oil phase at all and dissolves completely in water, so it is a water-soluble or greaseless base. A water-removable base such as hydrophilic ointment is an oil in water emulsion, so it still contains oil even though it washes off with water.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-2`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A paste differs from an ointment principally in that a paste contains`,options:[`A high proportion of insoluble powder, typically 20 to 50 percent`,`A larger proportion of water`,`An emulsifying agent`,`A volatile solvent`],answer:0,explanation:`The defining feature of a paste is its high solid content, which makes it stiffer, less greasy, more absorptive of exudate and more likely to stay exactly where it is applied. Emulsifying agents define creams, and water content is what distinguishes hydrophilic from oleaginous bases.`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-3`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Theobroma oil must not be heated above about 36 degrees C during suppository preparation because`,options:[`The drug will decompose`,`The base will oxidise and become rancid`,`The unstable alpha polymorph forms and the suppositories fail to set properly`,`It will react with the mould lubricant`],answer:2,explanation:`Theobroma oil is polymorphic; the stable beta form melts near 34.5 degrees C, but overheating destroys the crystal nuclei so it resolidifies as the unstable alpha form melting near 24 degrees C, which is too low to set in the mould or survive storage. Semisynthetic bases such as Witepsol were developed precisely to remove this problem.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-4`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The displacement value of a drug in a suppository base is defined as the number of parts by weight of drug that`,options:[`Dissolves in one part of the base`,`Displaces one part by weight of the base`,`Is displaced by one part of the base`,`Occupies one millilitre of mould volume`],answer:1,explanation:`Displacement value is the weight of drug that occupies the same volume as one part by weight of base, so a DV of 4.7 for zinc oxide means 4.7 g of the drug takes the space of 1 g of base. A value of 1.0 means the drug and base have the same density and no correction is required.`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-5`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Carbomer (Carbopol) forms a gel only after`,options:[`Heating above 70 degrees C`,`Addition of an electrolyte such as sodium chloride`,`Addition of a nonionic surfactant`,`Neutralisation with triethanolamine or sodium hydroxide to about pH 6 to 7`],answer:3,explanation:`Dispersed carbomer is an acidic, low viscosity slurry; neutralisation ionises the carboxyl groups so charge repulsion uncoils the polymer chains and builds the gel network. Adding electrolyte does the opposite, screening the charges and collapsing the gel, which is why carbomer gels are intolerant of salts and cationic drugs.`,tag:`Trap`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-6`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The spontaneous shrinking of a gel with exudation of the liquid phase is termed`,options:[`Imbibition`,`Swelling`,`Syneresis`,`Thixotropy`],answer:2,explanation:`Syneresis is the contraction of the gel network with expression of the entrapped liquid, seen as free liquid on the surface of a stored gel. Imbibition is uptake of liquid without volume increase, swelling is uptake with volume increase, and thixotropy is the reversible sol to gel change on standing and shaking.`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-7`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Twelve suppositories of 1 g mould calibration are to contain 300 mg of a drug with a displacement value of 3.0. The weight of base required is`,options:[`10.8 g`,`11.2 g`,`8.4 g`,`9.6 g`],answer:0,explanation:`Total drug = 12 x 0.3 = 3.6 g. Base displaced = 3.6 / 3.0 = 1.2 g. Base required = (12 x 1) minus 1.2 = 10.8 g. The 8.4 g distractor comes from subtracting the whole drug weight instead of the displaced volume equivalent, which is the commonest error in this calculation.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-8`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`According to the Higuchi model, drug release from an ointment in which the drug is suspended is proportional to`,options:[`Time`,`The square root of time`,`The logarithm of time`,`The cube root of time`],answer:1,explanation:`For a suspended drug in a matrix, diffusion through a progressively deepening depleted zone gives an amount released proportional to the square root of time, so a plot of Q against root t is linear. Zero order release with time would require a constant activity reservoir, and the cube root law applies to the dissolution of shrinking spherical particles, not to matrix release.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-1`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`An elixir is best defined as`,options:[`A concentrated aqueous solution of sucrose`,`A viscous preparation sipped slowly for cough`,`A dispersion of solid particles in a liquid`,`A clear, sweetened hydroalcoholic solution for oral use`],answer:3,explanation:`An elixir always contains alcohol and is always a clear solution, which is what lets it carry drugs too poorly soluble for a purely aqueous syrup. A concentrated aqueous sucrose solution is a syrup, a viscous preparation sipped for cough is a linctus, and a dispersion of solid particles is a suspension.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-2`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Simple syrup IP contains sucrose at a concentration of approximately`,options:[`20 percent w/w`,`45 percent w/w`,`66.7 percent w/w`,`85 percent w/w`],answer:2,explanation:`Simple syrup IP is 66.7 percent w/w sucrose, a near saturated solution. At this concentration the water activity is too low for microbial growth, so a preservative is unnecessary; a diluted syrup loses that protection and must then be preserved.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-3`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Compared with a deflocculated suspension, a flocculated suspension shows`,options:[`A lower sediment volume and a clear supernatant`,`A slower rate of sedimentation and a cloudy supernatant`,`A hard cake that resists redispersion`,`A higher sediment volume, a clear supernatant and easy redispersion`],answer:3,explanation:`Flocs settle quickly as loose aggregates, leaving a clear supernatant, a bulky sediment and no cake, so the product redisperses on gentle shaking. Deflocculated systems settle slowly with a cloudy supernatant and a small, tightly packed sediment that cakes and cannot be resuspended.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-4`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In the dry gum (continental) method of emulsion preparation, the ratio of oil to water to gum used for the primary emulsion with a fixed oil is`,options:[`4:2:1`,`2:1:4`,`1:2:4`,`3:2:1`],answer:0,explanation:`The classical primary emulsion for a fixed oil is four parts oil, two parts water and one part acacia, triturated rapidly until a characteristic clicking sound is heard. The ratio changes to 3:2:1 for volatile oils and 2:2:1 for oleaginous liquids such as liquid paraffin.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-5`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`An emulsion conducts electricity and dilutes freely with water. It is therefore`,options:[`A water in oil emulsion`,`A multiple emulsion`,`An oil in water emulsion`,`A microemulsion of unknown type`],answer:2,explanation:`Both the conductivity test and the dilution test identify the continuous phase, and both point to water. An oil in water emulsion has a continuous aqueous phase, so it carries current and mixes with water; a water in oil emulsion does neither because oil is the external phase.`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-6`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`According to Stokes law, the sedimentation velocity of suspended particles is`,options:[`Directly proportional to the particle diameter`,`Inversely proportional to the square of the particle diameter`,`Independent of the particle diameter`,`Directly proportional to the square of the particle diameter`],answer:3,explanation:`Velocity varies with d squared, so halving the particle diameter cuts the settling rate to a quarter. Velocity is also inversely proportional to the viscosity of the medium, which is why suspending agents are added; the density difference between phases is the third factor.`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-7`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Adding a small amount of an electrolyte to a deflocculated suspension in order to bring the zeta potential to about minus 20 mV is intended to`,options:[`Increase the zeta potential and prevent all aggregation`,`Allow particles to associate in the shallow secondary minimum so they floc loosely instead of caking`,`Reduce the viscosity of the vehicle`,`Dissolve the finest particles and stop Ostwald ripening`],answer:1,explanation:`Controlled flocculation works by lowering the repulsive barrier just enough for particles to sit in the shallow secondary energy minimum of the DLVO curve, where aggregation is reversible on shaking. Taking the zeta potential all the way to zero would let particles fall into the deep primary minimum, which is exactly the irreversible caking being avoided.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-8`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A 120 mL suspension leaves a 36 mL sediment on standing, while the corresponding deflocculated system leaves 18 mL. The sedimentation volume F and the degree of flocculation are`,options:[`F = 0.15, beta = 2`,`F = 0.30, beta = 2`,`F = 0.30, beta = 0.5`,`F = 0.36, beta = 1.5`],answer:1,explanation:`F equals Vu divided by Vo, that is 36 divided by 120, which is 0.30. For the deflocculated control F(infinity) is 18 divided by 120, which is 0.15. Beta equals F divided by F(infinity), that is 0.30 divided by 0.15, which is 2. A beta of 2 means flocculation has doubled the sediment volume, the desired outcome.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-1`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Propellant 12 in the aerosol numbering system is`,options:[`Dichlorodifluoromethane`,`Trichloromonofluoromethane`,`Dichlorotetrafluoroethane`,`1,1,1,2-tetrafluoroethane`],answer:0,explanation:`Propellant 12 is dichlorodifluoromethane, boiling at minus 29.8 degrees C, and it was the main CFC propellant before the Montreal Protocol. Propellant 11 is trichloromonofluoromethane, propellant 114 is dichlorotetrafluoroethane, and 1,1,1,2-tetrafluoroethane is HFA-134a, the replacement.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-2`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The optimum aerodynamic particle size range for deposition of an inhaled drug in the lower airways is`,options:[`0.1 to 0.5 micrometres`,`1 to 5 micrometres`,`10 to 20 micrometres`,`20 to 50 micrometres`],answer:1,explanation:`Particles of 1 to 5 micrometres sediment in the small airways and alveoli, which is the therapeutic target. Above about 10 micrometres inertial impaction deposits them in the oropharynx, and below about 0.5 micrometres they remain suspended in the air stream and are exhaled again.`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-3`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Chlorofluorocarbon propellants were withdrawn from metered dose inhalers because they`,options:[`Are flammable and explosive`,`Are systemically toxic on inhalation`,`Cannot generate enough vapour pressure`,`Deplete stratospheric ozone through chlorine released by photolysis`],answer:3,explanation:`Ultraviolet light splits chlorine radicals from CFCs in the stratosphere, and each radical catalytically destroys many ozone molecules, which led to the phase out under the Montreal Protocol of 1987. CFCs are in fact non-flammable, which was originally their attraction; the hydrocarbon alternatives are the flammable ones.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-4`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A metering valve in a pressurised inhaler typically delivers a volume of`,options:[`0.5 to 2 microlitres`,`1 to 2 millilitres`,`25 to 100 microlitres`,`5 to 10 millilitres`],answer:2,explanation:`The metering chamber of a pressurised inhaler holds roughly 25 to 100 microlitres of formulation per actuation, and after the propellant flashes off this leaves a drug dose in the microgram to low milligram range. Do not confuse the chamber VOLUME with the emitted MASS, which is often quoted as about 50 to 150 milligrams — the two are different quantities and mixing them up is the usual error here. Continuous spray valves, used for topical aerosols, are not metered at all and deliver product for as long as the actuator is held down.`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-5`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which statement correctly distinguishes a dry powder inhaler from a metered dose inhaler?`,options:[`The DPI is breath actuated and depends on the patient inspiratory flow, whereas the MDI requires coordination of actuation with inhalation`,`The DPI needs a propellant while the MDI does not`,`The DPI needs a spacer whereas the MDI does not`,`The DPI delivers a larger dose over 10 to 20 minutes`],answer:0,explanation:`A DPI has no propellant; the patient inspiratory effort, usually 30 to 60 L/min, both disperses the powder from its carrier and carries it into the lung, so no coordination is required. A spacer helps an MDI, not a DPI, and delivery over 10 to 20 minutes describes a nebuliser.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-6`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Compared with a liquefied gas system, a compressed gas aerosol system shows`,options:[`Constant pressure throughout the life of the container`,`A finer and drier spray at the end of the container`,`Complete independence of the spray quality from the amount used`,`A pressure that falls steadily as the container empties`],answer:3,explanation:`A liquefied propellant keeps pressure constant because vapour lost from the headspace is replaced by evaporation from the liquid reservoir. A compressed gas has no such reservoir, so pressure falls with each actuation, and the spray becomes coarser and wetter as the container empties.`,tag:`Trap`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-7`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The instrument used to measure the aerodynamic particle size distribution and the fine particle fraction of an inhalation aerosol is the`,options:[`Roche friabilator`,`Andersen cascade impactor`,`Brookfield viscometer`,`Coulter counter`],answer:1,explanation:`A cascade impactor fractionates the aerosol on successive stages by inertial impaction, so each stage collects a defined aerodynamic size band, allowing the MMAD and the fine particle fraction below 5 micrometres to be calculated. A Coulter counter measures the electrical zone size of particles in an electrolyte and gives geometric, not aerodynamic, diameter.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-8`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In a dry powder inhaler, the role of coarse alpha-lactose monohydrate is to`,options:[`Sweeten the inhaled dose`,`Act as the propellant`,`Act as a carrier that gives the micronised drug flow and metering, from which the drug must detach on inhalation`,`Absorb moisture so the drug stays dry`],answer:2,explanation:`Micronised drug alone is far too cohesive to flow or to be metered, so it is blended onto coarse lactose carrier particles. The fine particle fraction then depends on how readily the drug detaches from the carrier during inhalation, which is why lactose surface roughness and fines content are formulation critical variables rather than incidental.`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-1`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Type I glass owes its high hydrolytic resistance to the presence of`,options:[`Lead oxide`,`Boric oxide replacing part of the alkali`,`A surface sulphur dioxide treatment`,`Added alumina only`],answer:1,explanation:`Borosilicate glass contains boric oxide, which enters the silicate network and reduces the free alkali available to leach into the product, giving resistance throughout the thickness of the glass. Surface treatment with sulphur dioxide is what makes Type II, and it protects only the inner surface layer.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-2`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A container that is impervious to air or any other gas under ordinary conditions of handling is described as`,options:[`Well-closed`,`Tight`,`Hermetically sealed`,`Light-resistant`],answer:2,explanation:`A hermetically sealed container excludes air and gases entirely, the sealed glass ampoule being the standard example. A tight container excludes liquids, solids and vapours but is not gas impervious, and a well-closed container only protects against extraneous solids and loss of contents.`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-3`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The water attack test, performed on intact containers rather than on powdered glass, is applied to`,options:[`Type II glass`,`Type I glass`,`Type III glass`,`Type NP glass`],answer:0,explanation:`Type II glass is ordinary soda-lime whose inner surface has been dealkalised, so crushing it would destroy the very layer being tested. The intact container is therefore autoclaved and the extract titrated. Types I, III and NP are graded by the powdered glass test, which reflects bulk composition.`,tag:`Trap`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-4`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In the powdered glass test using 0.02 N sulphuric acid, the limit for Type I glass is`,options:[`Not more than 0.7 mL`,`Not more than 8.5 mL`,`Not more than 15.0 mL`,`Not more than 1.0 mL`],answer:3,explanation:`Type I is limited to 1.0 mL of 0.02 N acid, Type III to 8.5 mL and Type NP to 15.0 mL for the same 10 g of glass grains. The 0.7 mL figure belongs to the water attack test on Type II containers of 100 mL or less, a different test with a different sample form.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-5`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Butyl rubber is preferred for the stoppers of lyophilised product vials because it has`,options:[`The lowest cost among elastomers`,`The highest elasticity of all elastomers`,`The lowest permeability to moisture and gases`,`Complete freedom from extractables`],answer:2,explanation:`A freeze-dried cake must stay dry and often under an inert headspace, and butyl and halobutyl rubbers have the lowest water vapour and gas transmission of the pharmaceutical elastomers. Silicone is far more permeable despite being highly inert, and no elastomer is free of extractables, which is why fluoropolymer laminated faces are used.`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-6`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which plastic is chosen as a blister film component specifically for its outstanding moisture barrier?`,options:[`Low density polyethylene`,`Polystyrene`,`Polyvinylidene chloride (PVDC)`,`Polycarbonate`],answer:2,explanation:`PVDC is laminated or coated onto the PVC base web precisely because plain PVC is a mediocre moisture barrier; the PVDC layer raises it dramatically. Polystyrene has poor barrier properties, LDPE is permeable, and polycarbonate is chosen for mechanical strength rather than for barrier performance.`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-7`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which container closure integrity method is classified as deterministic rather than probabilistic?`,options:[`Microbial immersion challenge`,`Methylene blue dye ingress`,`Vacuum decay leak testing`,`Bubble emission test`],answer:2,explanation:`Vacuum decay measures a physical pressure change against a defined instrumental limit, giving a quantitative and reproducible result independent of the operator, which is what deterministic means. Microbial ingress, dye ingress and bubble emission all depend on chance events or operator judgement and are therefore probabilistic, with much greater variability.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-8`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The essential difference between extractables and leachables in packaging is that`,options:[`Extractables are obtained under exaggerated laboratory conditions, whereas leachables actually migrate into the product under normal storage`,`Extractables come from glass and leachables from plastic`,`Extractables are volatile and leachables are non-volatile`,`Extractables are measured on the finished product and leachables on the raw material`],answer:0,explanation:`An extractables study deliberately stresses the material with aggressive solvents, heat and time to map everything that could possibly come out, while a leachables study measures what genuinely appears in the product on stability. Leachables are therefore normally a subset of extractables, and the study order is raw material first, finished product second.`},{id:`q-pt-preformulation-9`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Micronising a poorly soluble drug increases its dissolution rate mainly because micronisation`,options:[`Raises the saturation solubility of the drug several fold`,`Converts the drug to a more soluble polymorph`,`Increases the effective surface area available to the dissolution medium`,`Removes the stagnant diffusion layer around each particle`],answer:2,explanation:`In the Noyes-Whitney expression the rate is proportional to the surface area A, so reducing particle size raises the rate without altering the equilibrium solubility Cs, which is a thermodynamic property of the solid form. Saturation solubility rises appreciably only in the submicron range through the Ostwald-Freundlich effect, and the diffusion layer is thinned by stirring, not by grinding.`},{id:`q-pt-preformulation-10`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The definitive routine technique for identifying and distinguishing polymorphs of a drug substance is`,options:[`Ultraviolet spectrophotometry of the solution`,`X-ray powder diffraction`,`Karl Fischer titration`,`Polarimetry`],answer:1,explanation:`Each polymorph has a distinct lattice and therefore a distinct powder diffraction pattern, so XRPD is the reference method and DSC and infrared are supporting evidence. Ultraviolet spectrophotometry is done on a solution, where all polymorphs are identical, Karl Fischer measures only water, and polarimetry detects optical rotation, which is a molecular and not a lattice property.`},{id:`q-pt-preformulation-11`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A weakly acidic drug of pKa 4.4 is placed in gastric fluid of pH 1.4. The percentage present in the unionised, absorbable form is about`,options:[`About 0.1 percent`,`About 50 percent`,`About 99.9 percent`,`About 90 percent`],answer:2,explanation:`For an acid, log (ionised / unionised) = pH minus pKa = 1.4 minus 4.4 = minus 3, so the ratio of ionised to unionised is 1 to 1000 and the unionised fraction is 1000/1001, that is about 99.9 percent. The 50 percent figure would apply only if the pH equalled the pKa, and 0.1 percent is the same calculation done for a weak base of the same pKa.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-preformulation-12`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A candidate drug is a primary amine. Which diluent should be avoided in its tablet formulation?`,options:[`Lactose monohydrate`,`Mannitol`,`Microcrystalline cellulose`,`Dicalcium phosphate dihydrate`],answer:0,explanation:`Lactose is a reducing sugar and its aldehyde group condenses with primary and secondary amines in the Maillard reaction, giving brown discolouration and assay loss; the classic case is amine drugs browning in lactose blends. Mannitol is a non-reducing polyol, microcrystalline cellulose is a polysaccharide with no free aldehyde, and dicalcium phosphate dihydrate is inorganic, so all three are safe on this count.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-preformulation-13`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Loss on drying differs from Karl Fischer titration in that loss on drying`,options:[`Is specific for water and excludes all other volatiles`,`Uses an iodine and sulphur dioxide reagent`,`Cannot be applied to a hydrated solid`,`Measures every volatile substance lost on heating, including residual solvent, whereas Karl Fischer measures water specifically`],answer:3,explanation:`Loss on drying is a gravimetric total volatile figure, so a solvate reports a value higher than its true water content, while Karl Fischer titrates water alone by the iodine and sulphur dioxide reaction. A large gap between the two results is itself the clue that residual solvent is present. The iodine and sulphur dioxide reagent belongs to Karl Fischer, not to loss on drying.`,tag:`Trap`},{id:`q-pt-preformulation-14`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A poorly soluble drug that degrades above 90 degrees C is to be converted into an amorphous solid dispersion at production scale. The most suitable process is`,options:[`Hot melt extrusion at 160 degrees C`,`Melt granulation with a waxy carrier`,`Dry co-milling with lactose in a ball mill`,`Spray drying from a common volatile solvent`],answer:3,explanation:`Spray drying dissolves drug and polymer in one solvent and removes it in milliseconds, so the solid is trapped in the amorphous state while the droplet itself stays near the wet bulb temperature, well below the degradation point. Hot melt extrusion and melt granulation both demand temperatures at or above the polymer softening range, and co-milling gives only partial and inconsistent amorphisation.`},{id:`q-pt-preformulation-15`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A drug has an aqueous solubility of 0.1 mg/mL and a highest dose strength of 100 mg. Taking the dose number as dose divided by the product of 250 mL and the solubility, the dose number is`,options:[`0.25`,`2.5`,`4`,`40`],answer:2,explanation:`Dose number = 100 mg / (250 mL x 0.1 mg per mL) = 100 / 25 = 4. A dose number above 1 means a glass of water cannot dissolve the dose, so the drug is not highly soluble under the BCS and dissolution or solubilisation will control absorption. The value 0.25 comes from inverting the fraction and 40 from omitting the 250 mL volume.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-preformulation-16`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Two polymorphs of a drug are described as enantiotropically related. This means that`,options:[`The metastable form converts irreversibly to the stable form at every temperature below melting`,`Each form is the thermodynamically stable one over its own temperature range, with a reversible transition point below both melting points`,`The two forms have identical solubility at every temperature`,`The two forms differ only in external crystal shape`],answer:1,explanation:`Enantiotropes have their free energy curves crossing at a transition temperature that lies below both melting points, so heating or cooling through that point converts one into the other reversibly. Irreversible conversion at all temperatures is the definition of monotropy, identical solubility would mean the forms were not distinct at all, and a difference only in external shape is crystal habit.`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-9`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Small amounts of the tablet surface are removed by and adhere to the engraved lettering on the punch face. This defect is`,options:[`Sticking`,`Mottling`,`Chipping`,`Picking`],answer:3,explanation:`Picking is the specific form of adhesion in which material is pulled out of the tablet face by the letters or logo of the punch, and it is worst with fine engraving, excess moisture or too little antiadherent. Sticking is adhesion over the whole punch face or die wall, chipping is breakage at the tablet edge, and mottling is uneven colour with no loss of material.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-10`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`On a rotary tablet press the weight of the tablet is set by`,options:[`The depth of penetration of the upper punch`,`The position of the lower punch as it passes the weight adjustment cam, which fixes the fill volume`,`The speed of the turret`,`The gap between the pre-compression rollers`],answer:1,explanation:`The die is volumetrically filled, so lowering or raising the lower punch under the weight adjustment cam changes the volume of granulation retained and therefore the tablet weight. Upper punch penetration and the main compression roller setting change the applied pressure and hence the hardness at constant weight, while turret speed alters dwell time and output.`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-11`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A coloured tablet batch shows an uneven, blotchy distribution of colour over the surface. The most likely cause is`,options:[`Over-lubrication with magnesium stearate`,`Worn punch tips`,`Too low a compression force`,`Migration of the soluble dye to the granule surface during drying of the wet mass`],answer:3,explanation:`As the wet granulation dries, water moving to the surface carries dissolved dye with it, so the dye concentrates on the outside of some granules and the compact looks blotchy; using a lake pigment rather than a soluble dye, or drying more slowly, corrects it. Over-lubrication softens tablets and slows dissolution but does not affect colour distribution, and worn tips produce burrs and weight variation.`,tag:`Trap`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-12`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A batch caps consistently on ejection. Which single change is most likely to correct it?`,options:[`Raising the main compression force further`,`Introducing a pre-compression stage and changing to tapered dies`,`Increasing the magnesium stearate concentration`,`Drying the granulation to a still lower moisture content`],answer:1,explanation:`Capping is caused by air entrapped in the compact and by rapid elastic recovery on decompression; pre-compression expels the air and lengthens the consolidation time, while a tapered die lets the compact relax gradually as it is ejected instead of springing out at once. Raising the force traps more air and worsens capping, extra stearate weakens interparticulate bonding, and over-drying removes the residual moisture that helps binding.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-13`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A granulation contains a thermolabile drug that is also readily oxidised. The most appropriate dryer is`,options:[`A tray dryer with circulating hot air`,`A fluidised bed dryer`,`A vacuum tray dryer`,`A spray dryer`],answer:2,explanation:`Under reduced pressure water boils at a much lower temperature, so a vacuum tray dryer dries at a low product temperature and, because the chamber is evacuated and usually bled with nitrogen, oxygen is largely excluded. Tray and fluidised bed dryers both use large volumes of hot air, which suits neither a thermolabile nor an oxidisable material, and a spray dryer takes a feed solution or slurry, not a wet granulation.`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-14`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A powder has a bulk density of 0.50 g/mL and a tapped density of 0.65 g/mL. Its Hausner ratio and flow character are`,options:[`1.30, passable flow`,`0.77, good flow`,`1.15, excellent flow`,`1.30, excellent flow`],answer:0,explanation:`Hausner ratio = tapped density / bulk density = 0.65 / 0.50 = 1.30, which sits in the passable band between about 1.26 and 1.34; the corresponding Carr index, 100 x 0.15 / 0.65, is 23 percent and gives the same verdict. The 0.77 value is the ratio taken upside down as bulk over tapped, which can never exceed 1 and is therefore always the wrong way round.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-15`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A flat-faced tablet of 10 mm diameter and 4 mm thickness fails diametrally at a force of 100 N. Its tensile strength is about`,options:[`1.59 MPa`,`0.80 MPa`,`3.18 MPa`,`0.40 MPa`],answer:0,explanation:`Tensile strength = 2F / (pi x D x t) = (2 x 100) / (3.1416 x 10 x 4) = 200 / 125.7 = 1.59 N per mm2, that is 1.59 MPa. The 3.18 MPa answer comes from leaving out one of the dimensions in the denominator. Tensile strength is preferred to a hardness reading because it is independent of the tester used and corrects for tablet size.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-tablets-16`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A direct compression blend containing 0.5 mg of a potent drug per 200 mg tablet repeatedly fails content uniformity with a high relative standard deviation. The most rational formulation remedy is`,options:[`Increase the compression force so the tablets are harder`,`Increase the magnesium stearate level to improve blend flow`,`Add a further 2 percent of superdisintegrant`,`Micronise the drug and build an ordered (interactive) blend on a fine carrier, adding the drug by geometric dilution`],answer:3,explanation:`At very low dose the limiting factor is the number of drug particles per tablet and their tendency to segregate from a coarse carrier; micronising raises the particle count enormously and adhering the fines to a fine carrier by ordered mixing locks the distribution in, while geometric dilution prevents a concentrated pocket at the start. Compression force, disintegrant level and lubricant level affect hardness, disintegration and flow, none of which controls dose uniformity.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-9`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which material is included in a film coating formulation as the opacifier?`,options:[`Triethyl citrate`,`Titanium dioxide`,`Polyethylene glycol 400`,`Shellac`],answer:1,explanation:`Titanium dioxide has a very high refractive index, so it scatters light and hides a mottled or coloured core, and it also protects photolabile drugs. Triethyl citrate and polyethylene glycol 400 are plasticisers, and shellac is a sealing and polishing material used in sugar coating.`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-10`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The purpose of the subcoating stage of sugar coating is to`,options:[`Give the finished tablet its gloss`,`Waterproof the core against the aqueous syrup`,`Round off the sharp edges of the core and build up bulk`,`Apply the identifying print`],answer:2,explanation:`Subcoating alternates a gum or gelatin binder solution with a dusting powder of calcium carbonate and talc, which rapidly builds weight and converts the sharp bevelled edge into a rounded contour that the later coats can cover evenly. Waterproofing is the earlier sealing stage, gloss comes from polishing with wax, and printing is the last operation of all.`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-11`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Film coated tablets come out with a rough, dull, orange peel surface. The cause and the correct remedy are`,options:[`Spray droplets drying before they can spread and coalesce; thin the coating solution and lower the inlet air temperature`,`Excess plasticiser making the film tacky; reduce the plasticiser`,`Foam in the coating liquid; add an antifoam`,`Colour migration during drying; change to a lake pigment`],answer:0,explanation:`Orange peel, also called roughness, means the droplets arrive too dry or too viscous to flow together into a continuous film, so a less concentrated solution, a lower inlet temperature or reduced atomising pressure will fix it. Foam causes infilling of the engraving rather than surface roughness, and dye migration during drying is a granulation defect that produces mottling, not texture.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-12`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Capsule-shaped tablets come out of a perforated pan coater stuck together in pairs (twinning). The most effective correction is`,options:[`Increase the spray rate so the film sets faster`,`Slow the pan down so the bed is disturbed less`,`Add more plasticiser to the coating solution`,`Reduce the spray rate and increase the drying air volume and pan speed`],answer:3,explanation:`Twinning happens when the bed is too wet, so two flat faces meet while the film is still tacky; spraying less liquid per unit time, drying it away faster and keeping the bed tumbling briskly all shorten the tacky window. Raising the spray rate or slowing the pan does the opposite, and extra plasticiser makes the film softer and more likely to stick, not less.`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-13`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Compared with sugar coating, a conventional film coat is characterised by`,options:[`A weight gain of about 30 to 50 percent and complete loss of the core shape`,`A weight gain of about 2 to 3 percent, retention of the core shape and legible embossing`,`A requirement for a separate polishing stage with beeswax`,`An inability to carry a colourant`],answer:1,explanation:`A film coat is a thin polymer membrane adding only a few percent to the weight, so the tablet keeps its original contour and any engraving stays readable, and the process is a single continuous spraying operation. The 30 to 50 percent weight gain and the loss of the engraved shape belong to sugar coating, which is also the process that needs a separate polishing stage.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-14`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Aqueous film coating in a conventional pear-shaped pan gives wet, sticky tablets. The standard equipment modification is to fit`,options:[`An immersion sword or immersion tube that delivers drying air directly into the tablet bed`,`A larger diameter spray gun`,`Additional baffles only, keeping the same air path`,`A second pan running in series`],answer:0,explanation:`In a plain pan the drying air only sweeps over the surface of the bed, so water accumulates inside it; an immersion sword or tube pushes air through the moving tablets, and the perforated pan achieves the same by drawing air through the bed. A bigger gun delivers more liquid rather than more drying, and baffles improve mixing but do nothing about the air path.`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-15`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A 200 mg tablet core is to be film coated to a 3 percent weight gain using a suspension containing 12 percent w/w solids. The mass of coating suspension required per tablet is`,options:[`50 mg`,`18 mg`,`72 mg`,`25 mg`],answer:0,explanation:`Dry film needed = 3 percent of 200 mg = 6 mg. Since the suspension carries 12 percent solids, the suspension needed = 6 / 0.12 = 50 mg per tablet, of which 44 mg is water that evaporates. The 25 mg distractor arises from using 24 percent solids by doubling, and 72 mg from multiplying by 12 percent instead of dividing.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-tablet-coating-16`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`An enteric coated tablet fails the acid stage of the disintegration test, and dye is seen entering at the bevelled edge. The most probable cause is`,options:[`Too high a plasticiser concentration in the film`,`Inclusion of titanium dioxide in the coating`,`Over-curing of the applied film`,`Too low a coating weight gain, so the edges, where the film is thinnest, are breached`],answer:3,explanation:`Coating deposits least material where the tablet contacts its neighbours and where curvature is greatest, so the edges are always the thinnest and the first place acid penetrates; enteric coats therefore need a substantially larger weight gain, typically 6 to 12 percent, than a cosmetic film coat. Excess plasticiser softens a film but does not create the edge-specific failure, and curing improves rather than damages an aqueous dispersion film.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-9`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which fill is unsuitable for a hard gelatin capsule shell?`,options:[`A dry powder blend`,`Coated pellets`,`A thixotropic semisolid fill`,`An aqueous solution of the drug`],answer:3,explanation:`Water dissolves and distorts the gelatin shell, so aqueous solutions cannot be filled into hard capsules; strongly hygroscopic powders are avoided for the opposite reason, that they draw moisture out of the shell and make it brittle. Powders, granules, pellets, tablets and non-aqueous liquid or semisolid fills are all routine.`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-10`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The bloom strength of a gelatin sample is a measure of its`,options:[`Solution viscosity at 60 degrees C`,`Gel rigidity, that is the force needed to depress the surface of a standard gel`,`Moisture content`,`Isoelectric point`],answer:1,explanation:`Bloom is measured with a gelometer as the force in grams needed to press a plunger a fixed distance into a standard gel, so it grades gel strength; a higher bloom gelatin makes a stiffer shell. Viscosity is a separate specification governing how the dipping solution behaves, and the isoelectric point distinguishes Type A from Type B gelatin.`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-11`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug of 0.5 mg dose gives poor content uniformity in powder blends but dissolves freely in polyethylene glycol 400. The most suitable presentation is`,options:[`A hard gelatin capsule filled with a powder blend`,`A directly compressed tablet`,`A sugar coated tablet`,`A soft gelatin capsule with the drug in solution in the polyethylene glycol`],answer:3,explanation:`Dosing a solution removes the segregation and sampling problem entirely, because the drug is molecularly dispersed and the volume delivered by the rotary die is highly reproducible, which is why soft capsules dominate very low dose and oily products. Any powder based dosage form, whether capsule or tablet, reintroduces the blend uniformity difficulty that caused the failure.`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-12`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which component is added to the gelatin mass of a soft capsule shell as the plasticiser?`,options:[`Magnesium stearate`,`Talc`,`Sodium lauryl sulphate`,`Glycerin or sorbitol`],answer:3,explanation:`Soft capsule shells contain roughly 20 to 30 percent plasticiser, usually glycerin or sorbitol, and it is this that makes them elastic rather than rigid; the plasticiser to gelatin ratio is what distinguishes a soft shell from a hard one. Magnesium stearate and talc are solid lubricants used in powder filling, and sodium lauryl sulphate is a wetting agent.`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-13`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Hard gelatin capsules should be stored at about 15 to 25 degrees C and 35 to 55 percent relative humidity because`,options:[`Below this humidity range the shells absorb water and soften`,`Outside this range the shells become brittle and split when too dry, and soft, tacky and distorted when too humid`,`Higher humidity hardens the shells and slows dissolution`,`Humidity affects only the adhesion of the printing ink`],answer:1,explanation:`A hard shell normally holds 13 to 16 percent water, and that water is its plasticiser; drying it below about 10 percent makes it brittle so it cracks in the blister, while above about 18 percent it softens and the capsules deform and stick together. The claim that low humidity makes shells absorb water and soften has the moisture moving the wrong way, and high humidity softens rather than hardens the shell.`,tag:`Trap`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-14`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Compared with a tamping machine using a dosing disc, a dosator machine`,options:[`Builds the plug by five or six successive light tamps in a hole in a rotating disc`,`Forms the plug in a single compression inside a dosing tube dipped into a powder bed of controlled height`,`Requires no plug formation, so powder flow is irrelevant`,`Can be used only for pellets and not for powders`],answer:1,explanation:`A dosator dips into the powder bed, a piston compresses the powder caught in the tube into one plug, and the plug is ejected into the capsule body, so the bed height and the powder consolidation behaviour are critical variables. Building the plug by several successive tamps in a disc is exactly what the tamping machines such as Bosch GKF do, which is the distinction being tested.`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-15`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A size 1 capsule body has a volume of 0.50 mL. For a granulation of tapped density 0.62 g/mL, the maximum fill weight is about`,options:[`310 mg`,`806 mg`,`124 mg`,`500 mg`],answer:0,explanation:`Fill weight = volume x tapped density = 0.50 mL x 0.62 g per mL = 0.31 g, that is 310 mg. The 806 mg figure comes from dividing density by volume instead of multiplying, and 500 mg from reading the volume in millilitres as a mass in milligrams. Tapped rather than bulk density is used because the machine consolidates the powder into a plug.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-capsules-16`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The liquid fill of a soft gelatin capsule is normally kept within a pH of about 2.5 to 7.5 because`,options:[`Outside this range the drug will not dissolve`,`A lower pH hydrolyses the gelatin so the shell weakens and leaks, while a higher pH tans the gelatin and impairs shell dissolution`,`The rotary die seals only within this pH range`,`Preservatives are inactive outside this range`],answer:1,explanation:`Strongly acidic fills hydrolyse the peptide chains and the shell loses strength and eventually leaks, whereas alkaline fills promote crosslinking or tanning of the gelatin, giving an insoluble shell that fails dissolution on stability. Aldehydes and reducing sugars in the fill cause the same crosslinking, which is why they are excluded regardless of pH.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-9`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Water for Injection IP is produced by`,options:[`Distillation of potable water in a still with an efficient baffle system`,`Ion exchange through mixed bed resins`,`Filtration of purified water through a 0.22 micrometre membrane`,`Boiling followed by cooling in a covered vessel`],answer:0,explanation:`The IP requires Water for Injection to be prepared by distillation, and the still carries baffles or a vapour separator so that droplets carrying dissolved endotoxin cannot be entrained into the condensate. Ion exchange removes ions but not pyrogens or organisms, and a sterilising filter retains bacteria while dissolved endotoxin passes straight through.`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-10`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The environment required at the point of fill for an aseptically filled injection is`,options:[`Grade A unidirectional airflow, ISO 5, within a Grade B background`,`Grade C, ISO 7`,`Grade D, ISO 8`,`A general manufacturing area with terminal fumigation`],answer:0,explanation:`Open product, exposed containers and stoppers must be under Grade A unidirectional airflow, and for aseptic processing the surrounding room must be Grade B so that any excursion still meets a high standard. Grade C and Grade D are background zones for preparation, component washing and the filling of products that will be terminally sterilised.`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-11`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A protein solution loses activity above 40 degrees C and is to be filled into vials. The appropriate sterilisation route is`,options:[`Dry heat at 160 degrees C for 2 hours`,`Autoclaving at 121 degrees C for 15 minutes`,`Filtration through a 0.22 micrometre membrane followed by aseptic filling`,`Ethylene oxide gas treatment of the sealed vials`],answer:2,explanation:`When the product cannot survive any terminal heat cycle, the only route left is to remove organisms physically by a validated sterilising grade filter and then fill under Grade A conditions, accepting the higher sterility assurance burden of aseptic processing. Both heat routes would denature the protein, and ethylene oxide cannot penetrate a sealed vial of liquid and leaves toxic residues.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-12`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A batch is suspected of containing a pyrogenic substance that is not a Gram-negative endotoxin. Which test will detect it?`,options:[`The gel clot limulus amoebocyte lysate test`,`The chromogenic limulus amoebocyte lysate test`,`The rabbit pyrogen test`,`The sterility test by membrane filtration`],answer:2,explanation:`All limulus amoebocyte lysate formats, gel clot, turbidimetric and chromogenic alike, respond only to lipopolysaccharide, so a fungal or chemical pyrogen or a Gram-positive product would pass them. The rabbit test measures the biological end point, a rise in body temperature, and therefore detects any fever-producing substance, which is why it is retained despite being slower and requiring animals.`,tag:`Trap`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-13`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which antioxidant is appropriate for an aqueous injection of a readily oxidised water-soluble drug?`,options:[`Butylated hydroxyanisole`,`Butylated hydroxytoluene`,`Alpha tocopherol`,`Sodium metabisulphite`],answer:3,explanation:`Oxidation of a dissolved drug happens in the aqueous phase, so the antioxidant must be water soluble and be present where the reaction occurs; sodium metabisulphite and sodium formaldehyde sulphoxylate are the standard choices, usually with disodium edetate to chelate catalytic trace metals. Butylated hydroxyanisole, butylated hydroxytoluene and alpha tocopherol are oil soluble and are used to protect fats and oily injections.`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-14`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The membrane filtration method is preferred to direct inoculation for the sterility testing of an antibiotic injection because`,options:[`It gives a result within 24 hours`,`It washes antimicrobial residues off the retained organisms and allows a much larger volume to be tested`,`It needs no incubation of media`,`It uses only one culture medium instead of two`],answer:1,explanation:`Any preservative or antibiotic carried into the medium would inhibit the very organisms the test is meant to reveal; filtration traps organisms on the membrane, which is then rinsed with a diluting fluid before being transferred to media, and the whole content of many containers can be passed through. Both methods still require incubation for 14 days in fluid thioglycollate and soyabean casein digest media.`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-15`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A 1 percent w/v solution of a drug depresses the freezing point by 0.12 degrees C. Taking 1 percent w/v sodium chloride as depressing it by 0.58 degrees C, the sodium chloride needed per 100 mL to make the solution isotonic is`,options:[`0.69 g`,`0.90 g`,`0.52 g`,`0.21 g`],answer:0,explanation:`Isotonicity requires a total depression of 0.52 degrees C; the drug already provides 0.12, leaving 0.40 to be supplied. Sodium chloride needed = 0.40 / 0.58 = 0.69 g per 100 mL. Using the full 0.52 without subtracting the drug contribution gives the 0.90 g answer, which is the tempting error.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-parenterals-16`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A terminal sterilisation cycle holds the product at 115 degrees C for 30 minutes. Taking z as 10 degrees C, the F0 delivered by the hold period is about`,options:[`7.5 minutes`,`30 minutes`,`12 minutes`,`3.0 minutes`],answer:0,explanation:`F0 = t x 10 raised to (T minus 121)/10 = 30 x 10 raised to minus 0.6 = 30 x 0.251 = 7.5 minutes. Since a terminal cycle normally needs an F0 of at least 8 to 12 minutes, this hold is inadequate and must be lengthened or run hotter. Quoting 30 minutes ignores that F0 is always expressed as the equivalent time at 121 degrees C, not the clock time.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-9`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which additive is included in an eye drop specifically to prolong the contact time of the drug with the cornea?`,options:[`Sodium chloride`,`Hydroxypropyl methylcellulose`,`Benzalkonium chloride`,`Disodium edetate`],answer:1,explanation:`Cellulose derivatives such as hydroxypropyl methylcellulose and methylcellulose, and polyvinyl alcohol, raise the viscosity of the drop so it drains more slowly from the cul-de-sac and more drug has time to permeate. Sodium chloride adjusts tonicity, benzalkonium chloride preserves the product, and disodium edetate is a chelating adjunct to the preservative.`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-10`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Ophthalmic ointments are subject to an official test that other ointments are not, namely a test for`,options:[`Metal particle contamination`,`Friability`,`Weight variation of the tube`,`Disintegration time`],answer:0,explanation:`Because a metal fragment could abrade the cornea, ten tubes are emptied, the base melted and the residue examined under a microscope for particles of 50 micrometres or larger in any dimension. Friability and disintegration are solid dosage form tests and have no meaning for a semisolid.`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-11`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Eye drops of a thermolabile drug are to be supplied in low density polyethylene dropper bottles. The correct sterilisation strategy is`,options:[`Autoclave the filled and sealed bottles at 121 degrees C for 15 minutes`,`Dry heat the filled bottles at 160 degrees C for 2 hours`,`Filter the solution through a 0.22 micrometre membrane and fill aseptically into pre-sterilised bottles`,`Add a double concentration of preservative in place of sterilisation`],answer:2,explanation:`Both the drug and the polyethylene container rule out terminal heat, so the solution is sterilised by filtration and filled aseptically into bottles that have themselves been sterilised beforehand by ethylene oxide or gamma radiation. A preservative controls organisms introduced during use and is never a substitute for sterilising the product.`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-12`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug is most stable at pH 4.5 but the eye is most comfortable near pH 7.4. The accepted formulation approach is to`,options:[`Buffer strongly at pH 7.4 and accept the loss on stability`,`Use a high capacity buffer at pH 4.5 to hold the drop acidic in the eye`,`Use a buffer at the stability pH but of deliberately low buffer capacity, so the tears rapidly restore the drop to physiological pH`,`Leave the product unbuffered and adjust the pH at the time of use`],answer:2,explanation:`Stability governs the pH chosen, and comfort is protected by keeping the buffer capacity low so that the small instilled volume is neutralised within seconds by the buffering power of the tear fluid. A high capacity buffer at pH 4.5 would resist that neutralisation and cause stinging and reflex lacrimation, which washes the dose away.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-13`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The principal limitation of chlorobutanol as an ophthalmic preservative is that it`,options:[`Is inactivated by anionic drugs such as sodium fluorescein`,`Is volatile, hydrolyses in solutions above about pH 5.5, and is lost by sorption into polyethylene containers`,`Is active against fungi but not against bacteria`,`Cannot be used together with disodium edetate`],answer:1,explanation:`Chlorobutanol is a volatile chlorinated alcohol that decomposes to hydrochloric acid as the pH rises, so the product loses both preservative and pH, and it partitions into plastic dropper bottles during storage. Inactivation by anionic drugs is the weakness of the cationic benzalkonium chloride, which is precisely why chlorobutanol is chosen for anionic products despite these drawbacks.`,tag:`Trap`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-14`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`An in situ gelling ophthalmic system based on gellan gum (Gelrite) is triggered to gel by`,options:[`The rise in temperature to about 34 degrees C at the ocular surface`,`The rise in pH to 7.4 on contact with tears`,`Simple dilution of the polymer by the tear volume`,`The cations present in the tear fluid`],answer:3,explanation:`Gellan gum is an anionic polysaccharide that is crosslinked by the sodium and calcium ions of tear fluid, so a low viscosity drop becomes a gel the moment it reaches the eye. Temperature triggered gelling belongs to poloxamer systems and pH triggered gelling to cellulose acetate phthalate latex, so the trigger is the discriminating point.`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-15`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`By the White-Vincent method, the volume of water in which 0.3 g of a drug of sodium chloride equivalent 0.20 must be dissolved to give an isotonic solution is`,options:[`6.67 mL`,`33.3 mL`,`3.33 mL`,`66.7 mL`],answer:0,explanation:`V = w x E x 111.1 = 0.3 x 0.20 x 111.1 = 6.67 mL. The constant 111.1 is the volume of isotonic solution produced by 1 g of sodium chloride, since 0.9 g makes 100 mL. The solution is made isotonic in that small volume first and is then brought to the final volume with an isotonic vehicle rather than with water.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-ophthalmics-16`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The cornea behaves as a lipid-water-lipid sandwich, so the drug that permeates it best is one that is`,options:[`Strongly hydrophilic and fully ionised at tear pH`,`Extremely lipophilic with negligible aqueous solubility`,`Of intermediate lipophilicity, with appreciable solubility in both water and lipid`,`Of high molecular weight and highly protein bound`],answer:2,explanation:`The lipoidal epithelium and endothelium admit unionised lipophilic molecules while the hydrated stroma between them admits water soluble ones, so a drug must be biphasic to cross all three layers. A very hydrophilic drug is halted at the epithelium and a very lipophilic one becomes trapped in it and cannot enter the stroma, which is why prodrugs such as dipivefrine are designed to sit in the middle of the range.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-9`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Wool fat (anhydrous lanolin) belongs to which class of ointment base?`,options:[`Oleaginous`,`Absorption`,`Water-removable`,`Water-soluble`],answer:1,explanation:`An absorption base is anhydrous but contains a water-in-oil emulsifier, so it takes up aqueous solutions and forms a water in oil system; wool fat and hydrophilic petrolatum are the standard examples. An oleaginous base such as white soft paraffin absorbs almost no water, and a water-removable base is already an oil in water cream.`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-10`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A suppository mould lubricant must be immiscible with the base. For a glycerinated gelatin base the correct lubricant is`,options:[`Soft soap in glycerin solution`,`Warm water`,`Propylene glycol`,`Liquid paraffin or arachis oil`],answer:3,explanation:`Glycerinated gelatin is water miscible, so an oily lubricant such as liquid paraffin is used; the rule reverses for a fatty base like theobroma oil, where a hydrophilic soap and glycerin film is applied. Water and propylene glycol would dissolve into the gelatin base and give no release film at all.`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-11`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`An oil phase requires an HLB of 12. Using Span 80 of HLB 4.3 and Tween 80 of HLB 15.0, the percentage of Tween 80 in the emulsifier blend is about`,options:[`72 percent`,`28 percent`,`55 percent`,`64 percent`],answer:0,explanation:`Let x be the fraction of Tween 80: 15.0x + 4.3(1 minus x) = 12, so 10.7x = 7.7 and x = 0.72, that is 72 percent Tween 80 with 28 percent Span 80. The 28 percent answer is the Span fraction, which is the commonest slip; check the result by noting that the required HLB of 12 lies much nearer 15.0 than 4.3, so the high HLB surfactant must predominate.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-12`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A nonionic oil in water cream separates an aqueous layer after storage in a warm climate. The most likely mechanism is`,options:[`Syneresis of a gel network`,`A polymorphic change in the wax`,`Ostwald ripening of the suspended drug`,`Coalescence following inversion as the storage temperature approaches the phase inversion temperature of the nonionic surfactant`],answer:3,explanation:`The polyoxyethylene chains of a nonionic surfactant dehydrate as temperature rises, so the emulsifier becomes progressively more lipophilic and at the phase inversion temperature the emulsion turns over and coalesces, which shows up as free water on cooling. Formulating so that the phase inversion temperature is at least 20 to 40 degrees C above the storage temperature is the standard preventive measure; syneresis is a gel phenomenon and needs no emulsifier at all.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-13`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A cream is to carry a cationic quaternary ammonium antiseptic. Which emulsifying wax must be avoided?`,options:[`Cetomacrogol emulsifying wax`,`Cetrimide emulsifying wax`,`Emulsifying wax based on sodium lauryl sulphate`,`Wool alcohols`],answer:2,explanation:`Sodium lauryl sulphate is anionic and forms an insoluble, inactive complex with a cationic drug, so both the emulsion and the antiseptic activity are lost. Cetrimide emulsifying wax is itself cationic and therefore compatible, cetomacrogol wax is nonionic and compatible with either charge, and wool alcohols are nonionic water in oil emulsifiers.`,tag:`Trap`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-14`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`After mixing, an ointment containing a suspended drug is passed through which equipment to reduce agglomerates and give a uniform, grit-free product?`,options:[`A planetary mixer run at higher speed`,`A triple roller mill`,`A ribbon blender`,`A vibratory sieve`],answer:1,explanation:`A triple roller mill shears the semisolid through the narrow gaps between three rollers turning at different speeds, breaking down agglomerates; a colloid mill does the same job for more fluid systems. A planetary mixer and a ribbon blender distribute material but exert far too little shear to break agglomerates, and a sieve cannot be used on a semisolid at all.`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-15`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Twenty suppositories of 2 g mould calibration are to contain 20 percent w/w zinc oxide, whose displacement value is 4.7. The weight of base required is about`,options:[`38.3 g`,`32.0 g`,`40.0 g`,`36.6 g`],answer:0,explanation:`Drug per suppository = 20 percent of 2 g = 0.4 g, so total drug = 20 x 0.4 = 8 g. Base displaced = 8 / 4.7 = 1.70 g. Base required = (20 x 2) minus 1.70 = 40 minus 1.70 = 38.3 g. The 32.0 g figure comes from subtracting the whole 8 g of drug rather than the volume equivalent it displaces, which is the usual error.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-semisolids-16`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A drug that is freely soluble in fats is to be given rectally for a rapid local effect. The rational base choice is`,options:[`Theobroma oil, because the drug dissolves readily in it`,`A polyethylene glycol base, because the drug partitions out of an aqueous base far more readily than out of a fatty one`,`A hard fat such as Witepsol, because it has a sharp melting point`,`Glycerinated gelatin, because it melts at body temperature`],answer:1,explanation:`Release depends on the drug leaving the base for the rectal fluid, so an oil soluble drug held in a fatty base has a very unfavourable partition coefficient and is released slowly; putting it in a water soluble polyethylene glycol base reverses that and speeds release. Solubility in the base is therefore an argument against that base, not for it, which is the trap in the suggestion to use theobroma oil because the drug dissolves readily in it. Glycerinated gelatin does not melt at body temperature either; it dissolves slowly in the rectal fluid.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-9`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A hydrophobic powder floats on the vehicle when a suspension is being made. Which additive corrects this by reducing the contact angle?`,options:[`Sodium carboxymethylcellulose`,`Polysorbate 80`,`Sodium benzoate`,`Sucrose`],answer:1,explanation:`A surfactant such as polysorbate 80 adsorbs at the solid-liquid interface and lowers the contact angle so the vehicle displaces the adsorbed air and wets the powder; glycerin and propylene glycol act as levigating agents in the same role. Sodium carboxymethylcellulose is a suspending agent that thickens the vehicle, sodium benzoate is a preservative, and sucrose is a sweetener.`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-10`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which suspending agent is a natural gum rather than a semisynthetic or synthetic polymer?`,options:[`Methylcellulose`,`Sodium carboxymethylcellulose`,`Tragacanth`,`Carbomer`],answer:2,explanation:`Tragacanth, along with acacia, guar and sodium alginate, is obtained directly from a plant and so is a natural suspending agent. Methylcellulose and sodium carboxymethylcellulose are cellulose derivatives, that is semisynthetic, and carbomer is a wholly synthetic acrylic acid polymer.`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-11`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A paediatric antibiotic hydrolyses rapidly in aqueous solution but must be given as a liquid. The correct presentation is`,options:[`An elixir containing 15 percent alcohol`,`A syrup preserved with methylparaben`,`A dry powder for reconstitution, made up at the time of dispensing and given a short in-use shelf life`,`An oil in water emulsion`],answer:2,explanation:`If water itself is the reactant, no preservative, cosolvent or emulsifier will help; the drug is packed as a dry granulated powder with the sweetener, suspending agent and preservative already included, and water is added only at dispensing, after which the product lasts about one to two weeks under refrigeration. Alcohol in an elixir does not stop hydrolysis and is in any case avoided in paediatric products.`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-12`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Methylparaben and propylparaben are used together in an oral liquid because`,options:[`One is antibacterial and the other antifungal only`,`The more water soluble methyl ester and the more oil soluble propyl ester partition into both phases and act synergistically, widening the spectrum`,`The combination raises the pH of the product`,`Each alone is inactivated by sucrose`],answer:1,explanation:`Preservative action depends on the concentration in the aqueous phase where organisms grow and on cover for the oily phase where they can shelter, so the pair gives a higher total effective concentration than either could alone at its solubility limit. Both esters are active against bacteria and fungi, and both are inactivated by nonionic surfactants such as polysorbates and hydrolyse above about pH 7.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-13`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A sucrose based syrup develops a crystalline deposit after storage in a cold store. The cause and remedy are`,options:[`Microbial growth; add a preservative`,`Hydrolysis of sucrose to invert sugar; buffer the syrup to pH 8`,`Sucrose crystallising out because its solubility falls as the temperature drops; include sorbitol or glycerol as a crystallisation inhibitor`,`Precipitation of the preservative; increase the alcohol content`],answer:2,explanation:`Simple syrup is close to saturation at room temperature, so cooling pushes it past saturation and sucrose crystallises, and the crystals then seed further growth; a polyol such as sorbitol or glycerol lowers the effective saturation and keeps the syrup clear. Inversion to glucose and fructose is favoured by acid rather than alkali and in fact reduces the tendency to crystallise.`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-14`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`An oil in water emulsion cracks after a large volume of calcium chloride solution is added. The emulsifier was most probably`,options:[`A nonionic polysorbate`,`Acacia`,`Methylcellulose`,`A monovalent alkali metal soap, converted to the insoluble divalent calcium soap`],answer:3,explanation:`Monovalent soaps such as sodium stearate favour oil in water emulsions, but calcium ions replace the sodium to give a calcium soap whose bulky lipophilic portion favours water in oil, so the emulsion inverts and then cracks. Polysorbates, acacia and methylcellulose are nonionic or only weakly ionic and tolerate divalent cations far better, though very high electrolyte concentrations can salt out any hydrophilic colloid.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-15`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`To prepare 500 mL of a 28 percent w/v sorbitol solution from a 70 percent w/v stock, the volumes of stock and of water required are`,options:[`200 mL of stock and 300 mL of water`,`140 mL of stock and 360 mL of water`,`250 mL of stock and 250 mL of water`,`350 mL of stock and 150 mL of water`],answer:0,explanation:`By the dilution relation C1V1 = C2V2, V1 = (28 x 500) / 70 = 200 mL of stock, made up to 500 mL, so 300 mL of water is added. The 140 mL answer comes from taking 28 percent of 500 mL as though it were the stock volume, which ignores the strength of the stock altogether.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-liquid-orals-16`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Particles of 10 micrometres diameter and density 2.0 g/cm3 settle in a vehicle of density 1.0 g/cm3 and viscosity 0.01 poise. Taking g as 981 cm/s2, the Stokes sedimentation velocity is about`,options:[`5.45 x 10^-3 cm/s`,`5.45 x 10^-2 cm/s`,`9.81 x 10^-4 cm/s`,`1.09 x 10^-2 cm/s`],answer:0,explanation:`v = d2 (rho s minus rho l) g / 18 eta. With d = 10 micrometres = 1 x 10^-3 cm, d2 = 1 x 10^-6 cm2, so v = (1 x 10^-6 x 1.0 x 981) / (18 x 0.01) = 9.81 x 10^-4 / 0.18 = 5.45 x 10^-3 cm/s. Raising the viscosity tenfold to 0.1 poise would cut this to 5.45 x 10^-4 cm/s, which is exactly why a suspending agent is added.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-9`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which component of a pressurised aerosol package determines the spray pattern and the droplet size of the emitted dose?`,options:[`The dip tube`,`The ferrule`,`The actuator and its orifice`,`The mounting cup gasket`],answer:2,explanation:`The actuator carries the expansion chamber and the final orifice, and its bore and geometry decide whether the output is a fine mist, a wet stream or a foam. The dip tube merely carries product up from the bottom of the can, while the ferrule and gasket are sealing components of the valve assembly.`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-10`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A nebuliser rather than a metered dose inhaler is preferred when`,options:[`A very small, precisely metered dose is required`,`The patient is in acute severe distress, or is very young or unconscious and cannot coordinate an actuation with inhalation`,`Portability is the overriding requirement`,`The drug is available only as a dry powder blend`],answer:1,explanation:`A nebuliser delivers drug continuously in tidal breathing over 10 to 20 minutes, so no coordination, breath holding or inspiratory effort is needed, which suits infants, the unconscious and patients in an acute attack. Its drawbacks are exactly the opposite of the other options: it is bulky, not portable, and its delivered dose is far less precisely metered than that of an inhaler.`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-11`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In a three-phase aerosol system, in which the product concentrate and the liquefied propellant form two separate liquid layers with vapour above,`,options:[`The container must be shaken before each use so the correct proportions are discharged`,`The system delivers a dry powder`,`The pressure rises steadily as the container empties`,`No dip tube is needed`],answer:0,explanation:`Because the aqueous concentrate and the propellant are immiscible and separate on standing, the dip tube would otherwise pick up whichever layer happens to sit at its inlet, so shaking to form a temporary emulsion is essential for a consistent discharge. The pressure stays constant while any liquefied propellant remains, since vapour lost from the headspace is replenished by evaporation.`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-12`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A blend contains 60 mole percent of a propellant of vapour pressure 85 psia and 40 mole percent of one of vapour pressure 13.4 psia. Assuming ideal behaviour, the vapour pressure of the blend is about`,options:[`56.4 psia`,`49.2 psia`,`98.4 psia`,`35.7 psia`],answer:0,explanation:`By Raoult law the partial pressures add: (0.60 x 85) + (0.40 x 13.4) = 51.0 + 5.4 = 56.4 psia. The 98.4 answer comes from adding the two vapour pressures without weighting them by mole fraction, and 49.2 from taking a simple arithmetic mean. Blending a high and a low pressure propellant in this way is how a formulator tunes the spray to the desired pressure.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-13`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Cold filling cannot be used for an aqueous aerosol product because`,options:[`The valve cannot be crimped at low temperature`,`Water dissolves the propellant completely`,`The propellant boils away instantly at that temperature`,`The process requires the concentrate to be chilled to about minus 30 to minus 40 degrees C, at which water freezes`],answer:3,explanation:`In cold filling both the concentrate and the liquefied propellant are chilled below the propellant boiling point so they can be metered as liquids into an open can, and an aqueous concentrate simply turns to ice at that temperature. Pressure filling is therefore used for aqueous and heat sensitive products, the propellant being forced through the crimped valve at room temperature.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-14`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The flame projection (flame extension) test on an aerosol product measures`,options:[`The flammability of the emitted spray`,`The rate of leakage from the valve`,`The moisture content of the propellant`,`The delivered dose uniformity`],answer:0,explanation:`The spray is discharged for a few seconds into an open flame and the extension of the flame, or any flashback to the container, is recorded, which grades the fire hazard of hydrocarbon and alcohol containing formulations. Leakage is assessed separately by weight loss over time, and delivered dose uniformity is measured by collecting actuations in a dose sampling apparatus.`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-15`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A suspension metered dose inhaler shows drifting delivered dose and visible caking of the drug in the canister. The most useful formulation change is to`,options:[`Raise the vapour pressure by using a more volatile propellant`,`Add a surfactant such as sorbitan trioleate, oleic acid or lecithin to disperse the micronised drug and lubricate the valve`,`Reduce the metering volume of the valve`,`Add coarse lactose as a carrier for the drug`],answer:1,explanation:`Micronised drug in a low viscosity propellant aggregates and cakes, so a small amount of surfactant adsorbs on the particles, keeps the suspension loosely flocculated and easily redispersed on shaking, and also lubricates the metering valve; matching the drug and propellant densities and excluding moisture help further. Reducing the metering volume simply lowers the dose, and a lactose carrier belongs to dry powder inhalers, not to a pressurised suspension.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-aerosols-16`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The mass median aerodynamic diameter of an inhalation aerosol is best defined as the diameter`,options:[`At which half of the particles by number are larger and half smaller`,`Of the largest particle capable of reaching the alveoli`,`Measured microscopically as the mean geometric diameter of the drug crystals`,`Above which half of the aerosol mass lies, expressed as the diameter of a unit density sphere with the same settling velocity`],answer:3,explanation:`Aerodynamic diameter normalises for particle density and shape by referring every particle to a sphere of unit density that settles at the same rate, and the mass median value splits the total mass into two equal halves, which is what governs where the aerosol deposits. A count median diameter weights every particle equally and is dominated by the numerous fine particles that carry almost none of the dose, and a microscopic diameter takes no account of density.`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-9`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A light-resistant container for a photolabile injection is normally made of`,options:[`Flint (colourless) glass`,`Amber glass, coloured by iron oxide`,`Polystyrene`,`Type NP soda-lime glass`],answer:1,explanation:`Amber glass owes its colour to iron and titanium oxides, which absorb ultraviolet and short wavelength visible light below roughly 470 nm, the region that drives most photodegradation. Flint glass transmits that light freely, and if a colourless container has to be used the product must be protected by an opaque secondary carton.`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-10`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A strip pack differs from a blister pack in that a strip pack`,options:[`Is made by sealing two flat webs of film around each unit, with no thermoformed cavity`,`Always uses a rigid PVC base web`,`Cannot be used for tablets`,`Requires a separate desiccant sachet`],answer:0,explanation:`In a strip pack the product is fed between two flat films that are heat sealed all round it, so no pocket is formed and the pack conforms to the shape of the unit. A blister pack, by contrast, thermoforms or cold forms a cavity in the base web and closes it with a lidding foil, which is why the product is visible in a blister but not in a typical foil strip.`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-11`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`An effervescent tablet is extremely hygroscopic. The most appropriate unit-dose pack is`,options:[`A plain PVC blister with aluminium lidding`,`A PVC/PVDC blister with aluminium lidding`,`A cold formed aluminium-aluminium blister`,`An amber glass bottle with a cotton wool plug`],answer:2,explanation:`Aluminium foil is effectively an absolute barrier to water vapour, so a cold formed aluminium base web with aluminium lidding gives the highest protection available in a blister and is the standard for effervescent and highly moisture sensitive products. Plain PVC is a poor moisture barrier and a PVDC coating only improves it; the cotton plug in a bottle actually holds moisture against the tablets.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-12`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Type III glass is intended for`,options:[`Aqueous alkaline injections`,`Non-parenteral products, and non-aqueous parenteral preparations and dry powders for injection`,`Ophthalmic solutions demanding the highest hydrolytic resistance`,`Any injection whatsoever`],answer:1,explanation:`Type III is ordinary soda-lime glass with no surface treatment, so it yields alkali to an aqueous product and is limited to non-aqueous parenterals, dry powders that are reconstituted immediately before use, and oral or topical products. Aqueous and especially alkaline injections demand Type I borosilicate, whose resistance runs through the whole thickness of the glass.`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-13`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A preserved solution loses potency of its preservative in a polyethylene bottle although nothing appears in the solution from the plastic. This phenomenon is`,options:[`Permeation`,`Leaching`,`Sorption`,`Chemical reaction with the polymer`],answer:2,explanation:`Sorption is the movement of drug or preservative from the solution into the container wall, and the classic examples are chlorobutanol and benzalkonium chloride disappearing into polyethylene and insulin or glyceryl trinitrate binding to plastic infusion sets. Permeation is the passage of vapour or gas through the wall, and leaching is the opposite direction of travel, a plasticiser or stabiliser migrating out of the plastic into the product.`,tag:`Trap`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-14`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Blow-fill-seal technology is used for small volume parenterals and eye drops because`,options:[`The container is extruded, formed, filled and sealed in one continuous automated sequence, so human intervention is almost eliminated`,`It allows the filled container to be terminally sterilised by dry heat`,`It produces glass containers more cheaply than moulding`,`It removes the need for any environmental monitoring`],answer:0,explanation:`The polymer is extruded as a hot parison, blown into the mould, filled through the still open neck and sealed immediately, so the container is at no stage exposed to the room and the operator never touches it, giving very high sterility assurance for an aseptic process. It makes plastic, not glass, containers, and monitoring of the critical zone is still required.`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-15`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A product in a thermoformed PVC blister fails on stability, and the measured water vapour transmission rate of the pack is ten times the specification although the film supplied met specification. The most likely root cause is`,options:[`The sealing temperature was set too low`,`The lidding foil was printed with the wrong ink`,`Too much desiccant was included in the carton`,`The film was drawn too deeply during forming, so the cavity walls and corners are much thinner than the flat web`],answer:3,explanation:`Permeation is inversely proportional to wall thickness, and thermoforming stretches the film most at the deepest points and the corners, so an aggressive draw ratio or too hot a forming plate can thin those regions to a fraction of the original gauge and the pack fails even though the incoming film was correct. A low seal temperature would show as open or weak seals rather than as uniform vapour ingress.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pt-packaging-16`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The classical leachable of toxicological concern from a flexible PVC infusion bag, and the usual way of avoiding it, are`,options:[`Di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate plasticiser, avoided by using non-PVC polyolefin bags or an alternative plasticiser`,`Titanium dioxide opacifier, avoided by using clear film`,`Calcium stearate lubricant, avoided by extruding at a lower temperature`,`Antimony catalyst residue, avoided by annealing the container`],answer:0,explanation:`Rigid PVC is made flexible by adding a large proportion of DEHP, which is not chemically bound and migrates readily into lipid containing fluids such as blood, lipid emulsions and taxane solutions, so multilayer polyolefin bags or non-phthalate plasticisers are used where this matters. Antimony is a genuine residue but it belongs to PET rather than to plasticised PVC, and titanium dioxide and calcium stearate are not the recognised concern here.`}],n=[{id:`f-pt-preformulation-1`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,front:`BCS Class II: solubility, permeability and rate limiting step?`,back:`Low solubility, high permeability; dissolution is rate limiting.`},{id:`f-pt-preformulation-2`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,front:`Difference between a polymorph and a pseudopolymorph?`,back:`Polymorphs have the same composition but different lattices; pseudopolymorphs are solvates and hydrates, so composition differs.`},{id:`f-pt-preformulation-3`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,front:`Elder equation for critical relative humidity of a mixture?`,back:`CRH(AB) = CRH(A) x CRH(B) / 100. The mixture value is always lower than either component.`},{id:`f-pt-preformulation-4`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,front:`Optimum log P range for oral absorption?`,back:`About 1 to 3 (n-octanol/water).`},{id:`f-pt-preformulation-5`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,front:`Which polymorph of chloramphenicol palmitate is active?`,back:`The metastable form B; the stable form A is essentially inactive.`},{id:`f-pt-preformulation-6`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,front:`Intrinsic dissolution rate that predicts absorption problems?`,back:`Below 0.1 mg per minute per cm2. Above 1 mg min-1 cm-2 dissolution will not limit absorption.`},{id:`f-pt-tablets-1`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,front:`Lubricant vs glidant vs antiadherent?`,back:`Lubricant cuts die wall friction on ejection; glidant improves powder flow; antiadherent stops sticking to punch faces.`},{id:`f-pt-tablets-2`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,front:`Name three superdisintegrants and their usual levels.`,back:`Sodium starch glycolate 2 to 8 percent, croscarmellose sodium 0.5 to 5 percent, crospovidone 2 to 5 percent.`},{id:`f-pt-tablets-3`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,front:`Friability test conditions and limit?`,back:`Roche friabilator, 100 revolutions at 25 rpm (4 minutes); weight loss not more than 1.0 percent.`},{id:`f-pt-tablets-4`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,front:`IP weight variation limits for tablets?`,back:`10 percent up to 80 mg; 7.5 percent above 80 and below 250 mg; 5 percent at 250 mg and above.`},{id:`f-pt-tablets-5`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,front:`IP disintegration limits: uncoated, film coated, sugar coated, dispersible?`,back:`15 min, 30 min, 60 min and 3 min respectively.`},{id:`f-pt-tablets-6`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,front:`Capping versus lamination?`,back:`Capping is separation of the top or bottom crown; lamination is splitting into two or more horizontal layers.`},{id:`f-pt-tablet-coating-1`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,front:`Stages of sugar coating in order?`,back:`Sealing, subcoating, smoothing (grossing), colouring, polishing, printing.`},{id:`f-pt-tablet-coating-2`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,front:`Name four enteric coating polymers.`,back:`Cellulose acetate phthalate, HPMC phthalate, polyvinyl acetate phthalate and Eudragit L or S grades.`},{id:`f-pt-tablet-coating-3`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,front:`Dissolution pH of Eudragit L100-55, L100 and S100?`,back:`Above pH 5.5, above pH 6.0 and above pH 7.0 respectively.`},{id:`f-pt-tablet-coating-4`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,front:`Four components of a film coating formulation?`,back:`Polymer, plasticiser, colourant or opacifier, and solvent or vehicle.`},{id:`f-pt-tablet-coating-5`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,front:`Cause of bridging of the tablet logo?`,back:`Film shrinkage and poor adhesion from too little plasticiser; the film lifts out of the engraving.`},{id:`f-pt-tablet-coating-6`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,front:`Which coater sprays from the bottom into a fluidised bed?`,back:`The Wurster fluidised bed coater, the standard for pellets.`},{id:`f-pt-capsules-1`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,front:`Largest and smallest human hard capsule sizes?`,back:`Largest 000 (about 1.37 mL), smallest 5 (about 0.13 mL).`},{id:`f-pt-capsules-2`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,front:`Gelatin Type A versus Type B?`,back:`Type A is acid hydrolysed, usually pork skin, IEP pH 7 to 9. Type B is alkali (lime) hydrolysed, usually bone, IEP pH 4.7 to 5.2.`},{id:`f-pt-capsules-3`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,front:`Which process makes soft gelatin capsules commercially?`,back:`The rotary die process of Scherer, which forms, fills and seals in one continuous step.`},{id:`f-pt-capsules-4`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,front:`Two dosing principles of automatic hard capsule fillers?`,back:`Dosator (dosing tube) machines and tamping machines with a dosing disc.`},{id:`f-pt-capsules-5`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,front:`Disintegration time limit for hard and soft gelatin capsules?`,back:`Within 30 minutes in water at 37 degrees C.`},{id:`f-pt-capsules-6`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,front:`What is gelatin crosslinking and how is it handled in dissolution testing?`,back:`An insoluble pellicle formed by aldehydes, heat and humidity; the test is repeated with pepsin or pancreatin added to the medium.`},{id:`f-pt-parenterals-1`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,front:`Which oils are permitted as parenteral vehicles, and which is banned?`,back:`Fixed vegetable oils such as arachis, sesame, cottonseed and castor are permitted; mineral oil is never permitted.`},{id:`f-pt-parenterals-2`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,front:`What are endotoxins chemically, and which part is pyrogenic?`,back:`Lipopolysaccharides from the Gram-negative cell wall; the lipid A portion is the pyrogenic moiety.`},{id:`f-pt-parenterals-3`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,front:`Depyrogenation conditions for glassware?`,back:`Dry heat at 250 degrees C for 30 minutes. Autoclaving does not destroy endotoxin.`},{id:`f-pt-parenterals-4`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,front:`LAL test versus rabbit test: key difference?`,back:`LAL detects Gram-negative endotoxin only, in vitro and in under an hour; the rabbit test detects all pyrogens but needs animals and about 3 hours.`},{id:`f-pt-parenterals-5`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,front:`Endotoxin limit formula and the value of K?`,back:`Limit = K / M. K is 5 EU per kg for most routes and 0.2 EU per kg intrathecally; M is the maximum dose per kg per hour.`},{id:`f-pt-parenterals-6`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,front:`Three drying stages of lyophilisation and what each removes?`,back:`Freezing immobilises the solute; primary drying sublimes the frozen free water; secondary drying desorbs bound water to 0.5 to 3 percent residual moisture.`},{id:`f-pt-ophthalmics-1`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,front:`Standard ophthalmic preservative and its concentration?`,back:`Benzalkonium chloride, about 0.01 percent, usually with disodium edetate.`},{id:`f-pt-ophthalmics-2`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,front:`Why is EDTA added with benzalkonium chloride?`,back:`It chelates cell wall metal ions and so potentiates activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa.`},{id:`f-pt-ophthalmics-3`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,front:`When must an ophthalmic product be preservative free?`,back:`Intraocular solutions, products used during eye surgery, and anything applied to an injured or traumatised eye. These are single use.`},{id:`f-pt-ophthalmics-4`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,front:`Tonicity and pH the eye tolerates?`,back:`Equivalent to 0.5 to 1.8 percent w/v sodium chloride, ideally 0.9 percent; tears are pH 7.4 and freeze at minus 0.52 degrees C.`},{id:`f-pt-ophthalmics-5`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,front:`What is Ocusert and what does it deliver?`,back:`An insoluble ocular insert with ethylene vinyl acetate membranes delivering pilocarpine at near zero order for 7 days.`},{id:`f-pt-ophthalmics-6`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,front:`Formula for sodium chloride to be added for isotonicity?`,back:`NaCl needed (g per 100 mL) = 0.9 minus (E x drug percent w/v).`},{id:`f-pt-semisolids-1`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,front:`The four ointment base classes with one example each?`,back:`Oleaginous (white soft paraffin), absorption (wool fat), water-removable (hydrophilic ointment), water-soluble (PEG ointment).`},{id:`f-pt-semisolids-2`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,front:`Fusion versus trituration for ointment preparation?`,back:`Fusion melts the base components together for waxes and paraffins; trituration levigates the drug into the base on a slab, for small scale and heat labile drugs.`},{id:`f-pt-semisolids-3`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,front:`Define displacement value.`,back:`The number of parts by weight of drug that displaces one part by weight of the suppository base.`},{id:`f-pt-semisolids-4`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,front:`Formula for base required in suppositories?`,back:`Base = (N x M) minus (N x D / DV), where N is the number, M the mould calibration and D the drug weight per unit.`},{id:`f-pt-semisolids-5`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,front:`Why must theobroma oil not be overheated?`,back:`Above about 36 degrees C it recrystallises as the unstable alpha form melting near 24 degrees C, so the suppositories will not set.`},{id:`f-pt-semisolids-6`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,front:`Higuchi release from a suspension-type ointment is proportional to what?`,back:`The square root of time.`},{id:`f-pt-liquid-orals-1`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,front:`Sucrose concentration of simple syrup IP?`,back:`66.7 percent w/w, a near saturated and self-preserving solution.`},{id:`f-pt-liquid-orals-2`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,front:`Syrup versus elixir versus linctus?`,back:`Syrup is aqueous and sugar based; elixir is a clear hydroalcoholic solution; linctus is a viscous sweet liquid sipped for cough.`},{id:`f-pt-liquid-orals-3`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,front:`Flocculated suspension: sediment, supernatant, cake?`,back:`High loose sediment, clear supernatant, no cake, easy redispersion.`},{id:`f-pt-liquid-orals-4`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,front:`Stokes law and the effect of halving the particle size?`,back:`v = d2 (rho s minus rho l) g / 18 eta. Halving d cuts the settling velocity to one quarter.`},{id:`f-pt-liquid-orals-5`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,front:`Dry gum method ratio for a fixed oil primary emulsion?`,back:`Oil 4, water 2, gum 1.`},{id:`f-pt-liquid-orals-6`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,front:`Creaming versus cracking of an emulsion?`,back:`Creaming is reversible droplet migration under gravity; cracking is irreversible coalescence into a separate layer.`},{id:`f-pt-aerosols-1`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,front:`Propellants 11, 12 and 114 by chemical name?`,back:`Trichloromonofluoromethane, dichlorodifluoromethane and dichlorotetrafluoroethane.`},{id:`f-pt-aerosols-2`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,front:`Which propellants replaced CFCs in metered dose inhalers, and why?`,back:`HFA-134a and HFA-227ea, because they contain no chlorine and so do not deplete stratospheric ozone (Montreal Protocol, 1987).`},{id:`f-pt-aerosols-3`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,front:`Optimum aerodynamic particle size for lung deposition?`,back:`1 to 5 micrometres. Above 10 micrometres impacts in the throat, below 0.5 micrometres is exhaled.`},{id:`f-pt-aerosols-4`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,front:`Metering valve delivery volume for an MDI?`,back:`About 50 to 150 microlitres per actuation.`},{id:`f-pt-aerosols-5`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,front:`Why does a compressed gas aerosol lose pressure while a liquefied one does not?`,back:`A liquefied propellant replenishes headspace vapour by evaporation; a compressed gas has no liquid reservoir.`},{id:`f-pt-aerosols-6`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,front:`Which instrument measures fine particle fraction of an inhaler?`,back:`A cascade impactor, for example the Andersen impactor; it gives MMAD and the fraction below 5 micrometres.`},{id:`f-pt-packaging-1`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,front:`Glass Types I to IV: composition in one line each?`,back:`I borosilicate; II soda-lime surface treated with sulphur dioxide; III plain soda-lime; NP general purpose soda-lime.`},{id:`f-pt-packaging-2`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,front:`Which glass type takes the water attack test and why?`,back:`Type II, because only its dealkalised inner surface resists attack, so intact containers and not powdered glass must be tested.`},{id:`f-pt-packaging-3`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,front:`Powdered glass test limits with 0.02 N acid?`,back:`Type I not more than 1.0 mL, Type III 8.5 mL, Type NP 15.0 mL.`},{id:`f-pt-packaging-4`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,front:`The five problems of plastic containers?`,back:`Permeation, leaching, sorption, chemical reaction and alteration of the container physical properties.`},{id:`f-pt-packaging-5`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,front:`Which rubber is used for lyophilised product stoppers and why?`,back:`Butyl or halobutyl rubber, for the lowest moisture and gas permeability.`},{id:`f-pt-packaging-6`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,front:`Deterministic versus probabilistic CCIT methods?`,back:`Deterministic (helium leak, vacuum decay, HVLD, laser headspace) give quantitative instrumental results; probabilistic (dye ingress, microbial ingress, bubble test) depend on chance and operator.`},{id:`f-pt-preformulation-7`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,front:`Henderson-Hasselbalch for a weak acid, and the meaning of pH = pKa?`,back:`pH = pKa + log (ionised / unionised). At pH equal to pKa the drug is 50 percent ionised; each pH unit below the pKa makes it ten times more unionised.`},{id:`f-pt-preformulation-8`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,front:`Dose number Do: formula and cut-off?`,back:`Do = dose / (250 mL x saturation solubility). Above 1 the dose will not dissolve in a glass of water, so the drug is not highly soluble.`},{id:`f-pt-preformulation-9`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,front:`Enantiotropy versus monotropy?`,back:`Enantiotropes swap stability at a reversible transition temperature below both melting points; monotropes have one stable form at every temperature and convert irreversibly.`},{id:`f-pt-preformulation-10`,topicId:`pt-preformulation`,front:`Which common diluent must be avoided with amine drugs, and why?`,back:`Lactose, a reducing sugar, through the Maillard browning reaction. Use mannitol, microcrystalline cellulose or dicalcium phosphate instead.`},{id:`f-pt-tablets-7`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,front:`Carr index and Hausner ratio values for good flow?`,back:`Carr index up to about 15 percent and Hausner ratio below 1.25. Carr uses tapped density as the denominator; Hausner is tapped over bulk.`},{id:`f-pt-tablets-8`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,front:`Tensile strength of a tablet from the crushing force?`,back:`Tensile strength = 2F / (pi x D x t). It is independent of the tester and of tablet size, unlike a hardness reading.`},{id:`f-pt-tablets-9`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,front:`Picking versus sticking versus mottling?`,back:`Picking is material pulled out into the punch engraving; sticking is adhesion over the punch face or die wall; mottling is uneven colour, usually from dye migration during drying.`},{id:`f-pt-tablets-10`,topicId:`pt-tablets`,front:`Two remedies for capping?`,back:`Add a pre-compression stage and use tapered dies to release entrapped air and allow gradual elastic recovery; also reduce fines and avoid over-drying the granulation.`},{id:`f-pt-tablet-coating-7`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,front:`Typical weight gain: film coating versus sugar coating?`,back:`Film coating about 2 to 3 percent, shape and embossing retained; sugar coating 30 to 50 percent or more, shape lost.`},{id:`f-pt-tablet-coating-8`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,front:`What causes orange peel (roughness) of a film coat, and how is it corrected?`,back:`Droplets drying before they spread. Thin the coating solution, lower the inlet temperature and reduce atomising pressure.`},{id:`f-pt-tablet-coating-9`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,front:`Why is talc or glyceryl monostearate added to an enteric coating dispersion?`,back:`As an antitacking agent, to stop tablets sticking to each other and to the pan while the film is wet.`},{id:`f-pt-tablet-coating-10`,topicId:`pt-tablet-coating`,front:`Why does an enteric coat fail the acid stage at the tablet edge?`,back:`The film is always thinnest at edges and points of contact, so too low a coat weight is breached there first; enteric coats need a much larger weight gain than cosmetic ones.`},{id:`f-pt-capsules-7`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,front:`What does bloom strength measure?`,back:`The rigidity of a standard gelatin gel, as the force needed to depress its surface. Higher bloom means a stiffer shell.`},{id:`f-pt-capsules-8`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,front:`Which fills are unsuitable for a hard gelatin shell?`,back:`Water and aqueous solutions, which dissolve the shell, and strongly hygroscopic powders, which dry it out and make it brittle.`},{id:`f-pt-capsules-9`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,front:`pH range a soft gelatin capsule fill must respect, and why?`,back:`About 2.5 to 7.5. Below 2.5 the gelatin hydrolyses and the capsule leaks; above 7.5 it is tanned and becomes insoluble.`},{id:`f-pt-capsules-10`,topicId:`pt-capsules`,front:`Storage conditions for hard gelatin capsules?`,back:`About 15 to 25 degrees C and 35 to 55 percent RH. Too dry makes them brittle, too humid makes them soft and distorted; normal shell moisture is 13 to 16 percent.`},{id:`f-pt-parenterals-7`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,front:`F0 formula and the usual acceptance value?`,back:`F0 = sum of delta t x 10 raised to (T minus 121)/10, with z of 10 degrees C. Terminal cycles normally need F0 of at least 8 to 12 minutes.`},{id:`f-pt-parenterals-8`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,front:`Route for a thermolabile injection that cannot be autoclaved?`,back:`Sterile filtration through 0.22 micrometre followed by aseptic filling. Terminal moist heat is always preferred where the product allows it.`},{id:`f-pt-parenterals-9`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,front:`Clean area grade needed at the point of aseptic fill?`,back:`Grade A unidirectional airflow (ISO 5) within a Grade B background. Grade C and D are preparation and support zones.`},{id:`f-pt-parenterals-10`,topicId:`pt-parenterals`,front:`Isotonicity by freezing point depression: the working sum?`,back:`Blood and tears freeze at minus 0.52 degrees C; 1 percent w/v NaCl depresses by 0.58. NaCl to add = (0.52 minus drug depression) / 0.58 g per 100 mL.`},{id:`f-pt-ophthalmics-7`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,front:`Which preservative for an anionic drug such as sodium fluorescein?`,back:`Chlorobutanol or a phenylmercuric salt. Benzalkonium chloride is cationic and is inactivated by anionic drugs.`},{id:`f-pt-ophthalmics-8`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,front:`Three in situ gelling triggers, with an example of each?`,back:`Temperature (poloxamer), tear fluid cations (gellan gum, Gelrite) and pH (cellulose acetate phthalate latex).`},{id:`f-pt-ophthalmics-9`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,front:`White-Vincent formula?`,back:`V = w x E x 111.1, the millilitres of water in which w grams of drug give an isotonic solution; 111.1 is the volume made isotonic by 1 g of NaCl.`},{id:`f-pt-ophthalmics-10`,topicId:`pt-ophthalmics`,front:`Which drug property gives the best corneal permeation, and why?`,back:`Intermediate lipophilicity with solubility in both water and lipid, because the cornea is a lipid epithelium, hydrated stroma and lipid endothelium in series.`},{id:`f-pt-semisolids-7`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,front:`How is a required HLB hit with two surfactants?`,back:`Blend a high and a low HLB surfactant so the weighted mean equals the required HLB: fraction of high HLB = (required minus low) / (high minus low).`},{id:`f-pt-semisolids-8`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,front:`Mould lubricant rule for suppositories?`,back:`The lubricant must be immiscible with the base: soap spirit or glycerin for fatty bases, liquid paraffin or arachis oil for glycerinated gelatin.`},{id:`f-pt-semisolids-9`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,front:`Which suppository base for an oil soluble drug, and why?`,back:`A water soluble PEG base, because the drug partitions out of it readily. In a fatty base an oil soluble drug stays dissolved and is released slowly.`},{id:`f-pt-semisolids-10`,topicId:`pt-semisolids`,front:`Why can a nonionic cream separate in a hot climate?`,back:`Polyoxyethylene chains dehydrate as temperature rises; near the phase inversion temperature the emulsion inverts and coalesces. Keep the PIT well above storage temperature.`},{id:`f-pt-liquid-orals-7`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,front:`Why are methylparaben and propylparaben used together?`,back:`Methyl is the more water soluble and propyl the more oil soluble, so the pair covers both phases synergistically. Both hydrolyse above about pH 7 and are inactivated by polysorbates.`},{id:`f-pt-liquid-orals-8`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,front:`What happens when calcium chloride is added to a soap-stabilised o/w emulsion?`,back:`The monovalent soap becomes an insoluble calcium soap that favours w/o, so the emulsion inverts and then cracks.`},{id:`f-pt-liquid-orals-9`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,front:`Wetting agent versus suspending agent?`,back:`A wetting agent (polysorbate 80, glycerin as levigating agent) lowers the contact angle so the powder disperses; a suspending agent raises viscosity to slow settling.`},{id:`f-pt-liquid-orals-10`,topicId:`pt-liquid-orals`,front:`When is a dry powder for reconstitution used?`,back:`When the drug hydrolyses in water, as with amoxicillin and ampicillin syrups. Water is added at dispensing and the in-use life is short, typically 7 to 14 days refrigerated.`},{id:`f-pt-aerosols-7`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,front:`Vapour pressure of a propellant blend?`,back:`P total = sum of (mole fraction x vapour pressure of each propellant), by Raoult law. Blending 11 with 12 is how the pressure is tuned.`},{id:`f-pt-aerosols-8`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,front:`Cold filling versus pressure filling?`,back:`Cold filling chills concentrate and propellant to about minus 30 to minus 40 degrees C, so it cannot be used for aqueous products; pressure filling meters propellant through the crimped valve at room temperature.`},{id:`f-pt-aerosols-9`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,front:`Define MMAD.`,back:`Mass median aerodynamic diameter: half the aerosol mass lies in larger particles. Aerodynamic diameter is that of a unit density sphere with the same settling velocity.`},{id:`f-pt-aerosols-10`,topicId:`pt-aerosols`,front:`Why is a surfactant included in a suspension MDI?`,back:`Sorbitan trioleate, oleic acid or lecithin disperse the micronised drug, prevent caking and irreversible aggregation, and lubricate the metering valve.`},{id:`f-pt-packaging-7`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,front:`Which blister construction for a highly hygroscopic product?`,back:`Cold formed aluminium-aluminium, an effectively absolute moisture and light barrier. PVC/PVDC only improves on plain PVC.`},{id:`f-pt-packaging-8`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,front:`Permeation, leaching and sorption in one line each?`,back:`Permeation is vapour or gas passing through the wall; leaching is a container component migrating into the product; sorption is drug or preservative taken up by the container.`},{id:`f-pt-packaging-9`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,front:`Classical leachable from a flexible PVC infusion bag?`,back:`The plasticiser DEHP, which migrates into lipid containing fluids. Avoided by non-PVC polyolefin bags or alternative plasticisers.`},{id:`f-pt-packaging-10`,topicId:`pt-packaging`,front:`What is blow-fill-seal and why is it used?`,back:`The container is extruded, blown, filled and sealed in one continuous automated sequence, so human intervention is almost eliminated in an aseptic fill.`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};